hadiths[597]{hadithnumber,text}:
"3727","Buraydah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ saw a man afflicted with balā' and asked him whether he had supplicated his Lord to hasten the balā' for him in this world, and the man acknowledged that he had done so, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ directed him to ask instead for 'āfiyah through the Qur'anic supplication. > **""Perhaps you asked your Lord to hasten the balā' for you? Why did you not ask your Lord for 'āfiyah and say: Our Lord, give us good in this world and good in the Hereafter and shield us from the punishment of the Fire?""**"
"3728","Abū Mas'ūd al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of certain servants of Allah Almighty whom He keeps in 'āfiyah throughout their states, causing them to live and die in 'āfiyah and to enter Paradise in 'āfiyah. > **""Indeed Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has servants whom He keeps alive in 'āfiyah, and causes to die in 'āfiyah, and admits into Paradise in 'āfiyah.""**"
"3729","Abū al-Dardā' رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned 'āfiyah and the abundant reward Allah has prepared for its possessor when he gives thanks, and mentioned balā' and the abundant reward Allah has prepared for its possessor when he shows ṣabr, whereupon Abū al-Dardā' declared his preference for being spared and grateful over being tried and patient, and the Prophet ﷺ affirmed his love for 'āfiyah alongside him. > **""And the Messenger of Allah loves 'āfiyah along with you.""**"
"3730","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described how Allah Almighty commands the angels to pour balā' upon His servant, and when the servant responds with praise of Allah and the angels return with their report, He commands them to return, out of love for hearing the servant's voice. > **""Allah, Mighty and Majestic, says to the angels: Go to My servant and pour balā' upon him in abundance. So they pour balā' upon him and he praises Allah. They return and say: Our Lord, we poured balā' upon him in abundance as You commanded us. So He says: Return, for indeed I love to hear his voice.""**"
"3731","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reports with the same chain that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened Allah's testing of a person through balā' to a man's testing of his gold with fire, yielding pure gold, lesser gold, and blackened gold, corresponding to those protected from doubts, those touched by some doubt, and those overcome by fitnah. > **""Indeed Allah tries one of you with balā' just as one of you tries his gold with fire. From it comes forth what is like pure refined gold, and that is the one whom Allah has protected from doubts; and from it comes forth what is below that, and that is the one who doubts with some doubt; and from it comes forth what is like black gold, and that is the one who has been subjected to fitnah.""**"
"3732","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reports with the same chain that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described Allah's address to His angels when a Muslim falls ill, declaring that He has restrained His servant with a restraint of His, such that seizure by death brings forgiveness and restoration to health brings a forgiven body without sin. > **""When the Muslim falls ill, Allah inspires His angels, saying: O My angels, I have restrained My servant with a restraint from My restraints. If I take him then I forgive him, and if I grant him 'āfiyah then his body is forgiven, having no sin upon him.""**"
"3733","Abū 'Inabah al-Khawlānī رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that when Allah intends good for a servant He subjects him to trial, and when He tries him He wears him down, and when asked about the meaning of wearing down, he ﷺ explained it as leaving him neither family nor wealth. > **""When Allah intends good for a servant He tries him, and when He tries him He wears him down. He leaves for him neither family nor wealth.""**"
"3734","Ibn 'Abbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that muṣībah will whiten the face of its possessor on the Day when faces are blackened. > **""Calamity whitens the face of its possessor on the Day when faces are blackened.""**"
"3735","'Ā'ishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that when the sins of a servant multiply and he has nothing to expiate them, Allah tries him with grief so that it becomes an expiation for him. > **""When the sins of the servant multiply and he has nothing to expiate them, Allah tries him with grief in order to expiate them from him.""**"
"3736","Maḥmūd ibn Labīd رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that when Allah loves a people He tries them, and whoever shows ṣabr obtains the reward of ṣabr, while whoever shows panic and impatience reaps only panic. > **""When Allah loves a people He tries them. So whoever is patient, then ṣabr is for him, and whoever panics, then panic is for him.""**"
"3737","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that divine love for a people is accompanied by their trial. > **""When Allah loves a people He tries them.""**"
"3738","'Ā'ishah رضي الله عنها reports that the pain of the kidney vein, which is in the flank, seized the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for a month so that he could not come out to the people, and she watched him in anguish, taking his hand and blowing upon it with recitation of the Qur'an then passing it over his face seeking the blessing of the Qur'an and the blessing of his hand, and when she urged him, as one whose supplication is answered, to supplicate Allah for relief, he ﷺ answered with mention of the severity of his own balā'. > **""O 'Ā'ishah, I am the severest of people in balā'.""**"
"3739","'Ā'ishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was struck by pain and lay complaining and turning upon his bed, and when she remarked that she would have resented such conduct from one of them, the Prophet ﷺ explained the rigour imposed upon the righteous and the expiation and elevation attained through every mishap befalling the believer, even a thorn and beyond. > **""Indeed the righteous are dealt with severely, and no mishap befalls the believer, from a thorn prick and what is above that, except that a sin is thereby removed from him and a rank is thereby raised for him.""**"
"3740","Abū 'Ubaydah ibn Ḥudhayfah reports from his paternal aunt Fāṭimah رضي الله عنها that she with a group of women visited the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in illness while a suspended waterskin dripped water upon him from the heat of fever, and when they suggested he supplicate Allah for cure, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ answered by ranking the severity of balā' upon the prophets and then those successively closest to them. > **""Indeed among the severest of people in balā' are the prophets, then those who come after them, then those who come after them, then those who come after them, then those who come after them.""**"
"3741","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that a man may hold a station with Allah which his deeds do not attain, so Allah continues to try him with what he dislikes until he attains it, and in one narration the station is described as lofty. > **""Indeed a man may hold a station with Allah which he does not reach by his deed, so Allah continues to try him with what he dislikes until he reaches it.""**"
"3742","Muḥammad ibn Khālid reports from his father from his grandfather, who was a Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that when a station has been decreed for a servant by Allah which he cannot reach by deed, Allah tries him in his body or his wealth or his child, then grants him ṣabr over that until He brings him to the station decreed for him by Allah, Mighty and Majestic. > **""Indeed when a station has been decreed for the servant by Allah which he cannot reach by deed, Allah tries him in his body, or in his wealth, or in his child, then grants him ṣabr over that until He brings him to the station which was decreed for him by Allah, Mighty and Majestic.""**"
"3743","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that balāyā continue to befall the believing man and believing woman until they meet Allah with no sin upon them. > **""Balāyā do not cease to befall the believing man and the believing woman until they meet Allah with no sin upon them.""**"
"3744","Muslim, the freedman of al-Zubayr, reports that he entered upon 'Abdullāh ibn Iyās ibn Abī Fāṭimah al-Ḍamrī, who narrated from his father from his grandfather that the latter sat with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he turned to them and asked who loves to remain healthy and never fall ill, and when they hastened to affirm this he ﷺ showed displeasure on his face, then asked whether they love to be like stray donkeys or rather to be people of expiations, and swore by Him in Whose Hand is the soul of Abū al-Qāsim that Allah tries the believer only out of honour for him, having settled him in a station his deeds cannot reach and conveying him to it through balā'. > **""Who among you loves to remain healthy and never fall ill? Do you love to be like stray donkeys? Do you not love to be people of expiations? By Him in Whose Hand is the soul of Abū al-Qāsim, Allah tries the believer with balā', and He does not try him with it except out of honour for him. Allah has settled him in a station which he cannot reach by any of his deeds, so He tries him with balā' by which He conveys him to that rank.""**"
"3745","Jābir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened the believer to an ear of grain which stands straight at times and falls at times, and likened the disbeliever to a pine tree which remains ever upright until it falls without perceiving. > **""The parable of the believer is like the parable of the ear of grain: it stands straight once and falls once. And the parable of the disbeliever is like the parable of the pine: it remains ever upright until it falls without perceiving.""**"
"3746","Ubayy ibn Ka'b رضي الله عنه reports that a man entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who asked him when he last encountered Umm Mildam, being heat between skin and flesh, and when the man replied that such a pain had never afflicted him at all, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ drew the parable of the believer as a tender shoot which reddens at one time and yellows at another. > **""When was your last encounter with Umm Mildam, which is heat between the skin and the flesh? The parable of the believer is the parable of the tender shoot: it reddens once and yellows another time.""**"
"3747","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened the believer to an ear of grain which inclines at times and stands at times. > **""The parable of the believer is the parable of the ear of grain: it inclines at times and stands at times.""**"
"3748","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened the believer to a feather in a barren wilderness which the wind overturns and another wind lifts. > **""The parable of the believer is like the parable of a feather in a desert land: the wind overturns it and another lifts it.""**"
"3749","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened the believer to a tender shoot of cultivation which the winds bend, even to the point that a wind may at times throw it down. > **""The parable of the believer is like the tender shoot of cultivation: the winds bend it until at times a wind comes to it and lays it low.""**"
"3750","'Ammār ibn Yāsir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened the believer to an ear of grain which inclines at times and stands at times, and likened the disbeliever to a pine tree which falls without perceiving it. > **""The parable of the believer is the parable of the ear of grain: it inclines at times and stands at times. And the parable of the disbeliever is like the parable of a pine: it falls without perceiving it.""**"
"3751","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that a woman came to the Prophet ﷺ and offered him her daughter, describing at length her beauty and loveliness, and he ﷺ accepted her, but she continued to praise her until she mentioned that the girl had never suffered headache nor complained of anything at all, whereupon he ﷺ declared he had no need of her daughter. > **""I have accepted her. I have no need of your daughter.""**"
"3752","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that a Bedouin entered upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who asked him whether Umm Mildam had seized him, being heat between skin and flesh, and the man denied ever experiencing it, then asked him whether ṣudā' had seized him, being a vein striking a person in his head, and the man again denied ever experiencing it, and when the Bedouin turned away he ﷺ described him as a man of Hellfire for whoever wishes to behold one. > **""Has Umm Mildam seized you, which is heat between the skin and the flesh? Has this ṣudā' seized you, which is a vein striking a person in his head? Whoever loves to look at a man from the people of Hellfire, let him look at this man.""**"
"3753","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whether he should inform them of the people of Paradise and the people of Hellfire, and when they affirmed, he ﷺ described the people of Paradise as the weak and oppressed, and described the people of Hellfire as every harsh swaggering brute, being those whose heads never ache. > **""Shall I not inform you of the people of Paradise? The weak and oppressed. Shall I not inform you of the people of Hellfire? Every harsh swaggering brute. They are those whose heads never ache.""**"
"3754","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that a Bedouin came to the Prophet ﷺ, who asked him when he last encountered Umm Mildam, described as heat between skin and bone that saps the blood and consumes the flesh, and when the man replied that he had never complained of it at all, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared him a man of Hellfire and ordered him removed from his presence. > **""When was your last encounter with Umm Mildam? Whoever wishes to look at a man from the people of Hellfire, let him look at this man. Take him away from me.""**"
"3755","Al-Barā' ibn 'Āzib رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that no vein throbs nor eye suffers except on account of sin, and what Allah forgives is greater. > **""No vein throbs nor eye suffers except on account of sin, and what Allah forgives is greater.""**"
"3756","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed that Allah Almighty said that when His servant complains and displays sickness before three days have passed, he has complained of Him. > **""Allah, Mighty and Majestic, said: When My servant complains and displays sickness before three days, then he has complained of Me.""**"
"3757","'Amr ibn Murrah states among what Allah Almighty sent down that Allah tries the servant, while loving him, in order to hear his humble supplication."
"3758","Abū Wā'il reports the like from Ibn Mas'ūd رضي الله عنه concerning Allah trying His beloved servant to hear his supplication."
"3759","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded visiting the sick and instructing them to supplicate for the visitors, for the supplication of the sick is answered and his sin is forgiven. > **""Visit the sick and command them to supplicate for you, for indeed the supplication of the sick is answered and his sin is forgiven.""**"
"3760","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that the sick is not visited except after three days. > **""The sick is not visited except after three days.""**"
"3761","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, when he missed one of his brothers for three days, would ask about him, supplicating for him if absent, visiting him if present, and visiting his sick if ill, and that he missed an Anṣārī man on the third day and was told he had become like a bare gourd with nothing entering his mouth but exiting from his rear, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded that the brother be visited, and went out with Abū Bakr and 'Umar رضي الله عنهما among the party to visit him and found him as described. When asked how he found himself and from what this befell him, the man explained that he had passed by the Prophet ﷺ while he recited Sūrah al-Qāri'ah to its end in Maghrib prayer and had supplicated that whatever sin merited punishment in the Hereafter be hastened for him in this world, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reproached his words and taught him the supplication for good in this world and the Hereafter and protection from the Fire, then ordered him to supplicate with it and supplicated for him himself, until the man rose as though loosed from bonds. When 'Umar رضي الله عنه then asked about the reward for visiting the sick which he ﷺ had just urged, he ﷺ answered that the Muslim who leaves his house to visit his Muslim brother wades in mercy. > **""How do you find yourself? And from what is that? Evil is what you said. Why did you not ask Allah to give you good in this world and good in the Hereafter and shield you from the punishment of the Fire? Visit your brother. Indeed the Muslim servant, when he leaves his house to visit his Muslim brother, wades in mercy.""**"
"3762","'Alī ibn 'Umar ibn 'Alī reports from his father from his grandfather, in marfū' form, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that the greatest in reward among acts of visiting the sick is the lightest of them, and that ta'ziyah is offered once. > **""The greatest in reward among acts of visitation is the lightest of them, and ta'ziyah is once.""**"
"3763","Ibn 'Abbās رضي الله عنهما states that visiting the sick on the first day is Sunnah, and what is beyond that is voluntary and supererogatory."
"3764","Abū Dāwūd reports that he came to Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه and remarked upon the distance and their desire to visit him, whereupon Anas raised his head and narrated that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that whoever visits a sick person wades in mercy and, when he sits with the sick, mercy envelops him, and when asked what belongs to the sick himself, he ﷺ answered that sins are removed from him, and added that when the servant is ill for three days he emerges from his sins as on the day his mother bore him. > **""Whichever man visits a sick person, he wades in mercy, and when he sits with the sick, mercy envelops him. His sins are removed from him. When the servant is ill for three days, he emerges from his sins like the day his mother bore him.""**"
"3765","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that the visitor of the sick wades in mercy, placing his hand upon his hip to illustrate his coming and going, and that when he sits with him mercy envelops him. > **""The visitor of the sick wades in mercy, and when he sits with him, mercy envelops him.""**"
"3766","Ka'b ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that whoever visits a sick person wades in mercy, and when he sits with him he intercedes therein, and that they have, Allah willing, interceded in mercy. > **""Whoever visits a sick person wades in mercy, and when he sits with him he intercedes therein.""**"
"3767","Jābir ibn 'Abdillāh رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that whoever visits a sick person continues to wade through mercy until he sits, and when he sits he plunges into it. > **""Whoever visits a sick person continues to wade through mercy until he sits, and when he sits he plunges into it.""**"
"3768","Ibn 'Abbās رضي الله عنهما reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ visited the sick, he would sit at his head."
"3769","'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn 'Awf رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that the visitor of the sick dwells in the makhrafah of Paradise, and when he sits with him mercy envelops him. > **""The visitor of the sick is in the makhrafah of Paradise, and when he sits with him, mercy envelops him.""**"
"3770","'Amr ibn Ḥazm رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that whoever visits a sick person remains in mercy until he sits with him and intercedes therein, and when he rises from him he continues to wade in it until he returns to the place from which he set out. > **""Whoever visits a sick person remains in mercy until, when he sits with him, he intercedes therein, and when he rises from him he continues to wade in it until he returns to the place from which he set out.""**"
"3771","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that whoever visits the sick wades in mercy, and when he sits with him he plunges into it. > **""Whoever visits the sick wades in mercy, and when he sits with him he plunges into it.""**"
"3772","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that whoever visits the sick wades in mercy, and when he sits with him he plunges into it. > **""Whoever visits the sick wades in mercy, and when he sits with him he plunges into it.""**"
"3773","Ibn 'Abbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that whoever visits a sick person wades in mercy and mercy envelops him when he sits with him, and that seventy thousand angels seek forgiveness for him if he visits in the first part of the day until evening and if in the last part of the day until morning, and when asked what belongs to the sick, he ﷺ answered that manifold times this belongs to him. > **""Whoever visits a sick person wades in mercy, and when he sits with him mercy envelops him. If he visits him in the first part of the day, seventy thousand angels seek forgiveness for him until evening, and if he visits in the last part of the day, seventy thousand angels seek forgiveness for him until morning. Manifold times this is for the sick.""**"
"3774","Zirr ibn Ḥubaysh reports that they came to Ṣafwān ibn 'Assāl al-Murādī رضي الله عنه, who asked whether they came as visitors, and when they affirmed, he narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever visits his believing brother wades in mercy until he returns, and whoever visits his believing brother in sickness wades in the gardens of Paradise until he returns. > **""Whoever visits his believing brother wades in mercy until he returns, and whoever visits his believing brother when sick wades in the gardens of Paradise until he returns.""**"
"3775","Abū al-Dardā' رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that when a man sets out to visit a believing brother of his, he wades in mercy up to his waist, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ placing his hand upon his knee to illustrate it, and that when he sits with him mercy envelops him. > **""Indeed when a man sets out to visit a believing brother of his, he wades in mercy up to his waist, and when he sits with him, mercy envelops him.""**"
"3776","Jubayr ibn Muṭ'im رضي الله عنه reports that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ visit Sa'īd ibn al-'Āṣ and observed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fomenting him with a cloth."
"3777","Jubayr ibn Muṭ'im رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would say to his Companions to set out with him to Banū Wāqif to visit al-Baṣīr, who was veiled in sight and blind. > **""Go with us to Banū Wāqif, that we may visit al-Baṣīr.""**"
"3778","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ visited a man from among his Companions suffering pain, while he was with him, and he ﷺ took hold of his hand and placed his hand upon his forehead, considering that part of the completion of visiting the sick, and conveyed that Allah said His fire He empowers over His believing servant so that it becomes his portion of Hellfire in the Hereafter. > **""Allah said: My fire, I empower it over My believing servant, that it may be his portion of the Fire in the Hereafter.""**"
"3779","Salmān رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon him to visit him in illness, and when he wished to leave he supplicated for him for removal of harm, forgiveness of sin, and 'āfiyah in religion and body until his appointed term. > **""O Salmān, may Allah remove your harm, forgive your sin, and grant you 'āfiyah in your religion and your body until your term.""**"
"3780","'Ā'ishah رضي الله عنها reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ visited a sick person, he would place his hand upon the place that ached and then say words seeking blessing in the Name of Allah for no harm to remain. > **""Bismillah, lā ba's.""**"
"3781","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon a fevered Bedouin to visit him and declared his fever an expiation and purification, but the Bedouin retorted that it was rather a raging fever upon an aged elder that would dispatch him to the graves, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rose and left him. > **""Kaffārah wa ṭahūr.""**"
"3782","'Abdullāh ibn 'Umar and Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنهم report that whoever walks to fulfil the need of his Muslim brother is shaded by Allah with seventy-five thousand angels who supplicate for him, and he continues to wade in mercy until he finishes, whereupon Allah records for him a Ḥajj and an 'Umrah, and whoever visits a sick person is shaded by Allah with seventy-five thousand angels, with a good deed recorded for every foot he lifts and a sin removed and a rank raised for every foot he sets down, until he takes his seat, whereupon mercy envelops him, and he remains thus until he returns to his dwelling. > **""Whoever walks to fulfil the need of his Muslim brother is shaded by Allah with seventy-five thousand angels who supplicate for him, and he continues to wade in mercy until he finishes, whereupon Allah records for him a Ḥajj and an 'Umrah. And whoever visits a sick person is shaded by Allah with seventy-five thousand angels: he does not lift a foot except that a good deed is recorded for him, and he does not set down a foot except that a sin is removed from him and a rank is raised for him thereby, until he takes his seat, whereupon mercy envelops him, and he remains thus until he reaches his dwelling.""**"
"3783","'Awf ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded visiting the sick and following the janāzah. > **""Visit the sick and follow the janāzah.""**"
"3784","Mu'ādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that five deeds place whoever performs one of them under the guarantee of Allah Almighty: visiting the sick, accompanying a janāzah, setting out for battle, entering upon a ruler intending his support and honour, and remaining in his house so that people are safe from him and he is safe from people. > **""Five deeds: whoever performs one of them is guaranteed by Allah, Mighty and Majestic: whoever visits a sick person, or accompanies a janāzah, or sets out as a warrior, or enters upon a ruler intending his support and honour, or sits in his house so that people are safe from him and he is safe from people.""**"
"3785","Ibn 'Abbās رضي الله عنهما reports that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked how he had risen in the morning, whereupon he ﷺ answered that he was well, better than a people who visit no sick and witness no janāzah. > **""Well, better than a people who visit no sick and witness no janāzah.""**"
"3786","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that three afflictions entail no visitation of their possessors: ophthalmia, toothache, and the boil. > **""Three whose possessors are not visited: ophthalmia, the possessor of the molar tooth, and the possessor of the boil.""**"
"3787","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that Abū Ṭālib fell ill and the Prophet ﷺ visited him, and Abū Ṭālib asked him as his nephew to supplicate the God he worships to grant him 'āfiyah, so he ﷺ supplicated for his uncle's cure and Abū Ṭālib rose as though loosed from bonds, then marvelled that the God he worships obeys him, whereupon he ﷺ answered that his uncle too, if he obeyed Allah, would be obeyed by Him. > **""O Allah, cure my uncle. And you, O uncle, if you obey Allah, He will obey you.""**"
"3788","'Iyāḍ ibn Ghuṭayf reports that they entered upon Abū 'Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ رضي الله عنه to visit him for a complaint that had befallen him, while his wife, a frail woman, sat at his head, and when asked how Abū 'Ubaydah had passed the night, she swore he had passed it with reward, but Abū 'Ubaydah, first facing the wall, turned to the party and denied he had passed the night with reward, then invited their question and narrated that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that surplus spending in the path of Allah is multiplied seven hundredfold, while spending upon oneself and one's family, visiting the sick, or removing harm earns tenfold for each good deed, and that fasting is a shield so long as it is not rent, and that Allah's trial of a man in his body is a cleansing for him. > **""Whoever spends a surplus expenditure in the path of Allah, it is seven hundredfold. And whoever spends upon himself and his family, or visits a sick person, or removes harm, then each good deed is tenfold its like. And fasting is a shield so long as he does not rend it. And whomever Allah tries in his body, it is a cleansing for him.""**"
"3789","Abū Zur'ah al-Bustānī reports that he set out with his father and others to visit Abū al-Dardā' رضي الله عنه, whom they found in a dilapidated house with his face turned to the wall and his wife at his head, and when the party asked how Abū al-Dardā' had passed the night, his wife answered that he had passed it with reward, but he turned his face to them and denied her assertion, and when the party fell silent he invited their question and explained that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that the believer when ill is not rewarded for his illness but is expiated thereby. > **""When the believer falls ill, he is not rewarded for his illness, but expiation is made for him.""**"
"3790","Abū Ma'mar reports that when they heard from 'Abdullāh ibn Mas'ūd رضي الله عنه something they disliked, they would remain silent until he explained it to them, and that one day 'Abdullāh told them that no reward is recorded for the possessor of sickness, which grieved and weighed upon them, whereupon he explained that Allah Almighty thereby expiates sins."
"3791","Jābir ibn 'Abdillāh رضي الله عنهما reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that no believing man or woman and no Muslim man or woman falls ill except that Allah thereby removes a sin from him, and in one narration, removes some of his sins from him. > **""No believing man or believing woman, and no Muslim man or Muslim woman, falls ill except that Allah thereby removes a sin from him.""**"
"3792","Al-Sā'ib ibn Khallād رضي الله عنه reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that nothing befalls the believer, even a thorn that pricks him, except that Allah thereby records a good deed for him or removes a sin from him thereby. > **""There is nothing that befalls the believer, even a thorn that pricks him, except that Allah thereby records a good deed for him, or removes a sin from him thereby.""**"
"3793","Mu'āwiyah رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that nothing befalls the believer in his body causing him hurt except that his evil deeds are thereby expiated. > **""There is nothing that befalls the believer in his body causing him hurt except that his evil deeds are thereby expiated.""**"
"3794","Asad ibn Kurz رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Prophet ﷺ say that the sins of the sick person fall away as the leaves of a tree fall away. > **""The sick person: his sins fall away as the leaves of a tree fall away.""**"
"3795","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to a tree and shook it until its leaves fell as Allah willed them to fall, then declared that calamities and pains remove the sins of the children of Adam more swiftly than his stripping of that tree. > **""Calamities and pains are swifter in removing the sins of the children of Adam than I have been with this tree.""**"
"3796","Abū al-Dardā' رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah say that ṣudā' and malīlah continue with the believer, even though his sin be like Uḥud, and they do not leave him while there remains upon him thereof even the weight of a mustard seed. > **""Indeed ṣudā' and malīlah continue with the believer, even though his sin be like Uḥud, and they do not leave him while there remains upon him thereof the weight of a grain of mustard seed.""**"
"3797","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that malīlah and ṣudā' continue with the servant and the maidservant, even though their sins be like Uḥud, and they do not leave them while there remains upon them the weight of a mustard seed. > **""Malīlah and ṣudā' continue with the servant and the maidservant, even though their sins be like Uḥud, and they do not leave them while there remains upon them the weight of a mustard seed.""**"
"3798","Abū Sa'īd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reports that a man asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ what reward lies in the illnesses that befall them, and he ﷺ answered that they are expiations, and when Ubayy asked even if slight, he ﷺ answered even a thorn and what is above it, whereupon Ubayy supplicated for himself that fever never leave him until death, without hindering him from Ḥajj or 'Umrah or jihād in the path of Allah or obligatory congregational prayer, and it remained with him until death such that whoever touched him felt its heat. > **""Expiations. Even a thorn and what is above it.""**"
"3799","Jubayr ibn Muṭ'im رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that Allah Almighty tries His believing servant with sickness until He expiates from him every sin. > **""Indeed Allah, Mighty and Majestic, tries His believing servant with sickness until He expiates from him every sin.""**"
"3800","'Abdullāh ibn 'Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that whoever suffers headache in the path of Allah and bears it with hope of reward is forgiven what preceded that of sin. > **""Whoever suffers headache in the path of Allah and bears it with hope of reward is forgiven what preceded that of sin.""**"
"3801","'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn Azhar رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened the believing servant when fever or high fever befalls him to a piece of iron entered into fire, whose dross departs and whose purity remains. > **""The parable of the believing servant when illness or fever befalls him is like the parable of a piece of iron entered into fire: its dross departs and its purity remains.""**"
"3802","Al-Hasan al-Basri reported that they entered upon Imran ibn Husayn رضي الله عنه during the severe illness that had afflicted him, whereupon one of the visitors expressed grief and pity at the condition he witnessed. Imran forbade him, addressing him as son of his brother, and swore by Allah that whatever is most beloved to Allah Almighty is most beloved to him, then recited the verse of Surat al-Shura and explained his illness as the earning of his own hands, with his Lord's pardon to follow for what remained. > **""Whatever misfortune befalls you is because of what your hands have earned, and He pardons much.""**"
"3803","Abu Umamah al-Bahili رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ spoke concerning the servant who turns to his Lord with humble entreaty while sick, and the state of purity in which Allah raises him from that illness. > **""There is no servant who supplicates humbly on account of illness except that Allah raises him from it pure.""**"
"3804","Aisha رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ described what the complaint of illness accomplishes for the believer in removing sins, likening it to the work of the blacksmith's furnace upon the dross of iron. > **""When the believer falls ill, that thereby purifies him of sins just as the furnace purifies the dross of iron.""**"
"3805","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning one whose head aches in the path of Allah while he seeks reward with patience, and the forgiveness granted for whatever sins preceded that affliction. > **""Whoever suffers headache in the path of Allah and seeks reward thereby is forgiven whatever sin preceded it.""**"
"3806","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning every believing man and believing woman who falls ill, and what Allah makes of that sickness regarding sins already committed. > **""There is no believing man nor believing woman who falls ill except that Allah makes it an expiation for his past sins.""**"
"3807","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ drew a parable for the sick person when he recovers and regains health after his illness, comparing him to hail descending from the sky with reference to its cleanness and colour. > **""The likeness of the sick person when he recovers and is cured of his sickness is that of hail falling from the sky in its purity and its colour.""**"
"3808","Uqbah ibn Amir رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ explained that the work of every day is sealed, and described what the angels say when the believer falls ill and is confined, together with the reply of the Lord Almighty commanding the recording to continue as before until recovery or death. > **""Seal for him the like of his deeds until he recovers or dies.""**"
"3809","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ described the affliction of any person in his body, and the command Allah Almighty gives to the angels who guard him to record each day and night the good he used to perform for as long as he remains held in divine bonds. > **""Write for My servant in every day and night what he used to do of good for as long as he remains in My bonds.""**"
"3810","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning the servant who travels upon a fine path of worship and then falls ill, and the instruction given to the angel entrusted with him to keep recording as in his days of freedom until Allah releases him or gathers him to Himself. > **""Write for him the like of his deeds when he was free, until I release him or gather him to Me.""**"
"3811","Abu al-Ashath al-Sanani reported that he set out early for the mosque of Damascus and met Shaddad ibn Aws al-Ansari رضي الله عنه accompanied by al-Sunabihi, and upon asking where they intended to go out of mercy upon them, they replied that they meant to visit a sick brother from Egypt, so he accompanied them until they entered upon the man. They asked him how he had risen that morning, he answered that he had risen in blessing, and Shaddad gave him glad tidings of the effacement of evils and the removal of sins, citing what he had heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in the form of a divine address concerning the servant who praises Allah over his trial and the command to the angels to maintain his former record. > **""When I test a believing servant of Mine and he praises Me for what I have tested him with, then record for him as you used to record for him while he was healthy.""**"
"3812","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described what Allah Almighty says to the angel when He tests the Muslim servant with an affliction in his body regarding the continued recording of his former righteous work, and the two outcomes of healing with washing and purification or seizure with forgiveness and mercy. > **""Write for him the righteous deeds he used to do; if I heal him I wash him and purify him, and if I seize him I forgive him and have mercy upon him.""**"
"3813","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the command Allah gives to the guardian of the servant who falls ill: that no evil he commits be recorded, that every good he performs be recorded tenfold, and that his former righteous work in health continue to be recorded for him even when he no longer performs it. > **""Whenever a servant falls ill, Allah commands his guardian that whatever evil he does, it is not to be written, and whatever good he does is to be written as ten good deeds, and that his righteous work be written for him as he used to do while healthy even if he does not perform it.""**"
"3814","Utbah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ expressed wonder at the believer and his anguish over sickness, noting that had he known its worth he would have wished to remain ill for a lifetime. The narrator added that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ then raised his head toward the sky and laughed, and when asked why he had raised his gaze and laughed, he told of his wonder at two angels who sought a servant in the prayer place where he used to be but did not find him, returned reporting that Allah had confined him in His bonds, and received the divine command to record undiminished his former day-and-night deeds with his reward resting upon Allah for the duration of his confinement. > **""Write for My servant the deeds he used to do in his day and night, and diminish nothing thereof; upon Me is his reward for as long as I confine him, and for him is the reward of what he used to do.""**"
"3815","Aisha رضي الله عنها said that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak concerning every Muslim pricked even by a thorn, and what Allah records for him, effaces from him, and raises for him by way of degrees. > **""There is no Muslim pricked by a thorn except that Allah writes ten good deeds for him, effaces ten evil deeds from him, and raises him ten degrees thereby.""**"
"3816","Aisha رضي الله عنها said that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak concerning the believer whenever a vein throbs upon him, and what Allah removes from him, records for him, and elevates for him thereby. > **""No vein ever throbs upon a believer except that Allah removes a sin from him, writes a good deed for him, and raises a degree for him.""**"
"3817","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ described the Day of Resurrection when the martyr is brought and set up for reckoning, then the charitable giver is brought and set up for reckoning, then the people of affliction are brought for whom neither scale nor register is set up, but reward is poured upon them in abundance, until the people of well-being wish their bodies had been clipped with scissors out of admiration for the excellent reward granted to the afflicted. > **""Then the people of affliction are brought, and neither scale is set up for them nor register; reward is poured upon them in abundance, until the people of well-being wish that their bodies had been clipped with scissors, for the excellence of Allah's reward for them.""**"
"3818","Al-Asbagh ibn Nubatah reported that he entered with Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه to visit al-Hasan رضي الله عنه in his illness, where Ali asked him how he had risen that morning, addressing him as son of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Al-Hasan answered that he had risen in praise of Allah, recovered, Ali replied that it would remain so if Allah willed, and then al-Hasan asked to be propped up, so Ali supported him against his chest. Al-Hasan then cited what he had heard from his grandfather, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, concerning a tree in Paradise named for tribulation, the bringing of the afflicted on the Day of Resurrection with no register raised and no scale erected while reward is poured upon them, followed by the recitation of the verse on the patient. > **""In Paradise there is a tree called the Tree of Tribulation. The people of affliction are brought on the Day of Resurrection, and no register is raised for them nor scale erected; reward is poured upon them in abundance. Only the patient shall be paid their reward without reckoning.""**"
"3819","Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه stated, as a report of his own, that on the Day of Resurrection the people of affliction, when they witness the reward with their own eyes, will wish that their skins had been clipped with scissors in the world for the sake of what they then behold."
"3820","Ubayy ibn Kaab رضي الله عنه asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, upon him be peace, what the recompense of fever is, and received an answer linking the flow of good deeds to the stumbling of the foot and the throbbing of the vein. Ubayy then supplicated for a fever that would not hinder his going forth in Allah's path, nor his going forth to His House, nor to the mosque of His Prophet, and the narrator added that no evening came upon him thereafter except that fever was upon him. > **""Good deeds flow for its bearer for as long as a foot stumbles upon him or a vein throbs upon him.""**"
"3821","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ described fever as a furnace belonging to Hell, and explained that whatever portion of it befalls the believer constitutes his share thereof. > **""Fever is a furnace from Hell; whatever of it befalls the believer is his share of Hell.""**"
"3822","Jabir رضي الله عنه reported that fever sought permission to enter upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who asked who she was, and she answered that she was Umm Mildam. He then ordered her toward the people of Quba, who suffered from her what Allah knows, until they came to him complaining of it. He offered them a choice between his supplicating Allah to remove it from them or its remaining as a means of purification, and when they asked whether he would truly do so and he confirmed it, they chose that she be left. > **""If you wish, I supplicate Allah and He removes it from you; and if you wish, it remains for you as a purification.""**"
"3823","Umm Tariq, the freedwoman of Saad رضي الله عنها, reported that the Prophet ﷺ came to Saad and sought permission, but Saad remained silent, then sought permission again and Saad remained silent, then repeated it and Saad remained silent, so the Prophet ﷺ turned away. Saad then sent her to him to explain that nothing had prevented them from granting permission except their desire that he increase them in greeting. She then heard a voice at the door seeking permission though she saw nothing, and the Prophet ﷺ asked who she was. She answered that she was Umm Mildam, he gave her no welcome and asked whether she would go to the people of Quba, she agreed, and he commanded her to proceed to them. > **""No welcome and no greeting. Will you go to the people of Quba? Then go to them.""**"
"3824","Salman رضي الله عنه reported that fever sought permission to enter upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who asked who she was, and she answered that she was fever which wears away flesh and sucks blood. He commanded her to proceed to the people of Quba, she went to them, and they came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with their faces yellowed, complaining of the fever. He offered them the choice of his supplicating Allah to repel it or leaving it so that it would efface their remaining sins, and they chose that it be left, addressing him as Messenger of Allah. > **""If you wish, I supplicate Allah and He repels it from you; and if you wish, you leave it and it effaces your remaining sins.""**"
"3825","Aisha رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ described fever as the portion of every believer drawn from the Fire. > **""Fever is the share of every believer from the Fire.""**"
"3826","Aisha رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ missed a man who used to keep his company and asked why he no longer saw him, whereupon those present said he had been seized suddenly, using the term sudden seizure for feverish ailment. The Prophet ﷺ commanded them to rise and visit him, and when he entered upon him the youth wept, so the Prophet ﷺ forbade him to weep and conveyed what Jibril had told him concerning fever as the share of his nation drawn from Hell. > **""Do not weep, for Jibril informed me that fever is the share of my nation from Hell.""**"
"3827","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described fever as the portion of his nation drawn from Hell. > **""Fever is the share of my nation from Hell.""**"
"3828","Abu Rayhanah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described fever as arising from the fierce heat of Hell and as the portion of the believer drawn from the Fire. > **""Fever is from the blast of Hell, and it is the share of the believer from the Fire.""**"
"3829","Shith ibn Saad رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ described Umm Mildam as consuming flesh and drinking blood, and explained that its cold and its heat belong to Hell. > **""Umm Mildam eats flesh and drinks blood; its cold and its heat are from Hell.""**"
"3830","Abd Rabbihi ibn Saeed ibn Qays reported from his paternal aunt that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described what Umm Mildam removes from the son of Adam, likening it to what the furnace removes from iron. > **""Umm Mildam expels the filth of the son of Adam just as the furnace expels the dross of iron.""**"
"3831","Fatimah al-Khuzaiyyah رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ visited a sick woman of the Ansar and asked her how she found herself. She answered that she was well except that Umm Mildam, meaning fever, had settled heavily upon her, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ commanded her to be patient and explained what fever eliminates from man by the parable of the furnace and iron. > **""Be patient, for it removes the filth of the son of Adam just as the furnace removes the dross of iron.""**"
"3832","Rafi ibn Khadij رضي الله عنه reported that Nuayman said, addressing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that he suffered a severe feverish ailment, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ answered him with a question concerning his distance from Mahyaah, which the narrator identified as a pestilential land. > **""And where are you, O Nuayman, from Mahyaah?""**"
"3833","Shurahbil رضي الله عنه reported that they were with the Prophet ﷺ when a tall Bedouin came trembling and spoke of an aged elder seized by a boiling fever that would deliver him to the graves. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ answered him with words of expiation and purification, the man repeated his gloomy utterance and the Prophet ﷺ repeated his own answer three or four times, and then the Prophet ﷺ warned him that since he refused, the matter would be as he insisted and whatever Allah decreed would come to pass. The narrator added that the man did not survive past the evening of the following day but died. > **""An aged elder with a boiling fever; it is for him an expiation and a purification. But since you refuse, it shall be as you say, and whatever Allah decrees shall come to pass.""**"
"3834","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that a woman afflicted with lamam came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked him to supplicate for her, whereupon he offered her a choice between his supplicating Allah to heal her or her enduring with patience so that no reckoning would fall upon her, and she chose endurance without reckoning. > **""If you wish, I supplicate Allah and He heals you; and if you wish, you are patient and there is no reckoning upon you.""**"
"3835","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that while the Prophet ﷺ was in Makkah, a woman of the Ansar came to him complaining that the malignant affliction had overcome her. He promised her that patient endurance in her present state would bring her on the Day of Resurrection with neither sin nor reckoning, she swore by Him who sent him with the truth that she would endure until meeting Allah, then confessed her fear that the affliction would expose her by stripping her. He supplicated for her, and thereafter whenever she sensed its approach she would come to the curtains of the Kaaba, cling to them, and command it to be gone, whereupon it would depart from her. > **""If you are patient upon what afflicts you, you shall come on the Day of Resurrection with neither sin upon you nor reckoning.""**"
"3836","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported that he entered with the Prophet ﷺ to visit Zayd ibn Arqam رضي الله عنه while he complained of his eyes, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ asked him what he would do were his sight taken, he answered that he would then be patient and seek reward, and the Prophet ﷺ promised him the state in which he would meet Allah Almighty if his sight were taken and he endured with expectation of reward. > **""If your sight were taken and then you were patient and sought reward, you would surely meet Allah Almighty with no sin upon you.""**"
"3837","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed a divine address to the son of Adam concerning the seizure of his two precious faculties, patience and expectation of reward at the first shock, and the reward with which Allah is then pleased for him, falling short of nothing but Paradise. > **""O son of Adam, if I take your two precious ones and you are patient and seek reward at the first shock, I am not pleased with any reward for you less than Paradise.""**"
"3838","Aisha bint Qudamah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described it as unbefitting the majesty of Allah to take the two precious faculties of a believer and then admit him into the Fire. Yunus, one of the transmitters, explained that the two precious ones meant his two eyes. > **""It is grave before Allah to take the two precious ones of a believer and then admit him into the Fire.""**"
"3839","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed a divine address concerning the seizure of the two precious faculties of His servant, the servant's patience and expectation of reward, and the reward with which Allah is then pleased for him, falling short of nothing but Paradise. > **""When I take the two precious ones of My servant and he is patient and seeks reward, I am not pleased with any reward for him less than Paradise.""**"
"3840","Buraydah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the severest trials of the servant, placing association with Allah at their head, then the departure of sight as the severest of the remainder, and promised forgiveness for the one tested with the loss of sight who endures with patience. > **""No servant is ever tested with anything harder upon him than association with Allah, nor is a servant tested with anything harder than the departure of his sight; and no servant is tested with the departure of his sight and is patient except that he is forgiven.""**"
"3841","Zayd ibn Arqam رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the severest trial befalling the servant after the loss of his religion, and promised the one tested in his sight who remains patient until meeting Allah the state in which he shall meet Him, free of reckoning. > **""No servant is tested after the loss of his religion with anything harder than his sight; and whoever is tested with his sight and is patient until he meets Allah shall meet Allah the Blessed and Exalted with no reckoning upon him.""**"
"3842","Al-Irbad ibn Sariyah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed, in what he narrates from his Lord, the divine address concerning the seizure of the two precious faculties from His servant while he cherishes them, and the reward with which Allah is then pleased for him, falling short of nothing but Paradise. > **""When I take from My servant his two precious ones while he cherishes them, I am not pleased with any reward for him less than Paradise.""**"
"3843","Jarir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed the divine address concerning the one deprived of his two precious faculties and the compensation granted in their place. > **""Whose two precious ones I take away, I compensate him with Paradise in their place.""**"
"3844","Unaysah bint Zayd ibn Arqam reported from her father رضي الله عنه that the Prophet ﷺ entered upon Zayd ibn Arqam to visit him in an illness he suffered, assured him there was no harm upon him from that illness, then asked him of his condition when granted long life after him and struck with blindness. Zayd answered that he would then be patient and seek reward, the Prophet ﷺ promised him entry into Paradise without reckoning in that event, and the narrator added that he did go blind after the death of the Prophet ﷺ, then Allah Almighty restored his sight to him, and thereafter he died, may Allah have mercy upon him. > **""Then you shall enter Paradise without reckoning.""**"
"3845","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning the one whose sight Allah removes and who endures with patience and expectation of reward, and the binding right thereby established upon Allah regarding his eyes and the Fire. > **""Whose sight Allah removes and he is patient and seeks reward, it is a binding right upon Allah that his two eyes shall never see the Fire.""**"
"3846","Abu Zilal al-Qasamli reported that he entered upon Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه, who asked him when his sight had been afflicted, and he answered that he could not recall. Anas then offered to narrate a report the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had narrated to them from Jibril, upon him be peace, from his Lord the Blessed and Exalted, asking Jibril what the reward of His servant is when his two precious ones are taken, and answering it with the vision of His Face and neighbourhood in His abode. The narrator added that he saw the companions of the Prophet ﷺ weeping around him, wishing their own sight had departed. > **""O Jibril, what is the reward of My servant when I take his two precious ones except gazing upon My Face and dwelling in My neighbourhood in My abode?""**"
"3847","Abu Saeed al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed the divine address concerning the one whose two precious faculties are taken and who endures with patience and expectation of reward, and the reward with which Allah is then pleased for him, falling short of nothing but Paradise. > **""Whose two precious ones I take and he is patient and seeks reward, I am not pleased with any reward for him less than Paradise.""**"
"3848","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed the address of Allah Almighty concerning the removal of the two beloved faculties of His servant, the servant's patience and expectation of reward, and the recompense appointed for him in the form of Paradise. > **""When I remove the two beloved ones of My servant and he is patient and seeks reward, I reward him with Paradise for them.""**"
"3849","Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning the one whose sight departs in this world and what Allah Almighty appoints for him on the Day of Resurrection in the form of light, provided he is righteous. > **""Whose sight departs in this world, Allah Almighty appoints a light for him on the Day of Resurrection, if he is righteous.""**"
"3850","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed the divine address concerning the seizure of the two precious faculties of His servant and the reward with which Allah is pleased, whereupon the questioner asked whether the same applied to a single eye, and the Prophet ﷺ confirmed that it did. > **""When I take the two precious ones of My servant, I am not pleased with any reward for him less than Paradise; and even if it be one.""**"
"3851","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ conveyed the address of their Lord the Blessed and Exalted concerning the seizure of the precious faculty of His servant while he cherishes it, his praising Allah for that, and the reward with which Allah is then pleased for him, falling short of nothing but Paradise. > **""When I seize the precious one of My servant while he cherishes it, and he praises Me for that, I am not pleased with any reward for him less than Paradise.""**"
"3852","Jabir ibn Abdullah رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared the true anxiety to be that of debt and the true pain to be that of the eye. > **""There is no anxiety except the anxiety of debt, and no pain except the pain of the eye.""**"
"3853","Abu Usayb, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ told how Jibril, upon him be peace, came to him bearing fever and plague, how he retained fever for Madinah and dispatched plague toward Sham, and how plague stands as martyrdom and mercy for his nation but filth upon the disbeliever. > **""Jibril came to me with fever and plague; I retained fever in Madinah and sent plague to Sham. Plague is martyrdom for my nation and mercy for them, and filth upon the disbeliever.""**"
"3854","Abu Bakr al-Siddiq رضي الله عنه reported that he was with the Prophet ﷺ in the cave when the Prophet supplicated for stabbing and plague, whereupon he asked, addressing him as Messenger of Allah, about the meaning of plague, since he already knew of his supplication regarding the deaths of his nation and understood stabbing. The Prophet ﷺ explained it as a discharge resembling a boil and told him that a long life would show it to him. > **""It is a discharge like a boil; if your life is long, you shall see it.""**"
"3855","Abu Qilabah reported that when plague fell upon Sham, Amr ibn al-As رضي الله عنه declared it a scourge and commanded the people to disperse from it into ravines and valleys. News of this reached Muadh رضي الله عنه, who refused to affirm what Amr had said and declared it instead to be martyrdom, mercy, and the invocation of their Prophet ﷺ, supplicating Allah to grant Muadh and his household their share of His mercy. Abu Qilabah added that he understood the martyrdom and the mercy but not the invocation of their Prophet, until he was informed that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, while praying one night, had said in his supplication words concerning fever or plague three times. When a member of his household asked him in the morning about a supplication heard in the night, he confirmed having heard it and explained that he had asked his Lord Almighty not to destroy his nation by famine and was granted it, asked Him not to empower a foreign enemy over them, asked Him not to clothe them in factions tasting one another's might but was refused, and thereupon thrice uttered the words concerning fever or plague. > **""Then fever or plague; then fever or plague; then fever or plague.""**"
"3856","Abu Munib al-Ahdab reported that Muadh رضي الله عنه preached in Sham, mentioned the plague, and declared it to be the mercy of their Lord, the invocation of their Prophet, and the seizure of the righteous before them, supplicating Allah to apportion to the household of Muadh their share of this mercy. He then descended from his station and entered upon Abd al-Rahman ibn Muadh, who recited the verse that the truth is from his Lord and he must not be of the doubters, and Muadh answered with the verse that he would be found, if Allah willed, among the patient. > **""It is the mercy of your Lord, the invocation of your Prophet, and the seizure of the righteous before you. O Allah, apportion to the household of Muadh their share of this mercy.""**"
"3857","Muadh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه said that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretell the emigration to Sham, its conquest, and the emergence among them of a malady resembling a boil or a burning pain seizing the soft underbelly of a man, through which Allah would grant them martyrdom and purify their deeds. Muadh then supplicated that if Allah knew he had heard this from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, He grant him and his household the fullest share of it. The narrator added that plague then struck them so that none of them remained, that Muadh was pierced in his forefinger, and that he used to declare that nothing, not even red camels, would please him in its place. > **""You shall emigrate to Sham and it shall be opened for you, and among you shall be a disease like a boil or like a burning pain seizing the soft belly of a man; Allah shall thereby grant them martyrdom and purify their deeds thereby.""**"
"3858","Abu Musa al-Ashari رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared the perishing of his nation to be through stabbing and plague, and when asked about plague since stabbing was already understood, he explained it as the thrust of their enemies from among the jinn, with martyrdom in each. > **""The perishing of my nation is by stabbing and plague. Plague is the thrust of your enemies from among the jinn, and in each is martyrdom.""**"
"3859","Abu Burdah ibn Qays, the brother of Abu Musa, رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated Allah to make the perishing of his nation take the form of killing in His path through stabbing and plague. > **""O Allah, make the perishing of my nation as killing in Your path by stabbing and plague.""**"
"3860","Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm reported that when plague fell upon Sham, Amr ibn al-As رضي الله عنه addressed the people declaring the plague a filth and commanding them to disperse from it into ravines and valleys. News of this reached Shurahbil ibn Hasanah رضي الله عنه, who came in anger dragging his garment with his sandals hung in his hand, declared that he had accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while Amr was more astray than his household's donkey, and affirmed instead that it is a mercy from their Lord, the invocation of their Prophet, and the death of the righteous before them."
"3861","Abu Munib reported, in a narration concerning another outbreak of plague, that Amr ibn al-As رضي الله عنه addressed the people declaring this chastisement to be like a road, which misses whoever turns aside from it, and like fire, which misses whoever turns aside from it but burns and pains whoever remains upon it."
"3862","Yazid ibn Humayr reported from Shurahbil ibn Hasanah رضي الله عنه a narration similar to the preceding, except that when news of Shurahbil's words reached Amr رضي الله عنه, he declared that Shurahbil had spoken truth."
"3863","Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm reported from the account of al-Harith ibn Umayrah that he arrived with Muadh رضي الله عنه from Yemen and abode with him in his house and dwelling, whereupon plague struck them, afflicting Muadh, Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah, Shurahbil ibn Hasanah, and Abu Malik رضي الله عنهم on a single day. When Amr ibn al-As perceived the plague he fled in great fright and commanded the people to disperse into the ravines, deeming what had descended a punishment and plague from the affair of Allah. Shurahbil ibn Hasanah told him he had lied, reminding him of their companionship with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and judging him more astray than his household's donkey, and Amr conceded that he had spoken truth. Muadh ibn Jabal then told Amr ibn al-As that he had lied, for it was neither plague in his sense nor punishment, but the mercy of their Lord, the invocation of their Prophet ﷺ, and the seizure of the righteous, supplicating Allah to grant the household of Muadh the fullest share of this mercy. Before evening his son Abd al-Rahman, the most beloved of creation to him and the one from whom he took his kunya, was afflicted. Muadh returned from the mosque to find him distressed, asked him how he was, and the son, whose supplication in his favour had been answered, replied with the verse that the truth is from his Lord and he must not be of the doubters, whereupon Muadh answered with the words that they would be, if Allah willed, among the patient. The son died that night and was buried the next morning. Muadh ibn Jabal then kept sending al-Harith ibn Umayrah to Abu Ubaydah to ask after him, Abu Ubaydah showed him a piercing in his palm, al-Harith wept before Abu Ubaydah and withdrew in fear when he saw it, and Abu Ubaydah swore by Allah that he would not love red camels in its place. Al-Harith returned to Muadh to find him unconscious, wept and made others weep, then Muadh revived and asked him, O son of al-Himyariyyah, why he wept over him, seeking Allah's refuge from him. Al-Harith swore he did not weep over him but over missing his morning and evening company. Muadh bade him seat him, al-Harith seated him in his lap, and Muadh bade him listen to a bequest: that what he wept over of coming and going was as nothing, for knowledge lies between the two boards of the mushaf, and if its interpretation proves difficult he should seek it after him with three men, Uwaymir Abu al-Darda, Salman al-Farisi, or Ibn Umm Abd, warning him against the slip of the scholar and the disputation of the hypocrite. Then the throes of death intensified upon Muadh with a severity none had suffered, and whenever he revived from a stupor he opened his eyes and addressed his Lord to tighten His grip, swearing by His might that He knew he loved Him. When he fulfilled his term, al-Harith set out to Abu al-Darda in Hims, abode with him as long as Allah willed, then told him that his brother Muadh had enjoined him concerning him, Salman al-Farisi, and Ibn Umm Abd, and that he saw no course but to proceed to Iraq. He arrived at Kufa and attended the gathering of Ibn Umm Abd morning and evening, until one day Ibn Umm Abd asked who he was, he answered a man from Sham, Ibn Umm Abd praised the people of Sham but for one fault, and when asked what it was, answered that they testify of themselves that they are of Paradise. Al-Harith twice or thrice uttered the words of return to Allah and declared that Muadh had spoken truth in what he had told him. Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه asked what he had told him, nephew, he answered that Muadh had warned him against the slip of the scholar, then swore that Ibn Masud was one of two men, either a man risen upon certainty testifying that none is worthy of worship but Allah and thus of Paradise, or a doubting man unknowing of his abode. Ibn Masud conceded that his brother had spoken truth, that it had been a slip, and asked him not to hold him to it, then took al-Harith by the hand to his lodging, where al-Harith abode as long as Allah willed. Al-Harith then declared he must visit Abu Abdillah Salman al-Farisi in al-Madain, set out until he reached him, greeted him, and Salman bade him stay in his place until he came out to him. Al-Harith swore he had not thought Salman would know him, Salman answered that his spirit had known his spirit before ever knowing him, for spirits are marshaled troops, those that recognised one another unite and those that disavowed one another differ except in Allah. Al-Harith abode with him as long as Allah willed, then returned to Sham. Such, the narrator concluded, are those who know one another in Allah and visit one another in Allah."
"3864","Muadh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ foretold an encampment at a station called al-Jabiya, or al-Juwaybiyah according to the doubt of the transmitter, where a malady resembling the two glands of a camel would befall them, through which Allah would grant martyrdom to them and their offspring and purify their deeds. > **""You shall alight at a station called al-Jabiya, or al-Juwaybiyah, where a disease like the two glands of a camel shall befall you; Allah shall thereby grant martyrdom to you and your offspring, and purify your deeds thereby.""**"
"3865","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared the perishing of his nation to be in stabbing and plague, and when asked about plague since stabbing was already understood, he explained it as the thrust of their enemies from among the jinn, with martyrdom in each. > **""The perishing of my nation is in stabbing and plague. Plague is the thrust of your enemies from among the jinn, and in each is martyrdom.""**"
"3866","Utbah ibn Abd رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ described the coming of the martyrs and those dead of plague, the claim of the plague-stricken that they are martyrs, and the command to examine whether their wounds resemble the wounds of martyrs flowing with blood bearing the fragrance of musk, in which case they are martyrs, and the finding that they are indeed so. > **""Look; if their wounds are like the wounds of the martyrs, flowing with blood bearing the fragrance of musk, then they are martyrs.""**"
"3867","Aisha رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that his nation perishes only by stabbing and plague, and when asked about plague since stabbing was already understood, he described it as a gland resembling the gland of a camel, with the one who remains therein as a martyr and the one who flees from it as one fleeing from the advancing ranks. > **""It is a gland like the gland of a camel. Whoever remains therein is as a martyr, and whoever flees from it is as one fleeing from the battle ranks.""**"
"3868","Aisha رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ described plague as a piercing wound befalling his nation from their enemies the jinn, resembling the gland of camels, with the one who remains therein counted as a frontier guard, the one afflicted counted as a martyr, the one who flees counted as one fleeing from the battle ranks, and the one patient therein as a warrior in the path of Allah. > **""It is a piercing wound befalling my nation from their enemies the jinn, a gland like the gland of camels. Whoever remains therein is as a frontier guard, whoever is afflicted thereby is a martyr, whoever flees from it is as one fleeing from the battle ranks, and whoever is patient therein is as a warrior in the path of Allah.""**"
"3869","Aisha رضي الله عنها reported that when asked about plague since stabbing was already understood, the Prophet ﷺ described it as resembling a boil emerging in the armpits and the soft underbellies, bearing the purification of their deeds, and constituting martyrdom for every Muslim. > **""It resembles a boil emerging in the armpits and the soft underbellies; therein is the purification of their deeds, and it is martyrdom for every Muslim.""**"
"3870","Jabir ibn Abdullah رضي الله عنهما said that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak concerning plague, likening flight from it to flight from the battle ranks and promising the reward of a martyr for whoever endures therein with patience. > **""Whoever flees from it is as one fleeing from the battle ranks, and whoever is patient therein shall have the reward of a martyr.""**"
"3871","Ikrimah ibn Khalid al-Makhzumi reported from his father, or from his uncle, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded during the expedition of Tabuk that when plague falls in a land while they are in it they must not leave it, and when it falls somewhere while they are not in it they must not advance upon it. > **""When plague falls in a land and you are in it, do not leave it; and when it falls somewhere and you are not in it, do not advance upon it.""**"
"3872","In another narration from the same chain it is conveyed that the Prophet ﷺ commanded that when plague occupies a land where they are not present, they must not draw near it. > **""When it is in a land and you are not in it, do not draw near it.""**"
"3873","Zayd ibn Thabit رضي الله عنه reported that plague was mentioned before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who described it as a filth that afflicted those before them, and commanded that those who hear of it in a town must not enter upon it, and those in a town where it falls must not leave fleeing from it. > **""It is a filth that afflicted those before you. When you hear of it in a town, do not enter upon it; and when it falls in a town and you are in it, do not leave fleeing from it.""**"
"3874","Yala ibn Shaddad ibn Aws reported that Muawiya رضي الله عنه mentioned plague in his sermon, whereupon Ubada رضي الله عنه remarked that his mother Hind knew better than them. Muawiya completed his sermon, prayed, then sent for Ubada, and some men of the Ansar set out with him, whom Muawiya seated before Ubada entered. Muawiya asked him whether he did not fear Allah and feel shame before his leader, Ubada asked whether he did not already know that he had pledged to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ never to fear the blame of any blamer in the cause of Allah, and then departed. Muawiya returned at the afternoon prayer, prayed, seized the post of the couch, and declared to the people that he had conveyed a report upon the pulpit, had then entered his house to find the report exactly as Ubada had narrated it to him, and bade them draw knowledge from Ubada, for he knew better than him."
"3875","Shahr ibn Hawshab al-Ashari reported from Rabbihi, a man of his tribe who had undertaken his mother's care after his father and who had witnessed the plague of Amwas, that when the affliction spread, Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah رضي الله عنه stood preaching among the people declaring this pain to be the mercy of their Lord, the invocation of their Prophet, and the death of the righteous before them, and asking Allah Almighty to apportion to Abu Ubaydah his share of it, whereupon he was afflicted and died, may Allah have mercy upon him. Muadh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه succeeded him over the people, stood preaching after him with the same declaration, and asked Allah to apportion to the household of Muadh their share of it, whereupon his son Abd al-Rahman was afflicted and died, may Allah have mercy upon him. Muadh then rose and supplicated his Lord for himself, was afflicted in his palm, may Allah have mercy upon him, and was seen gazing at it and kissing the back of his palm declaring he would not love anything of this world in exchange for what it bore. When he died, Amr ibn al-As رضي الله عنه succeeded over the people, stood preaching among them declaring that when this pain falls it spreads like the burning of fire, so they should take to the mountains away from it. Abu Wailah al-Hudhali told him he had lied, swearing by Allah that he had accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while Amr was worse than his own donkey. Amr swore by Allah he would not return upon him what he said, declared by Allah they would not remain upon it, then departed with the people following him, they dispersed from it, and Allah repelled it from them. When this judgement of Amr reached Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه, he did not dislike it, by Allah."
"3876","Abis al-Ghifari رضي الله عنه was once with his companions above a rooftop of his when people burdened for travel passed by, he asked who they were, and was told they were people fleeing the plague, whereupon he thrice called upon the plague to seize him. A nephew of his, who had attained companionship, reproached him for wishing for death despite having heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbid any of them to wish for death, since death seals the work of the believer without return for amends. Abis answered him, nephew, that he hastened to depart before traits he had heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that would appear in the last age and were feared for his nation. > **""The rule of fools, the multitude of guards, the belittling of blood, the severing of kinship, and youths who take the Quran as flutes, advancing a man neither most understanding in religion nor most knowledgeable among them, though among them are those more understanding and more knowledgeable, advancing him to sing to them in melody.""**"
"3877","Abis al-Ghifari رضي الله عنه reported in another narration that he himself heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak concerning one who wishes for death and the severing of his appointed term that such a wish entails. > **""Let none of you wish for death, for it comes at the cutting off of his term.""**"
"3878","Salman al-Farisi رضي الله عنه reported that he came to the Prophet ﷺ with zakat on three occasions, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ asked those present whom they counted as a martyr among them. They answered that it was one killed in the path of Allah, so the Prophet ﷺ declared the martyrs of his nation few if confined to that, then enumerated killing in the path of Allah, plague, death in childbirth, burning, drowning, consumption, and abdominal disease as martyrdom. > **""Indeed the martyrs of my nation are then few: being killed in the path of Allah is martyrdom, plague is martyrdom, death in childbirth is martyrdom, burning is martyrdom, drowning is martyrdom, consumption is martyrdom, and abdominal disease is martyrdom.""**"
"3879","Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Himyari reported that a Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ named Humamah رضي الله عنه went out to campaign at Isfahan in the caliphate of Umar رضي الله عنه. After Isfahan was opened he supplicated to Allah that if his claim to love meeting Him were true, Allah should decree it upon him with truthfulness, and if false, Allah should still decree it upon him even if disliked. He was then seized by abdominal disease and died at Isfahan, whereupon Abu Musa al-Ashari رضي الله عنه addressed the people swearing by Allah that according to all they had heard from their Prophet ﷺ and the extent of their knowledge, Humamah was a martyr."
"3880","Uqbah ibn Amir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the one who dies of pleurisy as holding the rank of a martyr. > **""One who dies of pleurisy is a martyr.""**"
"3881","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the death of one far from home as martyrdom, explaining that when his end approaches and he looks right and left seeing only strangeness, remembers his family and children, and draws breaths, Allah effaces for every breath he breathes two thousand thousand evil deeds and writes for him two thousand thousand good deeds. > **""The death of the stranger is martyrdom: when he is approached and casts his sight to his right and his left and sees only strangeness, and remembers his family and his children and breathes, then for every breath he breathes Allah effaces from him two thousand thousand evil deeds and writes for him two thousand thousand good deeds.""**"
"3882","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to seek refuge with Allah from sudden death, and that it pleased him for a man to fall ill before he died."
"3883","Aishah رضي الله عنها reported that she asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about sudden death, whereupon he described it as rest for the believer and a seizure of sorrow upon the wicked. > **""It is rest for the believer and a seizure of sorrow upon the wicked.""**"
"3884","Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to seek refuge from seven deaths: sudden death, the bite of a snake, a beast of prey, drowning, burning, falling upon a thing or a thing falling upon one, and being killed while fleeing from the battle ranks. > **""Sudden death, the bite of a snake, a beast of prey, drowning, burning, falling upon a thing or a thing falling upon him, and being killed while fleeing from the battle ranks.""**"
"3885","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicated for refuge against dying of anxiety or grief, dying by drowning, Satan confounding him at death, and dying by a sting or bite. > **""O Allah, I seek refuge with You from dying of anxiety or grief, or dying by drowning, or Satan confounding me at death, or dying by a sting.""**"
"3886","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported with his chain that the Prophet ﷺ once passed by a leaning wall and quickened his pace. When asked about it, he explained that he disliked a death that seizes unawares. > **""I dislike sudden death.""**"
"3887","Hibban Abu al-Nadr reported that he entered with Wathilah ibn al-Asqa رضي الله عنه upon Abu al-Aswad al-Jurashi in the illness in which he died. Abu al-Aswad greeted them and sat, then took the right hand of Wathilah and wiped it over his eyes and face because of its pledge to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Wathilah said he wished to ask one thing, and upon being asked what it was, he asked how Abu al-Aswad thought of his Lord. Abu al-Aswad signalled with his head that it was good, so Wathilah gave him glad tidings of what he had heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as a divine address concerning Allah being as His servant thinks of Him and the servant thinking what he wills. > **""Allah Almighty says: I am as My servant thinks of Me, so let him think of Me what he wills.""**"
"3888","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ conveyed the address of Allah Almighty concerning being as His servant thinks of Him, so that good thought brings good and evil thought brings evil. > **""Allah Almighty says: I am as My servant thinks of Me; if he thinks good, it is his, and if he thinks evil, it is his.""**"
"3889","Salman رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning one who dies in either of the two sanctuaries, that intercession becomes due for him and he shall be among the secure on the Day of Resurrection. > **""Whoever dies in either of the two sanctuaries, my intercession becomes due for him, and he shall be among the secure on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"3890","Jabir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised that one who dies in either of the two sanctuaries shall be raised secure on the Day of Resurrection. > **""Whoever dies in either of the two sanctuaries shall be raised secure on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"3891","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised protection from the punishment of the grave for one who dies on Friday. > **""Whoever dies on Friday is shielded from the punishment of the grave.""**"
"3892","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened dying in Bayt al-Maqdis to dying in heaven. > **""Whoever dies in Bayt al-Maqdis, it is as if he died in heaven.""**"
"3893","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that since Allah created him, the son of Adam has never met anything harder upon him than death, then added that death itself is easier than what comes after it. > **""The son of Adam has never met anything since Allah created him harder upon him than death; then death is easier than what comes after it.""**"
"3894","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said that the last hardship a believer meets is death."
"3895","Sawdah, wife of the Prophet ﷺ, رضي الله عنها reported that she asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whether, when they died, Uthman ibn Mazun would pray for them until he joined them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ answered her, addressing her as daughter of Zamah, that were she to know what he knew of death she would know it harder than she reckoned. > **""If you knew what I know of death, O daughter of Zamah, you would know it harder than you reckon.""**"
"3896","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened one who flees from death to a fox pursued by the earth for a debt: it runs until exhausted and panting, enters its den, is told by the earth to pay its debt, rushes out panting, continues thus until its neck breaks and it dies. > **""The likeness of one who flees from death is that of a fox pursued by the earth for a debt: it runs until, weary and panting, it enters its den, whereupon the earth says to it: O fox, my debt. It rushes out panting and continues thus until its neck breaks and it dies.""**"
"3897","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reported from the Prophet ﷺ that death is the gift of the believer. > **""The gift of the believer is death.""**"
"3898","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that in Makkah there were two disabled parents with a young son who each morning carried them to the mosque, earned for them through the day, and each evening carried them back. The Prophet ﷺ missed him and asked about him, was told that their son had died, and declared that had anyone been left, the son of the two disabled ones would have been left. > **""Had anyone been left, the son of the two disabled ones would have been left.""**"
"3899","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that Allah loves the meeting of one who loves meeting Him and dislikes the meeting of one who dislikes meeting Him. When Anas remarked that they all disliked death, the Prophet ﷺ explained that this was not mere dislike of death, but that the believer at his end receives a bearer of glad tidings from Allah so that nothing is more beloved to him than meeting Allah, while the wicked disbeliever at his end receives what evil he shall pass into, so dislikes meeting Allah and Allah dislikes meeting him. > **""Whoever loves the meeting with Allah, Allah loves meeting him; and whoever dislikes the meeting with Allah, Allah dislikes meeting him. It is not mere dislike of death, but when the believer is approached, a bearer of glad tidings comes to him from Allah, and nothing is more beloved to him than meeting Allah, so Allah loves meeting him; and when the wicked disbeliever is approached, there comes to him what evil he shall pass into or what evil he shall meet, so he dislikes the meeting with Allah and Allah dislikes meeting him.""**"
"3900","Ata ibn al-Saib reported that the first day he came to know Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he saw a white-headed elder on a donkey following a funeral, heard him narrate from one who had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that Allah loves the meeting of one who loves meeting Him and dislikes the meeting of one who dislikes meeting Him, saw the people weep and ask why they wept since they disliked death, and heard him explain that it was not that, but the state at the end: the near one given spirit, sweet basil, and Paradise of bliss loves meeting Allah and Allah loves his meeting more, while the denying stray given boiling water and roasting in Hell dislikes meeting Allah and Allah dislikes his meeting more, citing the verses of Surat al-Waqiah. > **""Whoever loves the meeting with Allah, Allah loves meeting him; and whoever dislikes the meeting with Allah, Allah dislikes meeting him. It is not that, but when he is approached, if he is of those brought near, then rest and sweet basil and a Paradise of bliss, and when he is given those glad tidings he loves the meeting with Allah and Allah loves his meeting more; and if he is of the denying stray, then a lodging of boiling water and roasting in Hell, and when he is given those tidings he dislikes the meeting with Allah and Allah Almighty dislikes his meeting more.""**"
"3901","Muawiyah رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that Allah loves the meeting of one who loves meeting Him and dislikes the meeting of one who dislikes meeting Him. > **""Whoever loves the meeting with Allah, Allah loves meeting him; and whoever dislikes the meeting with Allah, Allah dislikes meeting him.""**"
"3902","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said that whoever loves the meeting with Allah, Allah loves meeting him, and whoever dislikes the meeting with Allah, Allah dislikes meeting him, and that death comes before the meeting with Allah."
"3903","Muadh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ offered to inform them of the first thing Allah Almighty shall say to the believers on the Day of Resurrection and the first thing they shall say to Him. When they assented, he told how Allah shall ask the believers whether they loved meeting Him, they shall answer affirmatively, He shall ask why, they shall answer that they hoped for His pardon and forgiveness, and He shall declare His forgiveness due for them. > **""If you wish, I shall inform you of the first thing Allah Almighty shall say to the believers on the Day of Resurrection and the first thing you shall say to Him. Allah Almighty shall say to the believers: Did you love meeting Me? They shall say: Yes, our Lord. He shall say: Why? They shall say: We hoped for Your pardon and Your forgiveness. He shall say: My forgiveness has become due for you.""**"
"3904","Mahmud ibn Labid رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ described two things the son of Adam dislikes: death, though death is better for the believers than tribulation, and littleness of wealth, though littleness of wealth means less reckoning. > **""Two things the son of Adam dislikes: death, and death is better for the believers than tribulation; and he dislikes littleness of wealth, and littleness of wealth means less reckoning.""**"
"3905","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed that the believer stands with his Lord in the station of all good, praising Him even while his soul is drawn from between his sides. > **""Indeed the believer is with Me in the station of all good: he praises Me while I draw his soul from between his sides.""**"
"3906","The elders reported that they attended Ghudayf ibn al-Harith when his death-struggle grew severe, whereupon he asked whether any of them could recite Ya Sin. Salih ibn Shurayh al-Sakuni recited it, and when he reached its fortieth verse Ghudayf was taken. The elders used to say that when it is recited at death, the dying one is lightened thereby. Safwan added that Isa ibn al-Mutamir recited it at Ibn Mabad."
"3907","Salman رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out to visit a man of the Ansar. Entering upon him, he placed his hand upon his forehead and asked how he found himself, but the man gave no answer, and it was said that he was preoccupied away from the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ asked that he be left alone with him, so the people left, and when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ lifted his hand the sick man gestured for him to return it where it had been. Upon being called by name and asked what he found, the man answered that he found good and that two figures had attended him, one black and one white. Upon being asked which was nearer to him, he answered the black one, and the Prophet ﷺ explained the meaning concerning the littleness of good and the abundance of evil. The man then requested the benefit of a supplication, so the Prophet ﷺ supplicated for forgiveness of the much and increase of the little, then asked what he now saw. The man answered, swearing by his father and mother, that he saw good growing and evil dwindling with the black receding from him. Upon being asked which of his deeds held him most, he answered that he used to give water to drink. The Prophet ﷺ then asked Salman whether he found anything strange in him, and when Salman affirmed, describing the Prophet's state that day as unlike any station in which he had seen him, the Prophet ﷺ explained that he knew what the man suffered, with every vein feeling the pang of death distinctly. > **""How do you find yourself? Which of the two is nearer to you? Good is little and evil is much. O Allah, forgive the much and increase the little. What do you see? Which of your deeds holds you most? Listen, O Salman, do you find anything strange in me? I know what he suffers; there is no vein in him but that it feels the pang of death distinctly.""**"
"3908","Zadhan Abu Umar reported from one who heard the Prophet ﷺ declare that whoever is prompted at death with La ilaha illa Allah shall enter Paradise. > **""Whoever is prompted at death with La ilaha illa Allah shall enter Paradise.""**"
"3909","Zadhan Abu Umar reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that whoever is prompted with La ilaha illa Allah at death shall enter Paradise. > **""Whoever is prompted with La ilaha illa Allah at death shall enter Paradise.""**"
"3910","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه entered upon the Prophet ﷺ in grief, and when the Prophet ﷺ asked why he appeared grieved, he told of spending the previous night with a cousin in his death-throes. Upon being asked whether he had prompted him with La ilaha illa Allah, he answered that he had done so, and upon being asked whether the man had uttered it, he answered affirmatively, so the Prophet ﷺ declared Paradise due for him. When Abu Bakr asked how it stood for the living, the Prophet ﷺ twice declared that it demolishes their sins all the more. > **""Had he uttered it, Paradise has become due for him. It demolishes their sins; it demolishes their sins.""**"
"3911","Jabir رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ commanded prompting the dying among them with La ilaha illa Allah. > **""Prompt your dying ones with La ilaha illa Allah.""**"
"3912","Ali رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised that one whose last speech is La ilaha illa Allah shall never enter the Fire. > **""Whoever has La ilaha illa Allah as his last speech shall never enter the Fire.""**"
"3913","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded prompting the dying with La ilaha illa Allah and enjoining steadfastness with the declaration that there is no power except through Allah. > **""Prompt your dying ones with La ilaha illa Allah, and say: Steadfastness, steadfastness; and there is no power except through Allah.""**"
"3914","Ata ibn al-Saib reported from his father from his grandfather that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that whoever is prompted at death with the testimony that La ilaha illa Allah shall enter Paradise. > **""Whoever is prompted at death with the testimony that La ilaha illa Allah shall enter Paradise.""**"
"3915","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reported in marfu form that the Prophet ﷺ commanded prompting the dying with La ilaha illa Allah, explaining that the soul of the believer comes out in sweat while the soul of the disbeliever comes out from his jaws as the soul of the donkey comes out. > **""Prompt your dying ones with La ilaha illa Allah, for the soul of the believer comes out in sweat, and the soul of the disbeliever comes out from his jaws as the soul of the donkey comes out.""**"
"3916","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded prompting the dying with the testimony that La ilaha illa Allah, promising Paradise for one who utters it at death. When asked about one who utters it in health, he twice declared that even more binding, then swore by Him in whose Hand is his soul that were the heavens and the earth and all within them, between them, and beneath them placed in one scale of the balance and the testimony placed in the other, it would outweigh them. > **""Prompt your dying ones with the testimony that La ilaha illa Allah, for whoever utters it at his death, Paradise becomes due for him. That is even more binding, even more binding. By Him in whose Hand is my soul, were the heavens and the earth and all within them, between them, and beneath them brought and placed in one scale of the balance and the testimony that La ilaha illa Allah placed in the other scale, it would outweigh them.""**"
"3917","Safwan ibn Assal al-Muradi رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon a Jewish boy in sickness and asked him whether he testified that La ilaha illa Allah. When the boy assented, he asked whether he testified that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. When the boy assented, he was then taken, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the Muslims took charge of him, washed him, and buried him. > **""Do you testify that La ilaha illa Allah? Do you testify that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah?""**"
"3918","Saeed ibn Abdullah al-Awdi reported that he witnessed Abu Umamah al-Bahili رضي الله عنه in his death-struggle enjoining that when he died they should do with him as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had commanded: when one of their brethren dies and they have levelled the earth over him, one of them should stand at the head of his grave and call him by his name and his mother's name, for he hears but does not answer, then call again so that he sits upright, then call again so that he asks for guidance without them perceiving, then remind him of what he left the world upon — the testimony that La ilaha illa Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger, contentment with Allah as Lord, Islam as religion, Muhammad as Prophet, and the Quran as guide — for Munkar and Nakir shall each take the other's hand and depart, saying they shall not sit with one whose proof has been prompted, with Allah Himself as his advocate against them. When a man asked what if the mother's name were unknown, the Prophet ﷺ instructed ascription to Hawwa. > **""When one of your brethren dies and you have levelled the earth over him, let one of you stand at the head of his grave and say: O so-and-so son of so-and-so daughter of so-and-so, for he hears but does not answer; then say: O so-and-so son of so-and-so, for he sits upright; then say: O so-and-so son of so-and-so, for he says: Guide us, may Allah have mercy upon you, though you do not perceive. Then let him say: Remember what you left the world upon — the testimony that La ilaha illa Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger, and that you were content with Allah as Lord, Islam as religion, Muhammad as Prophet, and the Quran as guide. For Munkar and Nakir shall each take the hand of his companion and say: Let us go; why should we sit with one whose proof has been prompted, when Allah Himself is his advocate against us. If he does not know his mother, let him ascribe him to Hawwa: O so-and-so son of Hawwa.""**"
"3919","Hudhayfah رضي الله عنه reported that he supported the Prophet ﷺ against his chest, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ declared that whoever utters La ilaha illa Allah and is sealed therewith shall enter Paradise, whoever fasts a day seeking Allah's Face and is sealed therewith shall enter Paradise, and whoever gives charity seeking Allah's Face and is sealed therewith shall enter Paradise. > **""Whoever says La ilaha illa Allah and is sealed therewith shall enter Paradise; whoever fasts a day seeking the Face of Allah and is sealed therewith shall enter Paradise; and whoever gives charity seeking the Face of Allah and is sealed therewith shall enter Paradise.""**"
"3920","Jabir رضي الله عنه reported that he heard Umar رضي الله عنه ask Talhah ibn Ubaydillah رضي الله عنه why he appeared dishevelled and dusty since the death of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, wondering whether the leadership of his cousin troubled him. Talhah swore refuge with Allah and told of hearing the Prophet ﷺ declare knowledge of a word that, uttered at death, gives the soul rest until it leaves the body and becomes light on the Day of Resurrection, yet he had neither asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about it nor been told it, and that this troubled him. Umar declared that he knew it, and upon being praised and asked what it was, he identified it as the word the Prophet ﷺ offered his uncle, which Talhah confirmed as true. > **""I know a word that no man utters when death attends him except that his soul finds rest thereby until it leaves his body, and it shall be a light for him on the Day of Resurrection: La ilaha illa Allah.""**"
"3921","Yahya ibn Talhah reported that Umar رضي الله عنه saw Talhah ibn Ubaydillah رضي الله عنه grieved and asked what ailed him. Talhah told of hearing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare knowledge of words that, uttered at death, relieve distress, brighten the colour, and show what delights, yet inability had kept him from asking about them. Umar declared that he knew them, and upon being asked what they were, he asked whether any word could excel the word the Messenger of Allah ﷺ urged upon his uncle at death, which Talhah swore was indeed it, and Umar named it as La ilaha illa Allah. > **""I know words that no servant utters at death except that Allah relieves him and brightens his colour, and he sees what delights him: La ilaha illa Allah.""**"
"3922","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ visited a man of the Ansar and addressed him as maternal uncle, bidding him utter La ilaha illa Allah. The man asked whether he meant maternal or paternal uncle, the Prophet ﷺ clarified maternal uncle, and when the man asked whether good lay in uttering it, he confirmed it. > **""O maternal uncle, say: La ilaha illa Allah. Nay, maternal uncle. Yes.""**"
"3923","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade any of them to supplicate to be prompted with his proof in absolute terms, since even the disbeliever is prompted with his proof, and instructed instead to supplicate to be prompted with the proof of faith at death. > **""Let none of you say: O Allah, prompt me with my proof, for the disbeliever is prompted with his proof; but let him say: O Allah, prompt me with the proof of faith at death.""**"
"3924","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed that Allah Blessed and Exalted commands the soul to come out, it answers that it will come out only unwillingly, and He commands it to come out even if unwilling. > **""Allah Blessed and Exalted says to the soul: Come out. It says: I shall not come out except unwillingly. He says: Come out even if unwilling.""**"
"3925","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that the death of the believer comes with sweat of the forehead. > **""The death of the believer is with sweat of the forehead.""**"
"3926","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه, reported from the Prophet ﷺ that the believer dies with sweat of the forehead. > **""The believer dies with sweat of the forehead.""**"
"3927","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that the soul of the believer comes out in sweat, that he did not love a death like the death of the donkey, and upon being asked what the death of the donkey was, he identified it as sudden death, adding that the soul of the disbeliever comes out from his jaws. > **""The soul of the believer comes out in sweat, and I do not love a death like the death of the donkey. Sudden death. And the soul of the disbeliever comes out from his jaws.""**"
"3928","Al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj reported from his father that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ address the Angel of Death, upon him be peace, at the head of a man of the Ansar, bidding him be gentle with his companion for he was a believer. The Angel of Death answered bidding him be of good cheer and cool eye, for he is gentle with every believer, then addressed him as Muhammad ﷺ explaining his seizure of souls, his standing in the house with the soul when kin wail — protesting no wrong, no hastening of term or decree, no fault in the seizure, with reward for acceptance and sin for grief and wrath, no excuse remaining with them, and repeated returns — his daily survey of every household of hair or clay, righteous or wicked, plain or mountain, knowing young and old better than themselves, and his powerlessness to seize even a gnat's soul without Allah's leave. Jafar ibn Muhammad added that it reached him the survey occurs at prayer times, so that one guarding prayer is approached by the angel, shielded from Satan, and prompted with La ilaha illa Allah, Muhammad the Messenger of Allah, in that mighty state. > **""O Angel of Death, be gentle with my companion, for he is a believer. Be of good cheer and cool eye, for I am gentle with every believer. O Muhammad, when I seize the soul of the son of Adam and one of his household wails, I stand in the house with his soul and say: What is this wailing? By Allah, we wronged him not, nor hastened his term, nor hurried his decree, and we have no fault in seizing him. If you are content with what Allah has done you are rewarded, and if you grieve and rage you sin and bear burden. You have no reproach against us, and we shall return to you again and again, so beware, beware. O Muhammad, there is no household of hair or clay, righteous or wicked, plain or mountain, but I survey them every day and night until I know their young and old better than themselves. By Allah, O Muhammad, had I wished to seize the soul of a gnat I could not unless Allah had permitted its seizure.""**"
"3929","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that the soul of the believer comes out in sweat while the soul of the disbeliever slips out as the soul of the donkey comes out, that the believer's sin is made severe upon him at death as expiation, and that the disbeliever's good deed is made easy upon him at death as recompense. > **""The soul of the believer comes out in sweat, and the soul of the disbeliever slips out as the soul of the donkey comes out. The believer commits sin and it is made severe upon him at death as expiation for it, and the disbeliever performs good and it is made easy upon him at death as recompense for it.""**"
"3930","Salman رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out to visit a man of the Ansar. Entering upon him, he placed his hand upon his forehead and asked how he found himself, but the man gave no answer, and it was said that he was preoccupied away from the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ asked that he be left alone with him, so the people left. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ lifted his hand the sick man gestured to return it where it had been. Upon being called by name and asked what he found, the man answered that he found good and that two figures had attended him, one black and one white. Upon being asked which was nearer, he answered the black one, and the Prophet ﷺ explained the meaning concerning the littleness of good and the abundance of evil. The man then requested the benefit of a supplication, so the Prophet ﷺ supplicated for forgiveness of the much and increase of the little, then asked what he now saw. The man answered, swearing by his father and mother, that good was increasing and evil dwindling with the black receding. Upon being asked which of his deeds held him most, he answered that he used to give water to drink. The Prophet ﷺ then asked Salman whether he found anything strange in him, and when Salman affirmed, describing the Prophet's state that day as unlike any station in which he had seen him, the Prophet ﷺ explained that he knew what the man suffered, with every vein feeling the pang of death distinctly. > **""How do you find yourself? Which of the two is nearer to you? Good is little and evil is much. O Allah, forgive the much and increase the little. What do you see? Which of your deeds held you most? Listen, O Salman, do you find anything strange in me? I know what he suffers; there is no vein in him but that it feels the pang of death distinctly.""**"
"3931","Abu Ayyub al-Ansari رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described how the soul of the believer, when seized, is met by the merciful among His servants as a bearer of glad tidings is met in this world, told to let their companion rest for he was in severe distress, then questioned about such-and-such man and woman and marriages, and when asked about one who died before him, answers in surprise that he died before. They then declare the return to Allah and judge him taken to his mother Hawiyah, an evil mother and nurse. He further described deeds presented to kindred and kinsfolk among the people of the Hereafter: if good they rejoice, give glad tidings, and supplicate Allah to complete His favour upon the doer and cause him to die upon it; if evil they supplicate Allah to inspire him with righteous work pleasing to Him that draws him near. > **""When the soul of the believer is seized, it is met by the merciful among His servants as the bearer of glad tidings is met in this world, and they say: Let your companion rest, for he was in severe distress. Then they ask him: What did so-and-so do, and what did so-and-so woman do; did she marry? And when they ask him about a man who died before him, he says: Alas, he died before me. So they say: To Allah we belong and to Him we return; he has been taken to his mother Hawiyah, an evil mother and an evil nurse. Your deeds are presented to your kindred and kinsfolk among the people of the Hereafter: if good they rejoice and give glad tidings and say: O Allah, this is Your bounty and Your mercy, so complete Your favour upon him and cause him to die upon it; and if evil they say: O Allah, inspire him with righteous work with which You are pleased with him and draw him near to You.""**"
"3932","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reported that when the servant is killed in the path of Allah, Allah effaces all his sins with the first drop of his blood upon the earth, sends him a wrap from Paradise in which his soul is seized, fashions for him a body from Paradise into which his spirit is set, then he ascends with the angels as though with them since Allah created him until brought to the Most Merciful, prostrates before the angels, is forgiven and purified, then commanded to the martyrs whom he finds in green meadows and silken garments with a bull and a fish feeding them daily what was not fed the day before — the fish dwelling in Paradise rivers tasting every fragrance, slaughtered evenings by the bull's horn, the bull grazing Paradise fruits, slaughtered mornings by the fish's tail — each tasting in its flesh every fragrance and fruit of Paradise, gazing upon their dwellings and invoking the Hour. When Allah takes the believing servant, He sends two angels with cloth and sweet basil from Paradise bidding the tranquil soul come out to rest, basil, and a Lord not angry, with what it sent forth excellent, emerging as the sweetest musk scent, greeted by angels across heaven as a pure soul from earth, every gate opened and every angel praying upon it and interceding until brought to Allah Almighty, with angels prostrating before him and presenting him as a servant who kept His remembrance moist, until commanded to His mercy and Paradise. > **""O tranquil soul, come out to rest and sweet basil and a Lord not angry; come out, for excellent is what you sent forth.""**"
"3933","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that deeds are presented to dead kindred and kinsfolk: if good they rejoice, and if otherwise they supplicate Allah not to cause them to die until He guides them as He guided their dead. > **""Your deeds are presented to your kindred and kinsfolk among the dead: if good they rejoice, and if otherwise they say: O Allah, do not cause them to die until You guide them as You guided us.""**"
"3934","Umm Hani رضي الله عنها reported that she asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whether they would visit one another and see one another after death, whereupon he told that souls become birds clinging to trees until the Day of Resurrection, when every soul enters its body. > **""Souls become birds clinging to trees until, when the Day of Resurrection comes, every soul enters its body.""**"
"3935","Umm Hani al-Ansariyyah رضي الله عنها reported that she asked the Prophet ﷺ whether they would visit one another after death, and the narrator then mentioned the like of the preceding report concerning souls becoming birds clinging to trees until the Resurrection. > **""Souls become birds clinging to trees until, when the Day of Resurrection comes, every soul enters its body.""**"
"3936","Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik reported that when death attended Sad ibn Malik, Umm Mubashshir bint al-Bara ibn Marur entered upon him and asked him, addressing him as Abu Abd al-Rahman, to convey her greeting to her father if he met him. He invoked Allah's forgiveness upon her and protested their preoccupation, whereupon she asked whether he had not heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare the spirits of believers in the bellies of green birds clinging to Paradise trees. He assented, and she concluded that this meeting would indeed be. > **""The spirits of the believers are in the bellies of green birds clinging to the trees of Paradise.""**"
"3937","Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik reported from Kab رضي الله عنه that when death attended him, Umm Mubashshir came and asked him to convey greeting to her son. He asked whether she had not heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare the believer's spirit a bird clinging to Paradise trees until resurrected on the Day of Resurrection. She answered affirmatively but confessed forgetfulness. > **""The spirit of the believer is a bird clinging to the trees of Paradise until it is raised on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"3938","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما said that Paradise hangs suspended by the horns of the sun, spread forth once each year, and that the spirits of believers are in birds like starlings, recognising one another therein and provisioned from Paradise fruits. Khalid ibn Madan added that when the people of Paradise enter Paradise they shall ask their Lord whether He had not promised to bring them through the Fire, and He shall answer affirmatively but that they passed it while extinguished."
"3939","Abu Bakrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon Abu Salamah while in death, and when his sight fixed upwards the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stretched his hand and closed his eyes. When the household wailed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ silenced them, explaining that sight follows the soul when it departs and that angels attend the dead and affirm what the household utters. He then supplicated for Abu Salamah's elevation among the guided, succession among his descendants, and forgiveness on the Day of Recompense. > **""When the soul comes out, sight follows it, and the angels attend the dead and affirm what the household of the dead utters. O Allah, raise the rank of Abu Salamah among the guided, succeed him among his descendants that remain, and forgive us and him on the Day of Recompense.""**"
"3940","Khawlah bint al-Yaman, sister of Hudhayfah, رضي الله عنها reported that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare no good in gatherings of women nor in their presence at the dead, for when they gather they speak and speak. > **""There is no good in gatherings of women nor at the dead, for when they gather they speak and speak.""**"
"3941","Aishah رضي الله عنها reported that Bilal رضي الله عنه came to the Prophet ﷺ announcing that a certain woman had died and found rest, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ grew angry and declared that only one forgiven truly rests. > **""Only one forgiven truly rests.""**"
"3942","Aishah رضي الله عنها reported concerning a woman whom the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ used to laugh with and jest with: when she died and Aishah remarked that she had found rest, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that only one forgiven truly rests. > **""Only one forgiven truly rests.""**"
"3943","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared his nation granted at calamity what none of the nations was granted: Inna lillahi wa inna ilayhi rajiun. > **""My nation has been granted at calamity what none of the nations was granted: Inna lillahi wa inna ilayhi rajiun.""**"
"3944","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported concerning the verse Those who, when calamity befalls them, say Inna lillahi wa inna ilayhi rajiun, upon them are blessings from their Lord and mercy, and they are the guided, that Allah informed that when the believer submits to Allah's decree and returns with istirja at calamity, three goods are written for him: blessing from Allah, mercy, and realisation of guidance. He added that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that whoever says istirja at calamity shall have his calamity mended, his end made good, and a pleasing successor granted. > **""Whoever says istirja at calamity, Allah shall mend his calamity, make his end good, and grant him a successor with whom he is pleased.""**"
"3945","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared death a fright, and instructed that when one of them attends his brother's death he should utter the return to Allah and turning to their Lord, inscribe the dead among the good with his book in Illiyyin and succession among those after, and supplicate not to be deprived of his reward nor tried after him. > **""Death is a fright, so when one of you attends his brother's death, let him say: Inna lillahi wa inna ilayhi rajiun, and to our Lord we surely turn. O Allah, write him among the good, place his book in Illiyyin, succeed him among those after, O Allah, deprive us not of his reward nor try us after him.""**"
"3946","Al-Husayn ibn Ali رضي الله عنهما reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declare that no Muslim man or woman afflicted with calamity who remembers it even after long time and renews istirja thereupon shall fail to have Allah renew reward for him thereupon, granting the reward of the day he was afflicted. > **""No Muslim man or Muslim woman is afflicted with calamity and remembers it even after long time and renews istirja thereupon except that Allah renews for him thereupon and grants him its reward as on the day he was afflicted with it.""**"
"3947","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reported that they went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when the Prophet's sandal strap broke, whereupon he uttered Inna lillahi wa inna ilayhi rajiun. When a man remarked at a mere strap, he declared it a calamity. > **""Inna lillahi wa inna ilayhi rajiun. It is a calamity.""**"
"3948","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet's sandal thong broke and he uttered istirja. When asked in surprise whether this were a calamity, O Messenger of Allah, he explained that whatever befalls the believer of what he dislikes is a calamity. > **""Whatever befalls the believer of what he dislikes is a calamity.""**"
"3949","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded that when one's sandal strap breaks he should utter istirja, for it is among calamities. > **""When the strap of one of you breaks, let him utter istirja, for it is among calamities.""**"
"3950","Shaddad ibn Aws رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ the like of the preceding report concerning uttering istirja when the sandal strap breaks. > **""When the strap of one of you breaks, let him utter istirja, for it is among calamities.""**"
"3951","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promised that whoever is afflicted with calamity in wealth or body, conceals it, and does not complain of it to people, shall have Allah's forgiveness made due upon Allah. > **""Whoever is afflicted with calamity in his wealth or his body and conceals it and does not complain of it to people, it is due upon Allah to forgive him.""**"
"3952","Sabit رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ instructed that whenever any of them is struck by a calamity, he should recall his calamity through the Prophet himself, on account of its surpassing greatness over all other calamities. > **""When one of you is struck by a calamity, let him remember his calamity through me, for it is the greatest of calamities.""**"
"3953","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed through al-Baqi by a woman crouching over a grave and weeping, and thrice bade her fear Allah and be patient, addressing her as maidservant of Allah. She, not knowing him, answered each time that she was a bereaved mother burning with grief, that had he himself been afflicted he would have excused her, and finally that he had made himself heard and should leave her. The Prophet ﷺ went on his way, and when one of his Companions who had followed him informed the woman that the man who had gone was the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, she sprang up hastening after him and declared her patience, whereupon he told her twice where true patience lies. > **""Patience is at the first shock, patience is at the first shock.""**"
"3954","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ bade al-Fadl ibn Abbas رضي الله عنه prepare water for his purification, then went far out of sight for his need as was his custom. Upon his return he passed a woman wailing over the grave of one of her dead, stood over her without her recognizing him, and bade her fear Allah and be patient. She thrice told him to go about his own need, so he departed and took the vessel from al-Fadl. When al-Fadl then came to the woman and asked her what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said to her, she cried out at her ruin for not knowing him, hastened until she caught up with him at the door of the mosque, and swore by Allah that she had not recognized him, whereupon he told her thrice where patience lies. > **""Patience is at the first shock.""**"
"3955","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ defined the occasion of true patience as the very first onset of the shock. > **""Patience is at the first shock.""**"
"3956","Muadh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه lost a son, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote to him consoling him for his son, opening in the Name of Allah the Most Merciful the Most Compassionate from Muhammad the Messenger of Allah, praising before him Allah besides whom there is no deity, invoking magnified reward, inspired patience, and gratitude for them both, reminding him that selves, wealth, and families are among Allah's pleasant gifts and entrusted loans, that Allah had delighted him with the child in gladness and taken him back with abundant reward of prayer, mercy, and guidance, urging him to be patient if he seeks reward lest panic nullify his reward and leave him regretful, for panic returns no dead and repels no grief, and whatever is to descend is as though it already had, closing with peace. > **""In the Name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. From Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah, to Muadh ibn Jabal. Peace be upon you, and I praise before you Allah besides whom there is no deity. To proceed: may Allah magnify your reward, inspire you with patience, and grant us and you gratitude. For our selves, our wealth, and our families are among the pleasant gifts of Allah and His entrusted loans. Allah delighted you with him in gladness and joy, and took him from you with abundant reward of prayer, mercy, and guidance, if you seek reward for him. So be patient, and let not your panic nullify your reward lest you regret. And know that panic returns no dead nor repels grief, and whatever is to descend is as though it already had. Peace.""**"
"3957","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed away and his Companions sat grieving and weeping around him, there came a tall, fair-faced, eloquent man in a waist-wrapper and cloak, thick-haired upon shoulders and chest, who stepped past the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until he took hold of the two doorposts, wept over the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for an hour, then spoke words of consolation concerning every calamity, every perished one, and everything lost, urging return to Allah and desire toward Him, for only he is afflicted whom reward does not console. When the people asked whether any knew the man and looked right and left without seeing anyone, Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه declared his identity. > **""In Allah is consolation from every calamity, a successor for every perished one, and recompense for everything lost. So turn to Allah and desire Him, for only he is afflicted whom reward does not console.""**"
"3958","Ubayy ibn Kab رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded that when a man is seen consoling himself with the condolence of Jahiliyyah, he be rebuked bluntly and without euphemism. > **""When you see a man consoling himself with the condolence of Jahiliyyah, bite him and do not use euphemism.""**"
"3959","Abu Qatadah رضي الله عنه reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was called to a funeral he would ask about the deceased, and if he was praised with good he would rise and pray over him, whereas if he was praised with other than that he would leave the matter to the family and not pray over him. > **""Attend to her yourselves.""**"
"3960","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ spoke concerning any Muslim who dies while the people of four houses among his nearest neighbours testify for him, and what Allah declares in accepting their knowledge of him and forgiving him what they know not. In the narration of Abu Yala the wording adds that they know naught of him but good. > **""There is no Muslim who dies and the people of four houses among his nearest neighbours testify for him, testifying that they know naught but good, except that Allah says: I have accepted your knowledge of him, and I have forgiven him what you know not.""**"
"3961","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ, relating from his Lord Almighty, concerning any Muslim servant who dies while three houses among his nearest neighbours testify good for him, and what Allah Almighty declares in accepting the testimony of His servants for what they knew and forgiving him for what He knows. > **""There is no Muslim servant who dies and three houses among his nearest neighbours testify good for him except that Allah Almighty says: I have accepted the testimony of My servants for what they knew, and I have forgiven him for what I know.""**"
"3962","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that while they were with the Prophet ﷺ a funeral was brought and the people praised it with good, so the Prophet ﷺ pronounced the verdict, then another was brought and the people spoke ill of it as though assailing the man, so the Prophet ﷺ pronounced the same verdict. When the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked how both had received it, Umar رضي الله عنه put the question on their behalf, swearing by his father and mother, and the Prophet ﷺ explained the standing of their two testimonies and the station of the believers toward one another. > **""Your brother was brought and you testified as you testified, so your testimony became incumbent. Then your brother so-and-so was brought and you testified as you testified, so your testimony became incumbent. You are the witnesses of Allah on earth, some of you over others.""**"
"3963","Kab ibn Ujrah رضي الله عنه reported that he witnessed two sittings with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. In the first a funeral was brought and the man was named and declared an evil man with evil spoken of him, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whether they knew that to be so, they affirmed it, and he pronounced the verdict. In the other the funeral of a man was brought and he was praised with good, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whether they knew that to be so, they affirmed it, and he pronounced the verdict. > **""It has become incumbent.""**"
"3964","Salamah ibn al-Akwa رضي الله عنه reported that while they were with the Prophet ﷺ a funeral was brought and the people spoke over it in terms of wishing the man had been this or that, then another was brought and the people spoke likewise, praising one with good and the other with evil, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ defined the station of men and of angels. In another narration the consequence of their testimony is added. > **""You are the witnesses of Allah on earth, and the angels are the witnesses of Allah in heaven. So when you testify, it becomes incumbent.""**"
"3965","Amir ibn Rabiah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning the servant who dies while Allah knows his secret and the people speak good of him, and what Allah Almighty says to His angels in accepting the testimony of His servants over His servant and forgiving him what His own knowledge encompasses. > **""When the servant dies, while Allah knows his secret, and the people speak good, Allah Almighty says to His angels: I have accepted the testimony of My servants over My servant, and I have forgiven him what I know of him.""**"
"3966","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that while he sat with the Prophet ﷺ a funeral passed and the Prophet ﷺ asked whose it was. It was said to be the funeral of so-and-so son of so-and-so, who loved Allah and His Messenger, so he pronounced the verdict thrice. Then another passed and he asked whose it was. It was said to be the funeral of so-and-so son of so-and-so, who hated Allah and His Messenger, so he pronounced the verdict thrice. > **""It has become incumbent.""**"
"3967","Maryam bint Farwah reported that when death approached Imran ibn Husayn رضي الله عنه, he instructed that when he died a turban be bound tightly over his belly, and that when they returned from his burial they slaughter and feed people. Khalid reported that Hafs told him this is not as the people of his house, the family of al-Muhallab and Thaqif, do."
"3968","Jabir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning Adam عليه السلام informing his wife Hawwa that her son had died. When she asked what death is, he explained it as neither eating nor drinking nor grasping nor walking, at which she screamed, so he decreed wailing upon her and her daughters while absolving himself and his sons, and wailing gatherings thus fell upon women. > **""Wailing is upon you and your daughters, while I and my sons are quit of it.""**"
"3969","Amr ibn Abasah رضي الله عنه, as conveyed by Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه who had asked him for a narration heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ free of omission and error, reported that he heard him speak concerning whoever has three children born in Islam who die before puberty, and concerning whoever spends a pair in the path of Allah in relation to the eight gates of Paradise. > **""Whoever has three children born in Islam who die before reaching puberty, Allah admits him to Paradise by His mercy toward them. And whoever spends a pair in the path of Allah, then Paradise has eight gates through whichever of them he wills Allah admits him to Paradise.""**"
"3970","Uqbah ibn Amir رضي الله عنه reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning whoever loses three of his own loins and seeks reward for them with Allah in the path of Allah Almighty, and what becomes obligatory for him. > **""Whoever loses three of his loins and seeks reward for them with Allah in the path of Allah Almighty, Paradise becomes obligatory for him.""**"
"3971","Muhammad ibn Sirin reported that a woman called Mawiyah who used to visit them and was afflicted in her child came to Ubaydullah ibn Mamar al-Qurashi, with whom sat a Companion of the Prophet ﷺ. The Companion narrated that a woman once brought her son to the Prophet ﷺ asking him to supplicate Allah to preserve him for her, seeing that three had died before him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked her thrice whether this was since she embraced Islam, she affirmed it each time, and he gave her the glad tidings. Mawiyah added that Ubaydullah ibn Mamar bade her heed it, and Muhammad added that Mawiyah then left Ibn Mamar and narrated them this narration. > **""A firm shield.""**"
"3972","Umm Sulaym رضي الله عنها, the mother of Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه, reported that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak concerning any two Muslims who lose three children before puberty, and what Allah admits them to by the grace of His mercy toward those children. > **""There are no two Muslims who lose three children before puberty except that Allah admits them to Paradise by the grace of His mercy toward them.""**"
"3973","A woman called Raja reported that she was with the Prophet ﷺ when a woman brought her son and asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to supplicate Allah for blessing in him for her, seeing that three had died. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked her whether this was since she embraced Islam, she affirmed it, and he gave her the glad tidings, at which a man bade Raja heed what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was saying. > **""A firm shield.""**"
"3974","Uthman ibn Abi al-As رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning whoever is preceded by three children in Islam, and the protection he has thereby taken. > **""He has taken a firm shield, whoever is preceded by three children in Islam.""**"
"3975","Abd al-Rahman ibn Aidh reported that Shurahbil ibn al-Simt asked Amr ibn Abasah رضي الله عنه for a narration heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he affirmed and narrated a divine address concerning those who line up for Allah's sake and those who aid one another for His sake, together with the reward of every believing man or woman whom Allah bereaves of three children of their loins before puberty. > **""Allah Almighty says: My love is due for those who line up for My sake, and My love is due for those who aid one another for My sake. And there is no believing man nor believing woman whom Allah bereaves of three children of their loins before puberty except that Allah admits him to Paradise by the grace of His mercy toward them.""**"
"3976","Abd al-Rahman ibn Bashir al-Ansari رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning whoever loses three children before puberty, and his passing over the Fire only as one crossing a path, meaning the crossing over the Sirat. > **""Whoever loses three children before puberty shall not enter the Fire except as a passer-by, meaning the crossing over the Sirat.""**"
"3977","Habibah reported that she was with Aisha رضي الله عنها when the Prophet ﷺ came in to her and spoke concerning any two Muslims who lose three children before puberty: that they shall be brought on the Day of Resurrection until stood at the gate of Paradise and told to enter, that they shall refuse until their fathers enter, and that they shall then be told to enter together with their fathers. > **""There are no two Muslims who lose three children before puberty except that they are brought on the Day of Resurrection until stood at the gate of Paradise, and it is said to them: Enter Paradise. So they say: Not until our fathers enter. So it is said to them: Enter Paradise, you and your fathers.""**"
"3978","Zuhayr ibn Alqamah reported that a woman of the Ansar came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning a son of hers who had died, and when the people rebuked her she declared that two sons besides this one had died since she entered Islam, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ described the protection she had thereby prepared against the Fire. > **""Thou hast barricaded against the Fire with a strong barricade.""**"
"3979","Sinan, the freedman of Wathilah رضي الله عنه, reported that when the son of al-Rayyan died and Wathilah attended him, then sat at the gate of Damascus upon returning from the graveyard, al-Rayyan passed by him and Wathilah greeted him with wishes that Allah mend his calamity and forgive his deceased. He then cited what he had heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning whoever buries three children and what Allah forbids for him. > **""Whoever buries three children, Allah forbids the Fire for him.""**"
"3980","Abu Thalabah al-Ashjai رضي الله عنه reported that he told the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that two sons of his had died in Islam, and the Prophet ﷺ stated the reward of whoever loses two children in Islam. The narrator added that Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه afterwards met him, asked whether he was the one addressed concerning the two children, and upon his affirmation declared that such an address to himself would have been dearer to him than all over which Hims and Palestine are locked. > **""Whoever loses two children in Islam, Allah admits him to Paradise by the grace of His mercy toward them.""**"
"3981","Jabir رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak concerning whoever loses three children and seeks reward for them, and when they asked about two he affirmed the same. Mahmud then told Jabir that had they asked about one, he would likely have affirmed it likewise, and Jabir swore by Allah that he thought so too. > **""Whoever loses three children and seeks reward for them enters Paradise. And two likewise.""**"
"3982","Muadh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concisely concerning the possessor of three, and when Muadh asked about the possessor of two he affirmed him likewise, with the addition concerning one to follow in the coming chapter. > **""The possessor of three has made it obligatory. And the possessor of two likewise.""**"
"3983","al-Harith ibn Uqaysh رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning any two Muslims who lose four children, and when they asked about three and then about two he affirmed each likewise, all admitted to Paradise by the grace of His mercy. > **""There are no two Muslims who lose four children except that Allah admits them to Paradise by the grace of His mercy. And three likewise. And two likewise.""**"
"3984","al-Harith ibn Uqaysh رضي الله عنه reported that while they were with Abu Barzah رضي الله عنه he narrated to them that night from the Prophet ﷺ concerning any two Muslims who lose four farats, and when they asked about three and then about two he affirmed each likewise. The Prophet ﷺ then added that among his nation is one magnified for the Fire until he becomes one of its corners, and among his nation is one by whose intercession shall enter Paradise the like of Mudar. > **""There are no two Muslims who lose four farats except that Allah admits them to Paradise by the grace of His mercy. And three likewise. And two likewise. And among my nation is one who shall be magnified for the Fire until he becomes one of its corners, and among my nation is one by whose intercession shall enter Paradise the like of Mudar.""**"
"3985","Umm Sulaym bint Milhan رضي الله عنها, the mother of Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ thrice stated the reward of any two Muslims who lose three children before puberty, and when she asked about two he affirmed them likewise. > **""There are no two Muslims who lose three children before puberty except that Allah admits them to Paradise by the grace of His mercy. And two likewise.""**"
"3986","Buraydah رضي الله عنه reported that while he was with the Prophet ﷺ word reached him that the son of a woman of the Ansar had died and she had panicked over him, so the Prophet ﷺ rose with his Companions and, upon reaching the woman's door, it was announced to her that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ sought to enter and console her. He entered and confronted her with what had reached him, she protested that she could not but panic since she was a raqub with no child living for her, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ corrected her definition and stated the reward of the Muslim man or woman who loses a soul, or three of his children, seeking reward. When Umar رضي الله عنه, at the right of the Prophet ﷺ, asked about two, the Prophet of Allah ﷺ affirmed them likewise. > **""The raqub is only she whose child lives. No Muslim woman nor Muslim man loses a soul, or three of his children, seeking reward for them, except that Paradise becomes obligatory for him. And two likewise.""**"
"3987","Zuhayr ibn Abi Alqamah reported that a woman of the Ansar came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with a son of hers and declared that two sons besides this one had died, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the barricade she had thereby taken against the Fire. > **""Thou hast barricaded against the Fire with a strong barricade.""**"
"3988","Abu Thalabah al-Khushani رضي الله عنه reported that when two sons of his died he told the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who stated the reward of whoever loses two. Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه afterwards met him and asked whether he was the one addressed concerning the two children, and upon his affirmation declared that such a narration to himself would have been dearer to him than all over which Palestine is locked. > **""Whoever loses two children, Allah admits him to Paradise by the grace of His mercy toward them.""**"
"3989","Aisha رضي الله عنها reported that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak concerning whoever sends forth aught of his children, patient and seeking reward, and how they shall veil him from the Fire by Allah's leave. > **""Whoever sends forth aught of his children, patient and seeking reward, they shall veil him from the Fire by Allah's leave.""**"
"3990","Umm Mubashshir رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed her concerning whoever has three farats among his children, while she was cooking a dish. When she asked about two farats, he affirmed them likewise. > **""O Umm Mubashshir, whoever has three farats among his children, Allah admits him to Paradise by the grace of His mercy toward them. Or two farats.""**"
"3991","Muadh رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning any two Muslims who lose three children, and when they asked about two and then about one he affirmed each likewise. He then swore by Him in whose Hand is his soul concerning the miscarried foetus dragging its mother by its cord to Paradise when she seeks reward for it. > **""There are no two Muslims who lose three of their children except that Allah admits them to Paradise by the grace of His mercy toward them. Or two. Or one. And by Him in whose Hand is my soul, the miscarried foetus shall drag its mother by its cord to Paradise if she seeks reward for it.""**"
"3992","Hassan ibn Kurayb reported that when a boy of theirs died and his parents grieved intensely, Hawshab, a Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, offered to convey what he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say concerning the like of their son: that a man among his Companions had a son just toddling who used to come with his father to the Prophet ﷺ, then the son died and the father grieved and stayed away from the Prophet ﷺ near six days. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked after the man and was told his son had died and he grieved, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ put to him a series of choices between the boy's presence at each stage of life and the reward of Paradise. > **""O so-and-so, wouldst thou love thy son to be with thee now, most lively of boys in liveliness? Wouldst thou love thy son to be with thee, most daring of lads in daring? Wouldst thou love thy son to be with thee, grown, best of grown men? Or that it be said to thee: Enter Paradise as reward for what was taken from thee?""**"
"3993","Qurrah ibn Iyas رضي الله عنه reported that a man used to come to the Prophet ﷺ with a son of his, and when the Prophet ﷺ asked whether he loved him he affirmed it and invoked the like of Allah's love for the Prophet. When the Prophet ﷺ then missed him and asked after so-and-so son of so-and-so, they told him he had died, so the Prophet ﷺ asked his father whether he would not love to find the boy awaiting him at every gate of Paradise he comes to. When a man asked whether this was for him specially or for them all, he affirmed it for them all. > **""Wouldst thou not love never to come to any gate of Paradise but find him awaiting thee? Rather, for all of you.""**"
"3994","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that a woman came to the Prophet ﷺ with a sick son of hers asking the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to supplicate Allah to heal him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked her whether she had a farat, she affirmed it, he asked whether in Jahiliyyah or in Islam, she affirmed in Islam, and he thrice gave her the glad tidings. > **""A firm shield, a firm shield, a firm shield.""**"
"3995","Jabir ibn Samurah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated the reward of whoever buries three, patient and seeking reward. When Umm Ayman رضي الله عنها asked about two, he stated the same for whoever buries two, patient and seeking reward for them. When Umm Ayman asked about one, he fell silent and paused, then addressed her concerning whoever buries one, patient and seeking reward. > **""Whoever buries three and is patient over them and seeks reward, Paradise becomes obligatory for him. Whoever buries two and is patient over them and seeks reward for them, Paradise becomes obligatory for him. O Umm Ayman, whoever buries one and is patient over him and seeks reward, Paradise becomes obligatory for him.""**"
"3996","Abdullah ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that a man of the Ansar had a son who would go out whenever the Prophet ﷺ went out. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him whether he loved the boy, he affirmed it and invoked the like of Allah's love for the Prophet, and the Messenger replied that Allah is more intense in love for him than the man for the boy. Not long after, the son died and the man came to the Prophet ﷺ with his son's company now gone. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whether he had panicked and he affirmed it, the Messenger asked whether he would not be pleased at his son's company with Ibrahim beneath the shade of the Throne. > **""Wouldst thou not be pleased that thy son be with my son Ibrahim, playing with him beneath the shade of the Throne?""**"
"3997","Qays ibn Abi Hazim reported that Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه saw boys playing with his son and declared them lighter in his sight than their number of dung-beetles."
"3998","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning whoever loses a child, male or female, whether submitting or not, content or not, patient or not, and that his reward shall be naught less than Paradise. > **""Whoever loses a child, male or female, submitting or not submitting, content or not content, patient or not patient, his reward shall be naught less than Paradise.""**"
"3999","Sahl ibn Hunayf رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded marriage on account of his boasting of their multitude over the nations, and described the miscarried foetus standing haughtily at the gate of Paradise, told to enter, refusing until his parents enter. > **""Marry, for I shall boast of your multitude over the nations. And the miscarried foetus shall be seen standing haughtily at the gate of Paradise. It is said to him: Enter. He says: Not until my parents enter.""**"
"4000","One of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ speak concerning the Day of Resurrection, when it shall be said to the children to enter Paradise and they shall refuse until their fathers and mothers enter. They shall hold back, Allah Almighty shall ask why He sees them standing haughtily and command them to enter, they shall plead their fathers, and He shall command them and their fathers to enter together. > **""It shall be said to the children on the Day of Resurrection: Enter Paradise. So they say: O Lord, not until our fathers and mothers enter. So they refuse. So Allah Almighty says: Why do I see them standing haughtily? Enter Paradise. So they say: O Lord, our fathers. So He says: Enter Paradise, you and your fathers.""**"
"4001","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood over a gathering of Banu Salamah and asked who the raqub is among them. They named him who has no child, but he corrected them to him who has no farat. He then asked who the destitute is among them. They named him who has no wealth, but he corrected them to him who comes forth with no good with Allah. > **""O Banu Salamah, who is the raqub among you? Nay, he is who has no farat. Who is the destitute among you? Nay, he is who comes forth with no good with Allah.""**"
"4002","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whom they count as raqub among them. They named him who has no child, but he corrected them to him who has no farat. > **""Whom do you count as raqub among you? Nay, he is who has no farat.""**"
"4003","A man who witnessed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ preaching reported that he asked whether they knew what the raqub is. They named him who has no child, but he declared thrice that the utter raqub is he who has a child then dies without sending forth aught of them. He then asked whether they knew what the destitute is. They named him who has no wealth, but he declared thrice that the utter destitute is he who has wealth then dies without sending forth aught of it. The Prophet ﷺ then asked what wrestling is. They named throwing down, but the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared thrice that utter wrestling belongs to the man whose anger intensifies until his face reddens and his hair stands, then his anger throws him down, meaning he overcomes it. > **""Do you know what the raqub is? The utter raqub, the utter raqub, the utter raqub is he who has a child then dies without sending forth aught of them. Do you know what the destitute is? The utter destitute, the utter destitute, the utter destitute is he who has wealth then dies without sending forth aught of it. What is wrestling? Utter wrestling, utter wrestling, utter wrestling is the man who angers, so his anger intensifies, his face reddens, his hair stands on end, then his anger throws him down.""**"
"4004","Aisha رضي الله عنها reported that in his illness the Messenger of Allah ﷺ lifted a curtain and opened a door, looked at the people praying behind Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه, and rejoiced at it. He praised Allah that no prophet died until a man of his nation led him in prayer, then turned to the people and bade whoever among them is struck by a calamity after him to console himself through his calamity by the Prophet for the calamity striking him, since his nation shall never be struck after him with the like of their calamity through him. > **""Praise be to Allah, no prophet ever died until a man of his nation led him in prayer. O people, whoever among you is struck by a calamity after me, let him console himself through his calamity by me for the calamity striking him, for my nation shall never be struck after me with the like of their calamity by me.""**"
"4005","Sahl ibn Hunayf رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that whoever among them has no farat shall not enter Paradise except sparingly. When a man protested that not all of them have a farat, he answered with a question concerning missing one's Muslim brother. > **""Whoever among you has no farat shall not enter Paradise except sparingly. Is it not of one's farat that he misses his Muslim brother?""**"
"4006","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that when the Prophet ﷺ was consoled for his daughter Ruqayyah رضي الله عنها, he praised Allah and declared the burial of daughters to be among honours. > **""Praise be to Allah, the burial of daughters is among honours.""**"
"4007","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to speak concerning the absence of regret in this world except in three cases: a man with a source of watering and a drawing-camel watering his land, whose she-camel dies just as his land's thirst intensifies and its fruit emerges, leaving him regretting his trained watering camel of which he shall find no like and regretting his land's fruit lest it spoil before he contrives for it; a man upon a swift horse meeting a company of disbelievers, whose enemies flee as they draw near and he alone remains upon his horse, then just as he is about to catch them his horse breaks under him and he is left standing by it, regretting his horse of which he shall find no like and regretting the victory that had loomed before him; and a man with a wife whose form and religion please him, who bears him a boy then dies in her childbed, leaving him regretting his wife whom he thinks he shall never match and regretting her child whom he fears may perish neglected before he finds it a wet-nurse. He declared this last the greatest of those regrets. > **""There is no regret in this world except in three: a man who had a source of watering and a drawing-camel watering his land with it, then when his land's thirst intensified and its fruit emerged his drawing-camel died, so he regrets his watering camel trained in watering, that he shall find no like of it, and regrets his land's fruit, that it spoil before he contrives for it; a man upon a swift horse who met a company of disbelievers, then when they drew near one another the enemies of Allah fled, and the man remained upon his horse, then when he was about to catch them his horse broke under him and he was left standing by it, so he regrets his horse, that he shall find no like of it, and regrets what escaped him of the victory that had loomed before him; and a man with a wife whose form and religion pleased him, who bore him a boy then died in her childbed, so he regrets his wife, thinking he shall never meet her like, and regrets her child, fearing it perish neglected before he finds it a wet-nurse. So this is the greatest of those regrets.""**"
"4008","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning three things that shall not cease in his nation until the Hour rises: wailing, boasting in lineages, and seeking rain by the stars. > **""Three shall not cease in my nation until the Hour rises: wailing, boasting in lineages, and the stars.""**"
"4009","Junadah ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak concerning three matters of Jahiliyyah that the people of Islam shall never abandon: seeking rain by the stars, defamation in lineage, and wailing over the dead. > **""Three are of the affair of Jahiliyyah which the people of Islam shall never abandon: seeking rain by the stars, defamation in lineage, and wailing over the dead.""**"
"4010","Awf ibn Malik al-Muzani رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning three matters of Jahiliyyah that men shall not abandon: defamation in lineage, wailing, and their saying that they were rained by such-and-such star. > **""Three are of the affair of Jahiliyyah which men shall not abandon: defamation in lineage, wailing, and their saying: We were rained by such-and-such star.""**"
"4011","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning four things in his nation which they shall not abandon: pride in ancestries, defamation in lineages, and wailing, with the wailing woman who dies without repentance raised on the Day of Resurrection wearing a coat of tar. > **""Four are in my nation which they shall not abandon: pride in ancestries, defamation in lineages, and wailing. The wailing woman shall be raised on the Day of Resurrection, if she did not repent, wearing a coat of tar.""**"
"4012","al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took his hand and addressed him concerning three things his people shall not abandon: defamation in lineage, wailing, and seeking rain by the stars. > **""O Abbas, three thy people shall not abandon: defamation in lineage, wailing, and seeking rain by the stars.""**"
"4013","Salman رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet of Allah ﷺ concerning three matters of Jahiliyyah: pride in ancestries, defamation in lineages, and wailing. > **""Three are of Jahiliyyah: pride in ancestries, defamation in lineages, and wailing.""**"
"4014","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conquered Makkah, Iblis wailed a wail and his troops gathered to him. They declared despair of ever turning the nation of Muhammad ﷺ back to idolatry after that day, but resolved to tempt them in their religion and spread wailing among them."
"4015","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning the wailing woman and the woman who raises her voice in lamentation, and that the angels do not invoke blessings upon them. > **""The angels do not invoke blessings upon a wailing woman nor upon a lamenting woman.""**"
"4016","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ cursed the wailing woman and the woman who listens to her, and declared that women have no share in the funeral. > **""Women have no share in the funeral.""**"
"4017","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning two voices cursed in this world and the Hereafter: pipes at a blessing and wailing at a calamity. > **""Two voices are cursed in this world and the Hereafter: pipes at a blessing, and wailing at a calamity.""**"
"4018","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak concerning any wailing woman who dies before repenting, and how Allah shall clothe her and raise her before men on the Day of Resurrection. > **""Any wailing woman who dies before repenting, Allah shall clothe her in a garment of fire and raise her before men on the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"4019","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning the wailing women on the Day of Resurrection, made into two rows in Hell, one on the right of the people of the Fire and one on their left, barking at them as dogs bark. > **""These wailing women shall be made on the Day of Resurrection two rows in Hell: a row on their right and a row on their left, and they shall bark at the people of the Fire as dogs bark.""**"
"4020","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ cursed the wailing woman and the woman who listens to her. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ cursed the wailing woman and the listening woman.""**"
"4021","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning wailing women and the garments of tar upon them. > **""Wailing women shall wear garments of tar.""**"
"4022","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the wailing woman on the Day of Resurrection upon a path between Paradise and Hell, clothed in garments of tar with the Fire covering her face, if she did not repent. > **""The wailing woman on the Day of Resurrection shall be upon a path between Paradise and Hell, her garments of tar, and the Fire covering her face, if she did not repent.""**"
"4023","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he forbade wailing. > **""The Prophet ﷺ forbade wailing.""**"
"4024","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that no wailing was raised over the Prophet ﷺ. > **""No wailing was raised over the Prophet ﷺ.""**"
"4025","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that he heard the Prophet ﷺ visit Abu Salamah رضي الله عنه in his ailment, and hearing the voice of Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها weeping over him with the verse of the Book concerning the stupor of death bringing the truth, he held back from entering. He then entered, greeted, and invoked Allah's succession over her. When he came out with Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه, Abu Bakr observed that he had disliked entering because they were wailing, and the Prophet ﷺ declared the houses he does not enter. > **""May Allah succeed thee, O Umm Salamah. I do not enter a house wherein there is wailing, nor wherein there is a black dog.""**"
"4026","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reported that while walking with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ he heard the voice of lamentation, so the Prophet ﷺ bade him go and see what it was. They said Abdullah ibn Rawahah رضي الله عنه had died, but the Prophet ﷺ declared he had not died. The man then recovered from the faint that had seized him, was told the Prophet ﷺ was coming to him, and came out to meet him, explaining that he had fainted and the women had cried out in his praise while an angel bearing an iron mace stood it between his legs and challenged him over every word they uttered, which he denied, knowing a blow would follow assent. > **""Go and see what this is. He has not died.""**"
"4027","Al-Hasan al-Basri reported concerning Muadh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه falling unconscious while his sister raised lamentations over him, and his address to her when he recovered, explaining what had harmed him and what the stern angel had said at each of her cries. > **""You have continued to harm me today. There was constantly a sternly rebuking angel; whenever you said, 'And such-and-such,' he said, 'Are you such?' and I said, 'No.'""**"
"4028","Musab ibn Nuh reported that he met an elderly woman of his family who was among those who had pledged allegiance to the Prophet ﷺ, and she described coming to him one day when he took their pledge upon the matter of wailing. > **""You shall not wail.""**"
"4029","Samurah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he spoke concerning the dead person over whom wailing is raised, and the punishment that befalls him on that account. > **""The dead is punished for what is wailed over him.""**"
"4030","Aisha رضي الله عنها, wife of the Prophet ﷺ, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning a disbeliever from among the disbelievers of Quraysh who died while his family wept over him enumerating his feeding of bowls and his fighting, and what Allah added to him thereby. > **""A disbeliever from among the disbelievers of Quraysh would die and his family would weep over him saying, 'The feeder of bowls, the fighter who...,' so Allah would increase him in punishment for what they said.""**"
"4031","Umm Abbas reported concerning Umm Sad raising her voice over Sad, saying woe for the mother of Sad over his firmness and earnestness, and the instruction the Prophet ﷺ gave her not to exceed that, together with his testimony to what he knew of Sad. > **""Do not add to this, do not add to this. By Allah, as far as I knew he was cautious in the affair of Allah and strong in the affair of Allah.""**"
"4032","Muhammad ibn Ishaq reported concerning what Umm Sad said weeping when the bier of Sad was carried, describing him as firm, earnest, and a chief through whom protection was sought, and the verdict the Prophet ﷺ gave upon such weeping. > **""Every weeping woman lies except the one who weeps for Sad ibn Muadh.""**"
"4033","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reported that she asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the women of Banu Makhzum establishing mourning over al-Walid ibn al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah, that he gave permission, and what the mourning woman said weeping in verse over the brother of the clan. > **""I weep for al-Walid ibn al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah, I weep for al-Walid ibn al-Walid, the brother of the clan.""**"
"4034","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ disavowed whoever, at calamity, strikes the cheeks, tears the openings of garments, or calls out with the call of Jahiliyyah. > **""He is not from us who strikes the cheeks, tears the collars, and calls with the call of Jahiliyyah.""**"
"4035","Jabir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ disavowed whoever, at calamity, shaves the head, raises the voice in lamentation, or tears the garment. > **""He is not from us who shaves, nor who raises the voice in lamentation, nor who tears.""**"
"4036","Muadh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported that when the Prophet ﷺ sent him to Yemen, he ﷺ went out with him while Muadh rode and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ walked beneath his mount, foretold that Muadh might not meet him after that year but would pass by his grave and his mosque, and when Muadh wept out of eagerness at parting, forbade him with an explanation of such weeping. > **""O Muadh, perhaps you will not meet me after this year of mine, and you will pass by my grave and my mosque. Do not weep, O Muadh, for weeping is from Satan.""**"
"4037","Aisha رضي الله عنها reported that when Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr died and weeping was raised over him, Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه came out and excused the women as newly removed from Jahiliyyah, citing what he had heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning the dead and the weeping of the living. > **""The dead has boiling water sprinkled over him on account of the weeping of the living.""**"
"4038","Samurah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning the dead and the consequence upon him of the weeping of the living. > **""The dead is punished by the weeping of the living.""**"
"4039","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning the dead and the weeping of the living over him. > **""The dead is indeed punished by the weeping of the living.""**"
"4040","Hajib ibn Umar reported that he entered with al-Hakam ibn al-Araj upon Bakr ibn Abdullah al-Muzani while they discussed the dead being punished by the weeping of the living, whereupon Bakr narrated from a Companion of the Prophet ﷺ, contrasted it with the objection raised by Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه in the form of a question about a martyr slain far away whose widow weeps foolishly, and mentioned the reply of a man present affirming the Messenger and denying Abu Hurayrah. > **""By Allah, if a man goes out striving in the path of Allah then is killed as a martyr in some region of the earth, and his wife deliberately out of folly or ignorance weeps over him, will this martyr be punished for the weeping of this foolish woman over him?""**"
"4041","Abu al-Rabi reported that he was with Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما in a funeral procession when he heard the voice of a man shouting, so Ibn Umar sent to him and silenced him, and when asked why he had silenced him, explained the harm reaching the dead until he enters his grave. > **""The dead is harmed by it until he enters his grave.""**"
"4042","Rabi al-Ansari رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ visited the son of the brother of Jabr al-Ansari while his family wept over him, that Jabr told them not to harm the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with their voices, and that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ instructed that they be left until the soul becomes due. > **""Leave them to weep so long as he is alive; when it becomes due, then let them be silent.""**"
"4043","Asma bint Umays رضي الله عنها reported that when Jafar رضي الله عنه was struck down, the Prophet ﷺ came to them and instructed her regarding wearing the garments of grief for three days and then doing as she wished. > **""Mourn in grief for three days, then do as you wish.""**"
"4044","Asma bint Umays رضي الله عنها reported in another narration that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon her on the third day after the killing of Jafar رضي الله عنه and forbade her from further observance of mourning after that day. > **""Do not observe mourning after this day of yours.""**"
"4045","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reported that Asma wept over Hamzah رضي الله عنه and Jafar رضي الله عنه for three days, after which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded her to restrain her tears and apply kohl. > **""Restrain your tears and apply kohl.""**"
"4046","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that when Uthman ibn Mazun رضي الله عنه died his wife said congratulations to him upon Paradise, at which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ looked at her in anger and questioned her knowledge, and upon her appeal to his horsemanship and companionship declared his own station yet his lack of independent knowledge of what shall be done with him, so that the people feared for Uthman. He then reported that when Zaynab رضي الله عنها, daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, died, the Prophet ﷺ addressed her to join their righteous predecessor, the women wept, Umar رضي الله عنه moved to strike them, the Prophet ﷺ held his hand and restrained him, permitted weeping while warning against the croaking of Satan, and distinguished what comes from heart and eye from what comes from hand and tongue. In an added narration he sat at the edge of the grave with Fatimah رضي الله عنها weeping beside him and wiped from her out of mercy with his garment. > **""What do you know? I am indeed the Messenger of Allah, yet I do not know what will be done with me. Join our righteous predecessor Uthman ibn Mazun. Gently, O Umar. Weep, but beware of the croaking of Satan. Whatever is from the heart and the eye is from Allah Almighty and from mercy, and whatever is from the hand and the tongue is from Satan.""**"
"4047","Abdur Rahman ibn Awf رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ took his hand and went with him to his son Ibrahim while the child breathed his last, that the Prophet ﷺ took him into his lap until his soul departed, then laid him down and wept, and when questioned about weeping despite forbidding it, distinguished weeping from two foolish sinful voices, described tears as mercy, and addressed Ibrahim concerning the true promise, the joining of the last with the first, grief of heart and eye, and refusal to utter what angers the Lord. > **""I did not forbid weeping, but I forbade two foolish sinful voices: a voice at blessing with idle play and the flutes of Satan, and a voice at calamity with striking faces and tearing collars. And this is mercy, and whoever does not show mercy will not be shown mercy. O Ibrahim, were it not for a true promise and a just word, and that our last shall join our first, we would have grieved over you with a grief severer than this. And we are indeed grieved over you, O Ibrahim; the eye weeps and the heart grieves, and we do not say what displeases the Lord Almighty.""**"
"4048","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reported that when Ibrahim, son of the Prophet ﷺ, died, a man came to the Prophet ﷺ while his eyes shed tears and objected to his weeping over the infant, swearing that he himself had buried twelve sons alive in Jahiliyyah older than him, and the Prophet of Allah ﷺ replied questioning what can be done if mercy has departed, affirming grief and tears without displeasing speech. > **""What then if mercy has departed from you? The heart grieves and the eye sheds tears, and we do not say what displeases the Lord, and we are indeed grieved over Ibrahim.""**"
"4049","Saib ibn Yazid رضي الله عنه reported that when Tahir, son of the Prophet ﷺ, perished, the eye of the Prophet ﷺ shed tears, and when it was said to him in surprise that he wept, he explained the shedding of the eye, the overcoming of tears, and the grief of the heart without disobedience to Allah. > **""The eye sheds, tears overcome, and the heart grieves, yet we do not disobey Allah Almighty.""**"
"4050","Abdur Rahman ibn Awf رضي الله عنه reported that a daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent word that her daughter was overcome, that the Prophet ﷺ twice returned the messenger with the same message of consolation regarding what Allah takes and gives, then went with some Companions upon a third summons, was handed the child while her soul rattled in her chest, showed tenderness until his eyes shed tears, and when his Companions looked on in wonder, explained it as mercy placed by Allah where He wills and His mercy upon the merciful among His servants. > **""Say to her: to Allah belongs what He has taken and to Him belongs what He has given. What is the matter with you that you look on? It is the mercy of Allah which He places where He wills; Allah only shows mercy to the merciful among His servants.""**"
"4051","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported concerning the son of Fatimah رضي الله عنها falling grievously ill, her summoning of the Prophet ﷺ, his first reply that to Allah belongs what He takes and what He leaves and that all is for a decreed term, then his rising with his Companions when the child approached death, his recitation of the verses of Surat al-Waqiah when the soul reached the throat until the child passed, the flowing of his eyes, and his answer to Sad رضي الله عنه who questioned his weeping despite forbidding it. > **""When the soul reaches the throat and you at that time are looking on. It is only mercy, and Allah only shows mercy to the merciful among His servants.""**"
"4052","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that a daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ approached death, that he ﷺ came to her, embraced her to his breast while his eyes shed tears, that Umm Ayman رضي الله عنها wept and was questioned about weeping in the presence of the Messenger, and upon her reply that she wept because he wept, the Prophet ﷺ clarified that it was not wailing but mercy at beholding the soul being drawn forth. > **""Do you weep while the Messenger of Allah is with you? I do not weep, but it is mercy; I looked at her in this state while her soul was being drawn forth.""**"
"4053","Salim Abu al-Nadr reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon Uthman ibn Mazun رضي الله عنه while he was dying, ordered a cloth to cover him, remained bent over him a long time with his Companions while Uthman lodged with an Ansari woman called Umm Muadh, then withdrew weeping until the household wept, invoked mercy upon Abu al-Saib, and when Umm Muadh congratulated him upon Paradise, questioned her knowledge while affirming that certainty had come to him and that they knew only good of him, whereupon she vowed never to say it again for anyone after him. > **""May Allah have mercy upon you, O Abu al-Saib. What makes you know, O Umm Muadh? As for him, certainty has come to him, and we know only good.""**"
"4054","Jabir ibn Abdullah رضي الله عنهما reported that on the day of Uhud his father was slain by the polytheists who then mutilated him by cutting his nose and ears, that Jabir looked upon him and cried out, the Ansar covered him with a cloth, he uncovered and cried out again until they covered him again, all before the eyes of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and when the Ansar complained to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about his conduct, he ordered that he be left. > **""Leave him.""**"
"4055","Aidh ibn Amr رضي الله عنه reported that he was with the Prophet ﷺ on a campaign, that upon returning the wife of a man martyred alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wept, the Prophet ﷺ asked who the weeping woman was, was told she was Fatimah bint Ali, then turned to Aidh ibn Amr, married her to him, and entrusted him concerning her. > **""Who is this weeping woman?""**"
"4056","Abdullah ibn Yazid stated, as a report of his own, that concession was granted for weeping without wailing."
"4057","Amir ibn Sad reported that he entered a shelter in which were Qarazah ibn Kab رضي الله عنه and Abu Masud al-Ansari رضي الله عنه, and they narrated a hadith in which they stated that concession had been granted to them for weeping at calamity without wailing."
"4058","Umm Ishaq رضي الله عنها reported that she migrated with her brother toward the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Madinah, that on the way her brother asked her to wait while he returned to Makkah for his forgotten provision, she feared her wicked husband for him but he assured her if Allah willed, she waited days until a man she knew but would not name told her that her wicked husband had overtaken Ishaq and killed him, so she came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he performed ablution, told him weeping of the killing as he looked at her, and when she looked at him he lowered his gaze and sprinkled water from his palm upon her face. Bashshar added from his grandmother that thereafter even at great calamity tears would appear in their eyes without reaching their cheeks. > **""What keeps you sitting here, O Umm Ishaq? There is no Ishaq for you; your wicked husband overtook him and killed him.""**"
"4059","Abdullah ibn Utbah رضي الله عنه reported that when Utbah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه died, Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه wept, and when the people asked him in surprise, he affirmed it on account of brotherhood of lineage, companionship with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and love surpassed only by Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه, adding that he would not wish to have died before him, for that the other die and he reckon reward is dearer to him than the reverse. > **""Yes. He is my brother by lineage, my companion with the Messenger of Allah, and the most beloved of people to me, except for what is due to Umar ibn al-Khattab. That he should die and I reckon him in reward is dearer to me than that I should die and he reckon me.""**"
"4060","Umm Abdullah, wife of Abu Musa رضي الله عنه, reported that when Abu Musa fell ill she wept beside him and was forbidden, whereupon he instructed that she be left to pour forth a skinful or two of her tears. > **""Leave her to pour forth a skinful or two of her tears.""**"
"4061","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ restricted weeping to two kinds of men: the flagrantly sinful who completed his sinfulness, and the righteous who completed his righteousness. > **""Weeping is only for one of two men: a flagrantly sinful one who completed his sinfulness, or a righteous one who completed his righteousness.""**"
"4062","Aisha رضي الله عنها reported that she entered upon Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه, saw death upon him, and recited a verse lamenting one whose tears remain veiled yet shall once be buried, whereupon he forbade that wording and instructed her to recite instead the verse of Surat Qaf concerning the stupor of death bringing the truth. > **""And the stupor of death comes with the truth; that is what you used to flee from.""**"
"4063","Amir ibn Rabiah رضي الله عنه stated, as a report of his own, that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ kiss Uthman ibn Mazun رضي الله عنه after his death."
"4064","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded that whoever dies in the morning shall rest at midday only in his grave, and whoever dies in the evening shall spend the night only in his grave. > **""Whoever dies in the morning shall not rest at midday except in his grave, and whoever dies in the evening shall not spend the night except in his grave.""**"
"4065","Aisha رضي الله عنها reported that when death approached Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه he asked which day it was, was told it was Monday, and instructed that if he died that night they should not wait for the morrow, for the most beloved days and nights to him were those nearest to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""If I die tonight, do not wait with me for tomorrow, for the most beloved of days and nights to me are those nearest to the Messenger of Allah.""**"
"4066","Jabir ibn Abdullah رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ enumerated the rewards for digging a grave, washing the dead, shrouding, consoling the grieved and the afflicted, following the bier until burial, and sheltering the orphan and widow. > **""Whoever digs a grave, Allah builds for him a house in Paradise. Whoever washes a dead person emerges from his sins as on the day his mother bore him. Whoever shrouds a dead person, Allah clothes him from the garments of Paradise. Whoever consoles a grieving one, Allah clothes him with piety and sends blessings upon his spirit among the spirits. Whoever consoles an afflicted one, Allah clothes him with two garments of Paradise for which the world cannot stand. Whoever follows a bier until its burial is decreed, three qirats are written for him, each qirat greater than Mount Uhud. Whoever shelters an orphan or a widow, Allah shades him in His shade and admits him to Paradise.""**"
"4067","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning whoever washes a dead person and conceals what he sees, and whoever shrouds him, and the purification and clothing with sundus granted in return. > **""Whoever washes a dead person and conceals it from him, Allah purifies him of his sins; and if he shrouds him, Allah clothes him from sundus.""**"
"4068","Abu Rafi رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning whoever washes a dead person and conceals him, and whoever digs a grave for his brother until he lays him within it, likening the latter to providing a dwelling until resurrection. > **""Whoever washes a dead person and conceals him, Allah forgives him forty major sins. Whoever digs for his brother a grave until he lays him within it, it is as if he has settled him in a dwelling until he is raised.""**"
"4069","Aisha رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning whoever washes a dead person while fulfilling trust and not spreading what appears at that time, describing his emergence from sins as on the day of birth, and instructed that the nearest to the deceased should undertake him if known, otherwise one deemed possessed of piety and trustworthiness. > **""Whoever washes a dead person and fulfils trust therein and does not spread what appears of him at that time emerges from his sins as on the day his mother bore him. Let the nearest of you to him undertake him if he knows; and if he does not know, then one whom you deem possessed of piety and trustworthiness.""**"
"4070","Muawiyah ibn Khadij رضي الله عنه, who had companionship, stated as a report of his own that whoever washes a dead person, shrouds him, follows him, and takes charge of his body returns forgiven."
"4071","Abu Said al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that the dead recognizes who carries him, who washes him, and who lowers him into his grave. Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما, present in the gathering, asked from whom this was heard, was told from Abu Said, then went to Abu Said and asked him directly, and Abu Said affirmed hearing it from the Prophet ﷺ. > **""The dead indeed recognizes who carries him, who washes him, and who lowers him into his grave.""**"
"4072","Muhammad ibn Sirin stated, as a report of his own, that he washed Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه, and when he reached the private parts he told his sons that they had greater right to wash it, so the one who washed it placed a rag upon his hand with a garment over the body and washed that part from beneath the garment."
"4073","Humayd reported, as a narration of his own, that when Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه died there was placed in his embalming perfume a small vessel containing some of the sweat of the Prophet ﷺ."
"4074","Umm Sulaym رضي الله عنها, mother of Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gave detailed instructions for washing a deceased woman: beginning with the belly with gentle wiping if she is not pregnant and not moving her if pregnant; beginning the washing with the lower part while a covering cloth veils her nakedness; washing with cotton and washing well, then inserting the hand beneath the cloth and wiping thrice with cotton before ablution; performing ablution with water containing lote leaves while another standing woman pours and does nothing else until purification, with the nearest kin undertaking it or otherwise a pious Muslim woman, assisted by another if small or weak; washing the lower part cleanly with lote and water, then ablution as for prayer; then three washings with water and lote beginning with the head and cleansing it of lote with water without combing with a comb; increasing to five if impurity recurs after three, and to seven if after five, all in odd numbers with water and lote, with some camphor and lote in the third or fifth, placed in a new jar; then seating her and pouring from head to feet, covering with a clean cloth, removing the soiled covering from behind it, stuffing the lower part with as much cotton as possible; binding a long washed loincloth upon her buttocks as a waist-wrapper is tied, knotting it between her thighs, joining the thighs and drawing its end toward the knees; then perfuming and shrouding her, braiding her hair into three plaits of forelock and two braids without imitating men; shrouding in five garments including an izar wrapping the thighs; removing nothing of her hair by depilatory or otherwise, washing what falls and inserting it into the head hair, perfuming the head hair well, not washing with heated water, perfuming her and the shroud with seven sprinklings in odd numbers if wished, and likewise in odd if veiling her in her bier; and if she is confined or nursed in illness or the like, washing with a single rag following each part with water without moving her, out of fear that something may issue that cannot be returned. > **""When a woman dies and they wish to wash her, let them begin with her belly; let her belly be wiped gently if she is not pregnant, and if she is pregnant do not move her. If you wish to wash her, begin with her lower part and cast a veiling cloth over her nakedness. Then take cotton and wash her and wash her well. Then insert your hand beneath the cloth and wipe her with cotton three times, and wipe her well before you perform ablution for her. Then perform ablution for her with water containing lote leaves, and let a woman pour the water while standing, undertaking nothing else until she is cleansed with lote while you wash. Let the most entitled among people undertake her washing, otherwise a pious Muslim woman; and if she is small or weak, let another pious Muslim woman attend her. When you finish washing her lower part with a cleansing wash of lote and water, then perform ablution for her as for prayer; this is the exposition of her ablution. Then wash her after that three times with water and lote, beginning with her head before all else, and cleanse its washing of lote with water, and do not comb her head with a comb. If impurity issues after the three washings, make them five; and if it issues in the fifth, make them seven, and let all of that be odd with water and lote. And in the fifth or the third, put therein some camphor and some lote. Then place that in a new jar. Then seat her and pour over her, beginning with her head until you reach her feet. When you finish with her, cast a clean cloth over her, then insert your hand behind the cloth and remove it from her. Then stuff her lower part with cotton as much as you can. Then take a long washed loincloth and bind it upon her buttocks and tie it between her thighs and join her thighs, then cast its end toward her knees. This is the affair of her lower part. Then perfume her and shroud her, and braid her hair into three plaits: a forelock and two braids, and do not liken her to men. Let her shroud be in five garments, one of them an izar with which you wrap her thighs. Remove nothing of her hair with depilatory or otherwise, and whatever falls of her hair, wash it then insert it into the hair of her head, and perfume the hair of her head and perfume it well. Do not wash her with heated water. Perfume her and what you shroud her with, with seven sprinklings if you wish, and make everything thereof odd. And if it occurs to you to veil her in her bier, make it odd. This is the affair of her shroud and her head. And if she is confined or nursed in illness or the like, take a single rag and wash her with water, following each part of her, and do not move her, for I fear something may issue from her that cannot be returned.""**"
"4075","Al-Mughirah ibn Shubah رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ command whoever washes a dead person to take a ritual bath himself. > **""Whoever washes a dead person, let him take a ritual bath.""**"
"4076","Aisha رضي الله عنها stated, as a report of her own, that it is from the Sunnah that one of you women take something in her hands or upon her neck by which she supports the deceased woman when she is laid upon the bed of washing."
"4077","Hudhayfah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded whoever washes a dead person to take a ritual bath himself. > **""Whoever washes a dead person, let him take a ritual bath.""**"
"4078","Ibrahim al-Nakhai reported that Abdullah رضي الله عنه was asked about one who washes the dead, whether he must take a ritual bath, and replied that if they deemed their companion impure they should bathe, otherwise ablution suffices. > **""If you deem your companion impure, then bathe from him; otherwise ablution suffices you.""**"
"4079","Ishaq ibn al-Harith reported that he saw Khalid ibn al-Hawari, a man from Abyssinia among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, approach his wife, and when death approached him he instructed that he be washed twice: a washing for janaba and a washing for death. > **""Wash me two washings: a washing for janaba and a washing for death.""**"
"4080","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that Hamzah ibn Abdul Muttalib رضي الله عنه and Hanzalah ibn al-Rahib رضي الله عنه were struck down while in janaba, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that he saw the angels washing them both. > **""I saw the angels washing them both.""**"
"4081","Sinan ibn Gharafah رضي الله عنه, who had companionship, reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning a man who dies among women, or a woman who dies among men, while neither has a mahram present, that dry ablution be performed for them. > **""They shall be given tayammum.""**"
"4082","Said ibn Ubayd رضي الله عنه, who was called the Reciter in the time of the Prophet ﷺ, reported that after meeting the enemy and retreating, Umar رضي الله عنه offered him passage to Sham hoping Allah would favour him, but he refused except toward the enemy from whom he had fled. He then addressed the troops at al-Qadisiyyah, declaring that they would meet the enemy on the morrow, God willing, and would be martyred, instructing that no blood be washed from them and that they be shrouded only in the garments upon them. > **""We shall meet the enemy tomorrow, God willing, and we shall be martyred, so do not wash any blood from us, and we shall not be shrouded except in the garment that is upon us.""**"
"4083","Ali رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that the shroud is taken from the whole of the wealth. > **""The shroud is from the whole of the wealth.""**"
"4084","Ali رضي الله عنه stated, as a report of his own, that the Prophet ﷺ was shrouded in seven garments."
"4085","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ was shrouded in two sheets and a Najrani cloak."
"4086","Jabir ibn Samurah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ was shrouded in three white garments of izar and wrapping, and that Umar رضي الله عنه was shrouded in two garments."
"4087","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه instructed, as a report of his own, that when he dies they should not dress him in a shirt, for he saw that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was neither dressed in a shirt nor given a turban. > **""When I die, do not dress me in a shirt, for I saw the Messenger of Allah was neither dressed in a shirt nor given a turban.""**"
"4088","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ was shrouded in three garments, one of which was a shirt."
"4089","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ was shrouded in three garments."
"4090","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه stated, as a report of his own, that the Prophet ﷺ was shrouded in three garments: a Sanani cloak and two Hibara cloaks."
"4091","Abdullah ibn Mughaffal رضي الله عنه instructed, as a report of his own, that when he dies camphor be placed in his washing and that he be shrouded in two cloaks and a shirt, for the Prophet ﷺ was so treated. > **""When I die, place camphor in my washing and shroud me in two cloaks and a shirt, for the Prophet was so treated.""**"
"4092","Abu Ishaq reported that he asked the family of Muhammad, among them Ibn Nawfal, in what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was shrouded, and was told of a red hulla without a shirt and a strip of their velvet placed in his grave."
"4093","Abu Ishaq reported in another narration that he came to a circle of Banu Abdul Muttalib and asked their elders in how many garments the Prophet ﷺ was shrouded, and they narrated the like thereof."
"4094","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that when Hamzah ibn Abdul Muttalib رضي الله عنه was slain there was upon him a striped cloak, that Ali رضي الله عنه was the one who lowered him into his grave, that when his head was covered his feet appeared and when his feet were covered his head appeared, and that upon asking the Messenger of Allah ﷺ he was commanded to cover the head and to take some Idhkhir shrubs to place upon his feet. > **""Cover his head, and take for him some shrubs of Idhkhir and place them upon his feet.""**"
"4095","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that Hamzah رضي الله عنه was slain on the day of Uhud together with a man from the Ansar, that Safiyyah bint Abdul Muttalib رضي الله عنها brought two garments to shroud Hamzah in, that there was no shroud for the Ansari, so the Prophet ﷺ drew lots between the two garments and each of the two men was shrouded in one garment."
"4096","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that on the day of Uhud the Prophet ﷺ passed by Hamzah رضي الله عنه with his nose cut and his body mutilated, declared that were it not for the grief of Safiyyah رضي الله عنها he would have left him until Allah gathered him from the bellies of beasts and birds, and that he was shrouded in a striped cloak which, when the head was covered, exposed the feet, and when the feet were covered, exposed the head, so they covered his head. > **""Were it not that Safiyyah would grieve within herself, I would have left him until Allah gathers him from the bellies of beasts and birds.""**"
"4097","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded covering the faces of the dead and forbade imitating the Jews. > **""Cover the faces of your dead, and do not imitate the Jews.""**"
"4098","A sheikh from Qays reported from his father that the Prophet ﷺ came to them while they had a refractory young she-camel they could not manage, that the Prophet ﷺ drew near, wiped its udder until it filled and milked it, and that when his father died and he had bound him in his shroud and tightened the shroud with sula fibres, the Prophet ﷺ forbade tormenting his father therewith and spat upon his chest until the moisture of the spittle was seen there. > **""Do not torment your father with sula.""**"
"4099","The daughter of Uhban reported, as a narration of her own, that her father had commanded his family to shroud him without dressing him in shirts, that they dressed him in a shirt nonetheless, and by morning the shirt was found upon the drying rack."
"4100","Udaysah bint Uhban رضي الله عنها reported that when death approached her father he instructed that he not be shrouded in any sewn garment, that after his soul was taken and he was washed they sent to her for the shroud, she sent it, they asked for a shirt, she reminded them of his prohibition against a sewn shirt, sent to the fuller for a shirt of his held there, it was brought and he was dressed and taken away, she closed her door and followed, and upon returning found the shirt in the house. She then sent to those who had washed him asking whether they had shrouded him in a shirt, they affirmed, and upon showing it they affirmed it was that very one. > **""Do not shroud me in a sewn garment.""**"
"4101","Al-Zuhri reported that when death approached Sad ibn Abi Waqqas رضي الله عنه he called for a worn woollen jubba and instructed that he be shrouded in it, for in it he had met the polytheists on the day of Badr, and he had kept it aside for that very day. > **""Shroud me in it, for in it I met the polytheists on the day of Badr, and I kept it aside for this.""**"
"4102","Silah ibn Zufar reported concerning Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman رضي الله عنه that he was to be shrouded in two garments. He sent Silah and Abu Mas'ud رضي الله عنه, and they purchased for him a shroud of striped Yemeni cloth for three hundred dirhams. When they showed it to him upon his request, he declared it unsuitable for his shroud and deemed two white sheets without a shirt sufficient for him, explaining that only a short time would pass before he met with something better or worse than them, so they bought two white sheets for him. In another narration he sought refuge in Allah from wailing that leads to the Fire and asked for two garments to be bought for him."
"4103","Ali ibn Abi Talhah reported that Maymunah رضي الله عنها was shrouded in a shirt dyed with safflower."
"4104","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that a man came to announce a funeral to the people, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed the people concerning supplicating for their dead and not publicising them. > **""O people, ask Allah for your dead, and do not announce them to the people.""**"
"4105","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived in Madinah they would inform him of whoever among their dead was near death, so he ﷺ would come before death, attend him, seek forgiveness for him, and await his death. That sometimes detained him at length and weighed upon him, so they decided it would be gentler not to inform him until death had occurred. Thereafter when one died they informed him, and he ﷺ would come to his family, seek forgiveness for him and pray over him, then remain for the burial if he wished or leave if he wished. Later they decided it would be gentler to carry their dead to his house without burdening him, and they did so, and the matter continued thus."
"4106","Jabir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning perfuming the dead with incense and the number in which it should be done. > **""When you perfume the dead with incense, perfume him thrice.""**"
"4107","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning gatherings of women and their presence by the dead, and what results when they assemble. > **""There is no good in the assembly of women, nor in their presence by the dead, for when they gather they say and say.""**"
"4108","Asma bint Umays رضي الله عنها reported that a daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ died while men and women alike were carried upon open biers. She mentioned that she had been in Abyssinia among the Christian People of the Book who made for a woman a bier with ribs above it, disliking that any part of her form be described, and asked whether she should make the like for his daughter. The Prophet ﷺ permitted her to do so, and she was the first to make a covered bier in Islam, for Ruqayyah, daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""Make it.""**"
"4109","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning one who carries the four sides of the bier and what Allah effaces from him. > **""Whoever carries the four sides of the bier, Allah effaces from him forty major sins.""**"
"4110","Uthman ibn Affan رضي الله عنه is reported to have seen a funeral and stood for it, explaining that he had seen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ see a funeral and stand for it."
"4111","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reported that a man asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whether they should stand when the funeral of a disbeliever passed by them. The Prophet ﷺ affirmed it and explained that the standing is not for the bier itself but in veneration of the One who seizes souls. > **""Yes, stand for it, for you do not stand for it; you stand only in veneration of the One who seizes souls.""**"
"4112","Abu Sa'id ibn Zayd رضي الله عنه reported that a funeral passed by the Prophet ﷺ and he stood for it."
"4113","Sa'id ibn Zayd رضي الله عنه reported that a funeral passed by the Prophet ﷺ and he stood."
"4114","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that a Jewish funeral passed by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he stood. When it was said to him that it was the funeral of a Jew, he ﷺ explained it by the fright of death. > **""Indeed death has a terror.""**"
"4115","Abu Musa رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning standing when any funeral passes, whether of a Muslim, a Jew, or a Christian, and that the standing is not for the bier but for the angels accompanying it. Layth mentioned that he narrated this to Mujahid, who cited Abdullah ibn Sakhbarah al-Azdi as saying they were sitting with Ali رضي الله عنه awaiting a funeral when another passed and they stood. Ali asked what made them stand, they attributed it to what the companions of Muhammad ﷺ had brought them, and when questioned they quoted the report of Abu Musa. Ali replied that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had done so only once for a Jewish man, since he used to resemble the People of the Book, but when he was forbidden he desisted and never did so again. > **""When a funeral passes by you, whether it be of a Muslim, a Jew, or a Christian, then stand for it, for it is not for it that we stand, but we stand for the angels who are with it.""**"
"4116","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that a funeral passed by the Prophet ﷺ and he stood. When he was questioned about it, he ﷺ explained it by the fright belonging to death. > **""Indeed death has a fright.""**"
"4117","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stand for the funeral of a Jew that passed by him."
"4118","Zayd ibn Wahb reported that they discussed standing at funerals in the presence of Ali رضي الله عنه, and Abu Mas'ud رضي الله عنه said they had continued to practise it. Ali replied that he had spoken the truth, explaining it as what prevailed while they were as Jews."
"4119","Aishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood only for the funeral of a Jew that passed by him."
"4120","Al-Hasan ibn Ali رضي الله عنهما is reported to have passed with a group by a funeral where the people stood but he did not stand. When he asked what they had done, he explained that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had stood only from annoyance at the smell of the Jews."
"4121","Husayn رضي الله عنه and Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما, or one of them, reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood only on account of the funeral of a Jew that passed by him, explaining it by the annoyance its smell had caused him. > **""Its smell annoyed me.""**"
"4122","Abdullah ibn Ayyash ibn Abi Rabi'ah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood for that funeral only because it was of a Jewess, on account of the smell of its incense, remaining standing until it had passed him by."
"4123","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning women following funerals and the absence of reward therein. > **""There is no reward for women in following funerals.""**"
"4124","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported that they went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for a funeral and he saw women. He asked whether they would carry it and they said no, asked whether they would bury it and they said no, then commanded them to return laden with sin and without reward. > **""Do you carry him? Do you bury him? Then return laden with burden and without reward.""**"
"4125","Al-Mufaddal ibn Fadalah reported that he asked Rabi'ah al-Ma'afiri about al-kuda and he thought it meant the graves. When he saw doubt in Rabi'ah he met Yazid ibn Abi Habib, who reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ attended a man's funeral, and when it was placed for prayer he saw a woman and asked about her. When told she was the sister of the deceased, he ordered her to return and did not pray until she was out of sight. Yazid added that Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها had attended Abu Salamah رضي الله عنه. > **""Return.""**"
"4126","Usamah ibn Sharik رضي الله عنه reported that he was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when a funeral was brought near for him to pray over. He turned and saw a woman approaching, ordered her to be sent back, and she was turned back repeatedly until she disappeared. When he saw she had disappeared, he pronounced the takbir over it. > **""Send her back.""**"
"4127","Hanash ibn al-Mu'tamir reported from his father that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was about to pray over a funeral when a woman came with a censer intending to join the funeral. He shouted at her until she entered among the buildings of Madinah."
"4128","Abdur Rahman ibn Abza رضي الله عنه reported that he witnessed a funeral with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When he was about to pray over it he turned and saw a woman, ordered her to be driven away until he no longer saw her, then stepped forward and pronounced four takbirs over it."
"4129","Zayd ibn Arqam رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning three occasions at which Allah, Mighty and Majestic, loves silence. > **""Indeed Allah, Mighty and Majestic, loves silence at three: at the recitation of the Qur'an, at the battlefield, and at the funeral.""**"
"4130","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ witnessed a funeral, signs of grief were seen upon him and he engaged much in inward reflection."
"4131","Jabir رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he forbade the dead being followed by a wailing voice or by fire."
"4132","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning visiting the sick and walking with funerals and what the latter reminds of. > **""Visit the sick, and walk with the funeral, for it reminds you of the Hereafter.""**"
"4133","Uthman ibn Affan رضي الله عنه reported that they accompanied the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in residence and travel, and he would visit sick Muslims and attend their funerals, or he said, follow their funerals."
"4134","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning the first recompense granted to the servant after his death regarding all who followed his funeral. > **""Indeed the first with which the servant is recompensed after his death is that all who followed his funeral are forgiven.""**"
"4135","Abu Sa'id رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning the qirat for one who prays over a funeral and the two qirats for one who stays with it until burial, and the likeness of the qirat. > **""Whoever prays over a funeral has a qirat, and whoever follows it until it is buried has two qirats, and the qirat is like Uhud.""**"
"4136","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning one who follows a funeral, carries it from its upper part, casts earth into its grave, and remains seated until he is given permission, and the two qirats of reward with which he returns. > **""Whoever follows a funeral and carries it from its upper part, and casts earth into its grave, and remains seated until he is given permission, returns with two qirats of reward, each qirat like Uhud.""**"
"4137","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning one who follows a funeral until he prays over it and the qirat belonging to him. When the Prophet ﷺ was asked about the qirat, he likened it to Uhud. > **""Whoever follows a funeral until he prays over it has a qirat. The qirat is like Uhud.""**"
"4138","The companions asked whether the qirat is like their own small qirats, and the Prophet ﷺ denied it and likened it to Uhud or greater. In the narration of al-Kabir from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, whoever follows a funeral until he prays over it then returns has a qirat, and whoever prays over it then walks with it until it is buried has two qirats. When asked what the two qirats are, he likened them to Uhud. > **""No, but like Uhud, or greater than Uhud. Whoever follows a funeral until he prays over it then returns has a qirat, and whoever prays over it then walks with it until he buries it has two qirats. The two qirats are like Uhud.""**"
"4139","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning every Muslim who witnesses the funeral of a Muslim and the one qirat belonging to him, and the two qirats for one who remains seated until the earth is levelled over it, each like Uhud. In another narration, one who prays over a funeral has a qirat written for him. In the wording of al-Awsat, whoever follows a funeral and prays over it was asked what the qirat is, and he likened it to Uhud. > **""There is no Muslim who witnesses the funeral of a Muslim man except that he has a qirat of reward, and if he remains seated until it is levelled over him he has two qirats of reward, each qirat like Uhud.""**"
"4140","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning one who comes to a funeral among its family, then follows it, then prays over it, then awaits it until burial, with a qirat for each stage. > **""Whoever comes to a funeral among its family has a qirat, and if he follows it he has a qirat, and if he prays over it he has a qirat, and if he awaits it until it is buried he has a qirat.""**"
"4141","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak concerning two qirats like Uhud placed in the scale, meaning for one who followed a funeral. > **""Two qirats shall be placed in his scale, each qirat like Uhud.""**"
"4142","Abdullah ibn Yasar reported that Amr ibn Hurayth visited al-Hasan ibn Ali رضي الله عنه, and Ali رضي الله عنه asked how he could visit al-Hasan with what was in his heart. Amr replied that Ali was not his Lord to turn his heart as he willed. Ali said that would not prevent giving him counsel, and cited what he had heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning every Muslim who visits his brother and the seventy thousand angels sent to invoke blessings upon him from any hour of the day until evening or any hour of the night until morning. Amr asked him about walking with the funeral, before it or behind it. Ali explained the superiority of walking behind it over walking before it as that of the obligatory prayer in congregation over prayer alone. When Amr noted that he had seen Abu Bakr and Umar رضي الله عنهما walking in front of funerals, Ali explained that they disliked to constrain the people. > **""There is no Muslim who visits his brother except that seventy thousand angels are sent for him, invoking blessings upon him from whatever hour of the day until evening, and from whatever hour of the night until morning.""**"
"4143","Ibrahim ibn Muslim al-Hajari reported that he went out for the funeral of the son of Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa رضي الله عنه while the latter was upon a black mule. The women told its driver to put him in front of the funeral, and he did so. Ibn Abi Awfa asked where the funeral was, was told it was behind him, and after this happened once or twice he forbade being put in front of the funeral. When he heard the voice of a woman striking herself, or reciting elegy, he rebuked her and asked whether he had not forbidden them that, explaining that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to forbid them elegies, while each of them might shed of her tears as she wished."
"4144","Sahl ibn Sa'd رضي الله عنه reported that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ walking behind a funeral."
"4145","Darraj reported that he saw Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As رضي الله عنهما riding a mount in front of a funeral until he reached the graveyard, where he dismounted and sat before the funeral arrived."
"4146","Abu Hazim reported that he witnessed Husayn رضي الله عنه when al-Hasan رضي الله عنه died, pushing Sa'id ibn al-As forward by the back of his neck and telling him to step forward, for were it not the Sunnah he would not have put him forward. Sa'id was then the governor of Madinah."
"4147","Abu al-Huwayrith reported that Jabir ibn Abdullah رضي الله عنهما died and they attended his door among Banu Salamah. When his bier came out of his chamber, Hasan ibn Hasan was between its two poles, and al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf ordered him to come out from between them. He refused until they laid hands upon him, then the sons of Jabir asked him to come out, so he came out. Al-Hajjaj came and stood between the poles until it was set down and prayed over him, then came to the grave where Hasan ibn Hasan had descended into it. Al-Hajjaj ordered him to come out and he refused, then the sons of Jabir adjured him by Allah, so he came out, and al-Hajjaj plunged into the pit until he finished with him."
"4148","Abu Umamah ibn Tha'labah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ informed them of going out to Badr and he firmly intended to go out with him. His maternal uncle Abu Burdah ibn Niyar رضي الله عنه told him to remain with his mother, and Abu Umamah replied that he should rather remain with his sister. When that was mentioned to the Prophet ﷺ, he ordered Abu Umamah to remain with his mother and went out with Abu Burdah. When the Prophet ﷺ returned, she had died, so he prayed over her."
"4149","Junadah ibn Salm reported that Jabir ibn Samurah رضي الله عنه died and Amr ibn Hurayth prayed over him."
"4150","Qays ibn Abi Hazim reported that Jarir رضي الله عنه and al-Ash'ath gathered at a funeral, and al-Ash'ath put Jarir forward, so he prayed over it."
"4151","Thabit al-Bunani reported that A'idh ibn Amr left a will that Abu Barzah al-Aslami رضي الله عنه pray over him. Ubaydullah ibn Ziyad rode to pray over him, but when he reached the palace of Hisham he was told of the will for Abu Barzah, so he mounted his beast and returned."
"4152","Al-Harith ibn Wahb رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke concerning his Ummah remaining firm upon its religion so long as they do not entrust funerals to their families. > **""My Ummah shall remain upon a firm hold of its religion so long as they do not entrust funerals to their families.""**"
"4153","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه stated that no recitation nor wording was fixed for them in prayer over the dead, but to pronounce takbir as the imam pronounces takbir and to abound in fine speech."
"4154","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to raise his hands at the takbir in every prayer and over funerals."
"4155","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed over a funeral with seven men, arranging three in a row, two in a row, and two in a row."
"4156","Asma bint Yazid رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded reciting the Opening of the Book when praying over the funeral. > **""When you pray over the funeral, recite the Opening of the Book.""**"
"4157","Umm Afif رضي الله عنها reported that they pledged the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when the women pledged. He took from them that they would not speak to a man except a mahram, and he commanded them to recite the Opening of the Book over their dead."
"4158","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ recited over the funeral four times: Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds."
"4159","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the funeral of Jabir ibn Atik, or he said Sahl ibn Atik, was brought, the first for whom prayer was held at the place of funerals. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stepped forward, pronounced takbir, recited the Mother of the Qur'an aloud, then pronounced the second takbir, then the third and supplicated for the deceased, then pronounced the fourth and supplicated for the believing men and believing women, then gave salam. > **""O Allah, forgive him, have mercy upon him, and raise his rank.""**"
"4160","Abu Qatadah رضي الله عنه reported that he witnessed the Prophet ﷺ over a dead person and heard him say a supplication for the living and the dead, the present and the absent, the young and the old, the male and the female. Abu Salamah added a supplication for keeping alive upon Islam whom Allah keeps alive and taking in faith whom Allah takes. > **""O Allah, forgive our living and our dead, our present and our absent, our young and our old, our male and our female. Whomsoever Thou keepest alive among us, keep him alive upon Islam, and whomsoever Thou takest among us, take him upon faith.""**"
"4161","Abu Salamah ibn Abdur Rahman ibn Awf reported from his father from the Prophet ﷺ that he used to say in prayer over the dead a supplication for the living and the dead, the present and the absent, the male and the female, the young and the old, for life upon Islam and death upon faith. > **""O Allah, forgive our living and our dead, our present and our absent, our male and our female, our young and our old. Whomsoever Thou keepest alive among us, keep him alive upon Islam, and whomsoever Thou takest among us, take him upon faith.""**"
"4162","Aishah رضي الله عنها reported that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say in prayer over the dead a supplication for forgiveness, blessing, and bringing to the basin of His Messenger, with an addition of blessing therein. > **""O Allah, forgive him, bless him, and bring him to the basin of Thy Messenger, and bless him therein.""**"
"4163","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that when he prayed over a funeral he would say a supplication describing the deceased as His servant testifying to divine unity and the messengership of Muhammad ﷺ, asking increase for him if righteous and forgiveness if erring, with preservation of reward and protection from trial after him. > **""O Allah, Thy servant and son of Thy servant bore witness that there is no god but Thou and that Muhammad is Thy servant and Thy Messenger, and Thou knowest him best. If he was righteous, increase in his righteousness, and if he was erring, forgive him. Deprive us not of his reward, and try us not after him.""**"
"4164","Abu as-Siddiq an-Naji reported that they asked Abu Sa'id رضي الله عنه about prayer over the funeral, and he said they used to say a supplication addressing Allah as their Lord and his, who created him, sustained him, gave him life, and sufficed him, asking forgiveness for themselves and him, with preservation of reward and protection from straying after him."
"4165","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ, when he prayed over the dead, would say a supplication for the living, the dead, the present, the absent, the female and the male, for life upon Islam and death upon faith, repeating the request for pardon. > **""O Allah, forgive our living, our dead, our present, our absent, our female and our male. Whomsoever Thou keepest alive among us, keep him alive upon Islam, and whomsoever Thou takest among us, take him upon faith. O Allah, Thy pardon, Thy pardon.""**"
"4166","Al-Harith رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ taught them prayer over the dead with a supplication for the living and the dead, for rectifying relations and joining hearts, naming the servant as son of so-and-so with testimony to known good and Allah's greater knowledge, asking forgiveness for themselves and him. When the narrator, though the youngest of the people, asked what if he did not know good, he was told to say only what he knew. > **""O Allah, forgive our living and our dead, rectify our relations, and join between our hearts. O Allah, this is Thy servant, son of so-and-so, we know naught but good, and Thou knowest him best, so forgive us and him. Say naught but what you know.""**"
"4167","Yazid ibn Rukanah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ, when he prayed over the dead, would pronounce four takbirs and then say a supplication describing him as His servant and son of His bondwoman in need of His mercy while Allah is free of need for his punishment, asking increase if righteous and pardon if erring, then supplicating further as Allah willed. > **""O Allah, Thy servant and son of Thy bondwoman stands in need of Thy mercy, and Thou art free of need for his punishment. If he was righteous, increase in his righteousness, and if he was erring, pardon him.""**"
"4168","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported that a son of Abu Talhah رضي الله عنه died and the Prophet ﷺ prayed over him, with Umm Sulaym رضي الله عنها behind Abu Talhah, as though they were the crest of a rooster."
"4169","Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah reported that Abu Talhah رضي الله عنه invited the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for Umayr ibn Abi Talhah when he died. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to them and prayed over him in their house, with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in front, Abu Talhah behind him, and Umm Sulaym رضي الله عنها behind Abu Talhah, with none besides them."
"4170","Abu Musa رضي الله عنه reported that they prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ over a funeral, and he gave salam to his right and to his left."
"4171","Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه reported that there were traits the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to practise which the people abandoned, among them the greeting of the imam in the funeral like his greeting in prayer."
"4172","Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه is reported to have waited for Umm Abdullah until she prayed over Utbah."
"4173","Yahya ibn Abdullah al-Jabir reported that he prayed behind Isa, freedman of Hudhayfah, at al-Mada'in over a funeral, and he pronounced five takbirs. He then turned to them and said he had neither erred nor forgotten, but had pronounced takbir as his master and benefactor Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman رضي الله عنه had done. Hudhayfah had prayed over a funeral with five takbirs, then turned and said he had neither forgotten nor erred, but had pronounced takbir as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had done when he prayed over a funeral with five takbirs."
"4174","Abdullah ibn Ma'qil reported that Ali رضي الله عنه prayed over Sahl ibn Hunayf رضي الله عنه with six takbirs, then turned to them and said he was a Badri."
"4175","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه stated that there is no fixed time nor number in prayer over the funeral, meaning the takbir."
"4176","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pronounced seven, five, and four takbirs, so they should pronounce as the imam pronounces when they put him forward."
"4177","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed over the slain of Uhud, pronouncing nine takbirs at a time, then seven at a time, then four at a time, until he joined Allah."
"4178","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ used to pronounce seven takbirs over the people of Badr and five takbirs over Banu Hashim, then his last funeral prayers were with four takbirs until he left the world."
"4179","Jabir رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ commanded excellence in shrouding the brother and equality of the funeral prayer by night and by day with four takbirs. > **""When one of you shrouds his brother, let him make his shroud excellent, and pray over the dead by night and by day with four takbirs, alike by night and by day.""**"
"4180","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ prayed over his son Ibrahim رضي الله عنه and pronounced four takbirs over him."
"4181","Abu Sa'id رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ prayed over his son Ibrahim رضي الله عنه and pronounced four takbirs over him."
"4182","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said that the last funeral prayer the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed over, he pronounced four takbirs over it."
"4183","Ubayy ibn Ka'b رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that the angels washed Adam عليه السلام and pronounced four takbirs over him, declaring that practice to be the way of his descendants. > **""This is your sunnah, O children of Adam.""**"
"4184","Amir ibn Rabi'ah رضي الله عنه said that he saw the Prophet ﷺ pray over Uthman ibn Maz'un رضي الله عنه and pronounce four takbirs over him, stand over his grave, and throw three handfuls of earth into it."
"4185","Imran ibn Abi Ata said that he was present with Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyyah when Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما died at Taif, and Muhammad took charge of him, pronounced four takbirs over him, lowered him into the grave from the side of the qiblah until he placed him in the grave, and kept a tent pitched over his grave for three days."
"4186","Aishah رضي الله عنها said that she saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray over a funeral while the sun was seen only upon the edges of the walls."
"4187","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ forbade praying over funerals between graves."
"4188","Al-Sha'bi said that Ali رضي الله عنه prayed on the day of Siffin over Ammar ibn Yasir رضي الله عنه and Hashim ibn Utbah رضي الله عنه, with Ammar nearer to Ali and Hashim nearer to the qiblah."
"4189","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that no man is prayed over by one hundred except that Allah forgives him. > **""There is no man over whom one hundred pray except that Allah forgives him.""**"
"4190","Abu al-Malih al-Hudhali said that he went out with a funeral, and when the bier was set down he turned to the people and bade them straighten their rows and make their intercession well. He then related from Sulayk, the brother of Maymunah, Mother of the Believers رضي الله عنها, from Maymunah, that the Prophet ﷺ explained the numbers by which intercession is granted for the brother. > **""Whoever is prayed over by one hundred, they are granted intercession for their brother; and the community is from forty to one hundred, and the clan is from ten to forty, and the company is from three to ten.""**"
"4191","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that a black man who used to clean the mosque died and was buried at night. When the Prophet ﷺ was informed, he asked to be taken to his grave, described how his prayer illumines the darkness of graves, prayed over the grave, and then went with an Ansari to pray over his brother's grave as well. > **""Set out for his grave. Indeed these graves are filled with darkness over their people, and Allah Almighty illumines them through my prayer over them. Where is his grave?""**"
"4192","Abu Qatadah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ prayed over a grave after the dead had been buried."
"4193","Sahl ibn Hunayf رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to visit the poor of Madinah and attend their funerals. When a woman from al-Awali died, he asked to be informed when she was ready, but the people found him asleep late in the night and disliked waking him while fearing the darkness and vermin, so they buried her. In the morning he walked to her grave and prayed over her with four takbirs. > **""When she is ready, inform me.""**"
"4194","Husayn ibn Wahwah reported that Talhah ibn al-Bara رضي الله عنه met the Prophet ﷺ as a youth and offered absolute obedience, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ tested him with a command against his father, then recalled him and disavowed severing kinship. Talhah later fell ill, the Prophet ﷺ visited him in winter cold and cloud, and bade his family inform him and hasten so he might attend and pray over him. Talhah died at night before the Prophet ﷺ reached Banu Salim ibn Awf, enjoining burial without summoning the Messenger ﷺ for fear of the Jews. In the morning the Prophet ﷺ came, stood over his grave with the people in rows, raised his hands, and supplicated for him. > **""Go and kill your father. Come forward, for I was not sent to sever kinship. I see nothing in Talhah but that death has come upon him, so inform me of him that I may attend him and pray over him, and hasten. O Allah, meet Talhah while he laughs to You and You laugh to him.""**"
"4195","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ prayed over al-Najashi."
"4196","Sa'id ibn Zayd رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ prayed over al-Najashi."
"4197","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه said that Jibril عليه السلام descended upon the Prophet ﷺ, announced the death of Mu'awiyah ibn Mu'awiyah al-Laythi, and asked whether he wished to pray over him. When he assented, Jibril struck the earth with his wing so that every tree and hill was levelled, the bier was raised into view, and he pronounced the takbir with two rows of angels behind him, seventy thousand in each row. When asked by what means that rank was attained, Jibril attributed it to love of Surat al-Ikhlas and its constant recitation in every state. > **""Mu'awiyah ibn Mu'awiyah al-Laythi has died; would you like to pray over him? Yes. O Jibril, by what did he attain this station with Allah? Through his love of Qul Huwa Allahu Ahad and his recitation of it going and coming, standing and sitting, and in every state.""**"
"4198","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه said that Jibril عليه السلام came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was at Tabuk and bade him witness the funeral of Mu'awiyah ibn Mu'awiyah al-Muzani. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out with Jibril and seventy thousand angels; Jibril placed his right wing upon the mountains so they sank and his left wing upon the lands so they sank, until Makkah and Madinah were seen. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Jibril, and the angels prayed over him, and when they finished he asked by what means that station had been reached. > **""O Muhammad, attend the funeral of Mu'awiyah ibn Mu'awiyah al-Muzani. O Jibril, by what did Mu'awiyah ibn Mu'awiyah al-Muzani reach this station? Through reciting Qul Huwa Allahu Ahad, standing and sitting, riding and walking.""**"
"4199","Mu'awiyah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was campaigning at Tabuk when Jibril عليه السلام came and asked whether he desired the funeral of Mu'awiyah ibn Mu'awiyah al-Muzani. When he assented, Jibril gestured with his hand so the mountains and hills parted, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ walked with Jibril and seventy thousand angels and prayed over Mu'awiyah. When asked by what means he had attained that rank, Jibril attributed it to abundant recitation of Surat al-Ikhlas by night and day. > **""O Muhammad, do you desire the funeral of Mu'awiyah ibn Mu'awiyah al-Muzani? Yes. By what did Mu'awiyah attain this? Through much recitation of Qul Huwa Allahu Ahad; he used to recite it standing and sitting and lying, and thereby he attained what he attained.""**"
"4200","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ prayed over al-Najashi and pronounced four takbirs over him."
"4201","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ prayed over al-Najashi when his death was announced, and when it was objected that he would pray over an Abyssinian slave, Allah Almighty sent down the verse concerning those among the People of the Book who believe in Allah. > **""And indeed, among the People of the Book are those who believe in Allah.""**"
"4202","Kathir ibn Abdillah reported from his father from his grandfather that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed over al-Najashi and pronounced five takbirs over him."
"4203","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه said that when news of al-Najashi's death reached the Prophet ﷺ, he commanded the companions to go out and pray over an unseen brother. They went out, the Prophet ﷺ advanced with them ranged behind him, and they prayed. When the hypocrites marvelled that he prayed over a Christian chief he had never seen, Allah sent down the verse concerning those among the People of the Book who believe in Allah, to the end of the verse. > **""Go out and pray over a brother of yours whom you have never seen.""**"
"4204","Jarir رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ announced the death of al-Najashi and commanded prayer over him. > **""Indeed al-Najashi has died, so pray over him.""**"
"4205","Ibn Kharijah said that when news of al-Najashi's death reached the Prophet ﷺ, he announced the death of their brother, they went out and formed rows behind him, and they prayed though they saw nothing before them. > **""Indeed your brother has died.""**"
"4206","Wahshi ibn Harb رضي الله عنه said that when al-Najashi died, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ bade his companions rise and pray over their brother al-Najashi. When a man asked how they could pray over one who died in disbelief, he directed them to Allah's word concerning those among the People of the Book who believe in Allah and in what was sent down, to the end of the verse. > **""Indeed your brother al-Najashi has died, so rise and pray over him. Have you not heard the word of Allah: And indeed, among the People of the Book are those who believe in Allah and in what was sent down to you and in what was sent down to them.""**"
"4207","Hudhayfah ibn Asid رضي الله عنه reported that when news of al-Najashi's death reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he gave leave to whoever wished to pray over him, then turned toward Abyssinia and pronounced four takbirs over him. > **""Indeed your brother al-Najashi has died, so whoever wishes to pray over him, let him pray over him.""**"
"4208","Jabir رضي الله عنه said that a man died and they washed him, shrouded him, perfumed him, then brought him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to pray over him. When they asked him to pray, he stepped forward, then asked about debt upon him. Upon learning of two dinars, he withdrew until Abu Qatadah assumed them, then he prayed over him, and later asked about their payment until he was told they had been settled. > **""Is there debt upon him? Allah has fulfilled the right of the creditor, and the dead is free of it. What became of the two dinars? Now his skin has cooled upon him.""**"
"4209","Isa ibn Sadaqah ibn Abbad al-Yashkuri said that he entered with his father upon Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه and asked for a beneficial narration. Anas bade whoever was able to die free of debt to do so, for he had seen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ refuse the funeral of a debtor until his debt was guaranteed, explaining that his prayer would benefit him, and describing the unguaranteed debtor as held hostage in his grave. > **""I shall not pray over him until you guarantee his debt, for my prayer over him will benefit him. He is held hostage in his grave.""**"
"4210","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ was brought a funeral to pray over, asked whether debt lay upon the dead, and upon being told yes, explained that Jibril had forbidden him to pray over a debtor and described the debtor as held hostage in his grave until his debt is settled on his behalf. > **""Is there debt upon him? Indeed Jibril forbade me to pray over one upon whom is debt. The holder of debt is held hostage in his grave until his debt is settled on his behalf.""**"
"4211","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ was brought a funeral and rose to pray over it, but when told of debt upon the dead, he bade the people take their companion and pray over him. When a man assumed the debt and asked him to pray, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rose and prayed over him. > **""Take your companion and pray over him.""**"
"4212","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه said that they were with the Prophet ﷺ when a man was brought for prayer. He asked whether debt lay upon their companion, and upon being told yes, he explained that his prayer would be of no benefit to one whose soul is held hostage in his grave and does not ascend to heaven, unless a man guaranteed the debt, in which case his prayer would benefit him. > **""Is there debt upon your companion? What benefit is it to you that I pray over a man whose soul is held hostage in his grave and whose soul does not ascend to heaven? Were a man to guarantee his debt, I would stand and pray over him, for my prayer would benefit him.""**"
"4213","Abu Qatadah رضي الله عنه said that a funeral was brought, the Prophet ﷺ asked whether debt lay upon their companion, and upon being told yes, he bade them pray over their companion. When a man assumed it upon himself, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed over him. > **""Is there debt upon your companion? Pray over your companion.""**"
"4214","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما said that a dead man was carried past the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he was called to pray over him. He asked about debt upon their companion, and upon learning of two dinars, he bade them pray over their companion until a kinsman assumed the debt with confirmation that the dead was thereby freed. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then prayed over him, afterwards pressed the guarantor about payment, bade him give coolness to his companion and hasten settlement, and upon being told it was settled, confirmed that coolness had been given. > **""Is there debt upon your companion? Pray over your companion. Is it upon you, and is he free of it? What have you done? Give coolness to your companion. Now you have given coolness to your companion.""**"
"4215","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه said that a man died in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ leaving two dinars of debt with no means of settlement. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ refused to pray over him and bade the people pray over their companion, until Abu Qatadah رضي الله عنه rose and undertook its payment, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rose and prayed over him. > **""Pray over your companion.""**"
"4216","Asma bint Yazid رضي الله عنها said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was called to the funeral of a man of the Ansar. When the bier was set down and the Prophet of Allah ﷺ advanced to pray over him, he turned and asked about debt upon their companion. Upon learning of two dinars, he bade them pray over their companion until Abu Qatadah رضي الله عنه took the debt upon himself for the Prophet ﷺ, whereupon he prayed over him. > **""Is there debt upon your companion? Pray over your companion.""**"
"4217","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه said that a man died in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with no shroud found for him. When the Prophet ﷺ was approached, he bade them look inside his waist-wrapper, where a dinar or two dinars were found. He described them as two brands and bade them pray over their companion, until a man undertook their payment, whereupon he prayed over him. > **""Look inside his waist-wrapper. Two brands. Pray over your companion.""**"
"4218","Thawban, freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, رضي الله عنه said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said on a journey that they would travel by night and that no rider of a refractory or weak mount should travel by night. A man travelled by night upon a refractory she-camel of his, fell, broke his thigh, and died. The Prophet ﷺ commanded prayer over him, then commanded a caller to announce thrice among the people that Paradise is not lawful for the disobedient. > **""We are travelling by night, so let no rider of a refractory or weak mount travel by night. Indeed Paradise is not lawful for the disobedient.""**"
"4219","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه said that they went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on an expedition he undertook, and he commanded the caller to announce that whoever was with a weak mount should return. The people withdrew until they reached a narrow place in the road, where a man's she-camel threw him and killed him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw it, summoned the Muslims, asked what had kept them, and upon being told and asked to pray over the man, he declined and commanded a caller to announce the exclusion of the disobedient from Paradise and the prohibition of domestic donkeys and every fanged or clawed predator. > **""Whoever is with a weak mount among us, let him return. What is your affair, or what has detained you? Indeed Paradise is not lawful for the disobedient. Indeed domestic donkeys are forbidden, and every predator with fang or claw.""**"
"4220","Imran ibn Husayn رضي الله عنه reported that a man freed six slaves of his at his death, and his heirs from the Bedouins came and informed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ of what he had done. He asked in astonishment whether he had done that, and said that had they informed him, he would not have prayed over him. > **""Did he do that? Had you informed us, we would not have prayed over him.""**"
"4221","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed over an adulteress who died in childbed together with her child."
"4222","Ata ibn al-Sa'ib reported from his father from his grandfather that the Prophet ﷺ passed by a well in which lay a dead black man. He looked into the well, asked why he had been cast there, and upon being told that he was harsh in religion, praying at times and abandoning prayer at times, he ordered his removal, washing, shrouding, carrying, and prayer, describing the hastening of the angels toward him. > **""Why is he cast in the well? Woe to you, take him out. Carry him. The angels had nearly outrun us to him.""**"
"4223","Anas رضي الله عنه said that a young servant boy of the Prophet ﷺ fell ill, and the Prophet ﷺ visited him, invited him to bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that he is the Messenger of Allah, and upon the boy's father bidding him assent to what Muhammad ﷺ said, the boy accepted and then died. The Prophet ﷺ bade his companions pray over their brother. > **""Do you bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah? Pray over your brother.""**"
"4224","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reported that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ intended to pray over Abdullah ibn Ubayy, Jibril seized his garment and forbade prayer over any of them or standing over his grave. > **""Do not pray over any of them, nor stand over his grave.""**"
"4225","Hudhayfah رضي الله عنه said that Umar رضي الله عنه was called to a funeral and went out to attend it, so he clung to him and bade him sit, for the dead was of those hypocrites. Umar adjured him by Allah whether he himself was of them, and he answered no, adding that he would clear no one after him. > **""Sit, O Commander of the Faithful, for he is of those. I adjure you by Allah, am I of them? No, nor shall I clear anyone after you.""**"
"4226","Abu Sa'id رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ passed in Madinah by a party digging a grave, asked about it, and upon being told that an Abyssinian who had arrived had died, he declared the divine unity and said that he had been driven from his land and sky to the soil from which he was created. > **""La ilaha illa Allah. He was driven from his land and his sky to the soil from which he was created.""**"
"4227","Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنه said that the Prophet ﷺ passed by them while they were digging a grave and asked what they were doing. When they said they were digging a grave for a black man, he said that his appointed death had brought him to his soil. Abu Usamah added that he narrated this to the people of Kufah because Abu Bakr and Umar رضي الله عنهما were created from the soil of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""What are you doing? His death brought him to his soil.""**"
"4228","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that an Abyssinian was buried in Madinah, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that he had been buried in the clay from which he was created. > **""He was buried in the clay from which he was created.""**"
"4229","Aishah رضي الله عنها and Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that a lahd was made for the Prophet ﷺ."
"4230","Buraydah رضي الله عنه said that a lahd was made for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, unbaked bricks were set upright over him, and he was taken into the grave from the side of the qiblah."
"4231","Ubayy ibn Ka'b رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that when Adam عليه السلام died, the angels washed him with water an odd number of times, made a lahd for him, and declared it the sunnah of Adam and his children. > **""When Adam died, the angels washed him with water an odd number of times, a lahd was made for him, and they said: This is the sunnah of Adam and his children.""**"
"4232","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that when Ruqayyah رحمها الله died, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade any man who had parted from his wife to enter the grave, so Uthman رضي الله عنه did not enter the grave. > **""No man who has parted from his wife shall enter the grave.""**"
"4233","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said that the Prophet ﷺ, Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه, and Umar رضي الله عنه used to bring the dead into the grave from the side of the qiblah."
"4234","Safwan ibn Amr al-Saksaki said that they went out with a funeral whose people were bringing the dead into the grave from the side of the qiblah, whereupon Karb al-Yahsabi cited Nu'man ibn Bashir رضي الله عنه that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that every house has a door and the door of the grave lies toward its feet. > **""Indeed every house has a door, and the door of the grave is from the direction of its feet.""**"
"4235","Muhammad said that he was with Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه at a funeral, and Anas commanded concerning the dead, so he was slipped into the grave from the side of the feet of the grave."
"4236","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه said that it is of the sunnah to begin with the burial of the dead and for earth to be cast from the side of the qiblah."
"4237","Kathir ibn Abdillah reported from his father from his grandfather that Abdullah Dhu al-Bijadayn رضي الله عنه perished during the expedition of Tabuk in the depth of the night. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ descended into his grave pit, bade Abu Bakr and Umar رضي الله عنهما lower their brother to him, and when he placed him in his lahd, he declared his pleasure with him and asked for Allah's pleasure. Abu Bakr then wished that he had been the occupant of that pit. > **""Lower your brother to me. O Allah, I am pleased with him, so be pleased with him.""**"
"4238","Abu Sa'id رضي الله عنه said that on the day of Uhud the caller of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ announced that the slain should be returned to their resting places. > **""Return the slain to their resting places.""**"
"4239","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه said that when Umm Kulthum, daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, رضي الله عنها was placed in the grave, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited the verse of Surah Ta-Ha, pronounced the burial formula with uncertainty in one wording, and when her lahd was built, he handed pebbles and bade the gaps between bricks be filled, explaining that though it avails nothing, it comforts the living. > **""From it We created you, and into it We shall return you, and from it We shall bring you forth another time. In the name of Allah, and in the path of Allah, and upon the religion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Fill the gaps between the bricks. Indeed this is nothing, but it comforts the soul of the living.""**"
"4240","Ibn Sirin said that Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه attended the funeral of a man of the Ansar, the people openly sought forgiveness for him without Anas objecting, and they brought him into the grave from the side of the feet of the grave."
"4241","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه said that he asked Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه whether walking behind the funeral or before it is more excellent. When he cited the practice of Abu Bakr and Umar رضي الله عنهما walking before it, Ali invoked mercy and forgiveness upon them, explained that they had heard as they had heard but preferred ease, and instructed him to walk behind his Muslim brother in silence and reflection upon his own likeness to him, to remain standing at the grave when others sit, to pronounce the burial formula with supplication for the servant who has left the world behind, and to throw three handfuls of earth. > **""In the name of Allah, and in the path of Allah, and upon the religion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. O Allah, Your servant has come to You, and You are the best with whom to stay. He has left the world behind his back, so make what he goes to better than what he left behind, for You said: And what is with Allah is better for the righteous.""**"
"4242","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما said that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ command hastening the dead to his grave without detention and reciting the Opening of the Book at his head and the seal of Surat al-Baqarah at his feet in his grave. > **""When one of you dies, do not detain him, hasten him to his grave, and let the Opening of the Book be recited at his head and the seal of Surat al-Baqarah at his feet in his grave.""**"
"4243","Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Ala ibn al-Lajlaj said that his father bade him, when he died, to make a lahd for him, to pronounce the burial formula when placing him in his lahd, to pour earth over him, and to recite at his head the opening and the seal of al-Baqarah, for he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that. > **""O my son, when I die, make a lahd for me. When you place me in my lahd, say: In the name of Allah and upon the religion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Then pour earth over me, then recite at my head the opening of al-Baqarah and its seal.""**"
"4244","Wathilah رضي الله عنه said that when the dead was placed in his grave, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pronounced the burial formula upon the sunnah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, placed a clod behind his nape, a clod between his shoulders, a clod between his knees, and another behind him. > **""In the name of Allah, and upon the sunnah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.""**"
"4245","Al-Hakam ibn Harith al-Sulami رضي الله عنه, who campaigned with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in three expeditions, instructed that when they buried him they should sprinkle water over his grave, stand over his grave, face the qiblah, and supplicate for him. > **""When you bury me and sprinkle water over my grave, stand over my grave, face the qiblah, and supplicate for me.""**"
"4246","Qatadah said that Anas رضي الله عنه buried a son of his and supplicated that Allah keep the earth from his sides, open the doors of heaven for his soul, and replace his abode with one better than it. > **""O Allah, keep the earth from his sides, open the doors of heaven for his soul, and replace him with an abode better than his abode.""**"
"4247","Anas رضي الله عنه had with him a small staff of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and when he died it was buried with him between his breast and his shirt."
"4248","Sa'id ibn Abdillah al-Awdi said that he attended Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه in his death throes, and he bade them do with him as the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had commanded them to do with their dead. He related that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded that when a brother dies and the earth is levelled over his grave, one of them should stand at the head of his grave and call him thrice by his mother's name, for he hears without answering, then sits upright, then asks for guidance without their perceiving, whereupon he should be reminded of the testimony with which he left the world, his pleasure with Allah as Lord, Islam as religion, Muhammad ﷺ as Prophet, and the Qur'an as guide, so that Munkar and Nakir withdraw from one thus taught his proof, with Allah as his disputant besides them. When asked about one whose mother's name is unknown, he bade him be ascribed to Hawwa. > **""When one of your brothers dies and you level the earth over his grave, let one of you stand at the head of his grave, then say: O so-and-so, son of so-and-so. Remember what you left the world upon: testimony that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger, and that you were pleased with Allah as Lord, with Islam as religion, with Muhammad as Prophet, and with the Qur'an as guide. O so-and-so, son of Hawwa.""**"
"4249","Amir ibn Rabi'ah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ stood over the grave of Uthman ibn Maz'un رضي الله عنه and commanded that water be sprinkled over it."
"4250","Aishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ sprinkled water over the grave of his son Ibrahim رضي الله عنه."
"4251","Abu al-Hajjaj al-Thumali رضي الله عنه said that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the grave addresses the dead when he is placed in it, reproaching the son of Adam for his delusion though knowing it the house of trial and darkness and for his strutting past it. If he was righteous, the responder of the grave answers on his behalf by citing his enjoining of good and forbidding of evil, whereupon the grave turns green for him, his body becomes light, and his soul ascends to the Lord of the worlds. When Ibn A'idh asked about the strutter, he described one who steps forward with one foot and delays the other, like his nephew's gait at times, while dressed and prepared that day. > **""Woe to you, O son of Adam, what deluded you of me? Did you not know that I am the house of trial and the house of darkness? What deluded you when you used to pass by me strutting? If he used to enjoin good and forbid evil, then I turn green for him, his body becomes light, and his soul ascends to the Lord of the worlds.""**"
"4252","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that they went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for a funeral, and he ﷺ sat beside one of the graves and described how no day passes over the grave except that it calls out, reminded the son of Adam of its solitude, estrangement, wildness, worms and constriction, and then described the grave as either a garden or a pit. > **""O son of Adam, how have you forgotten me? Did you not know that I am the house of solitude, and the house of estrangement, and the house of wildness, and the house of worms, and the house of constriction, except for one for whom Allah makes me spacious? The grave is either a garden from among the gardens of Paradise, or a pit from among the pits of the Fire.""**"
"4253","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه reports that they were with the Prophet ﷺ at a funeral, and when they reached the grave he ﷺ sat at its side and kept gazing into it, then described the compression of the believer from which his shoulder-straps are displaced and the filling of fire upon the disbeliever. > **""The believer is compressed therein with a compression from which his ḥamā'il are removed, and it is filled with fire upon the disbeliever.""**"
"4254","Jābir ibn 'Abd Allāh رضي الله عنهما reports that when Sa'd ibn Mu'ādh رضي الله عنه was buried while they were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he ﷺ glorified Allah and the people glorified long with him, then he ﷺ magnified Allah and the people magnified, and when they asked why he ﷺ had glorified, he ﷺ explained the constriction of the grave upon that righteous man and Allah's relief of him. > **""This righteous man was constricted in his grave until Allah, Mighty and Majestic, relieved him.""**"
"4255","'Ā'ishah رضي الله عنها reports from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the pressure of the grave and that had anyone been saved from it, Sa'd ibn Mu'ādh رضي الله عنه would have been saved. > **""Indeed the grave has a pressure; had anyone been saved from it, Sa'd ibn Mu'ādh would have been saved from it.""**"
"4256","'Ā'ishah, Mother of the Believers رضي الله عنها, reports that a Jewish woman came to her and spoke of the punishment of the grave, and when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came she informed him of her words and he ﷺ gave her no reply at first, then afterwards commanded her to seek refuge and mentioned Sa'd ibn Mu'ādh رضي الله عنه and the embrace. > **""O 'Ā'ishah, seek refuge with Allah from the punishment of the grave, for had anyone been saved, Sa'd ibn Mu'ādh would have been saved from it, but it did no more than embrace him.""**"
"4257","Ibn 'Abbās رضي الله عنهما reports that on the day Sa'd ibn Mu'ādh رضي الله عنه was buried, the Prophet ﷺ was sitting at his grave and declared that had anyone been saved from the trial or questioning of the grave, Sa'd would have been saved, yet he was embraced and then released. > **""Had anyone been saved from the trial of the grave, or the questioning of the grave, Sa'd ibn Mu'ādh would have been saved; he was embraced with an embrace, then he was released from it.""**"
"4258","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that when Zaynab, daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, passed away, they went out with him ﷺ and saw him ﷺ deeply anxious and grieved so they did not speak to him until they reached the grave whose laḥd was not yet finished, and he ﷺ sat with them around him, reflected for a while and gazed at the sky, then descended into the grave with increasing grief, and when he ﷺ came out his distress was lifted and he ﷺ smiled, and when they asked about the change he ﷺ mentioned the straitness and anguish of the grave, the weakness of Zaynab, his supplication for lightening, and the embrace heard by all except jinn and men. > **""I was remembering the straitness of the grave, and its anguish, and the weakness of Zaynab, and that weighed upon me, so I supplicated Allah, Mighty and Majestic, to lighten it for her, and He did. Yet it compressed her with a compression heard by all between the two horizons except jinn and men.""**"
"4259","Abū Ayyūb رضي الله عنه reports that when a child was buried, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that had anyone escaped the embrace of the grave, that child would have escaped. > **""Had anyone escaped the embrace of the grave, this child would have escaped.""**"
"4260","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ prayed over a boy, or a girl, and declared that had anyone been saved from the embrace of the grave, that child would have been saved. > **""Had anyone been saved from the embrace of the grave, this child would have been saved.""**"
"4261","Nāfi' reports that they came to Ṣafiyyah bint Abī 'Ubayd and she narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ expressed his view that had anyone been exempted from the pressure of the grave, Sa'd ibn Mu'ādh رضي الله عنه would have been exempted, yet he was embraced. > **""I indeed hold that had anyone been exempted from the pressure of the grave, Sa'd ibn Mu'ādh would have been exempted, yet he was embraced with an embrace.""**"
"4262","'Abd Allāh ibn 'Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned the trial of the grave, whereupon 'Umar رضي الله عنه asked whether their minds would be restored to them, and the Messenger ﷺ confirmed it would be as their state today, whereupon 'Umar رضي الله عنه answered with his confident retort. > **""Yes, as your state today. Then stone in his mouth.""**"
"4263","Abū Sa'īd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reports that he witnessed a funeral with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he ﷺ addressed the people concerning the trial of this community in their graves, describing the coming of an angel with a hammer who seats the dead and questions him about the man, the answer of the believer and his two openings, the answer of the disbeliever or hypocrite and his beating heard by all creation except the two heavy ones, and when some people expressed fear of the standing angel, he ﷺ recited the verse of the firm word. > **""O people, indeed this community will be tried in their graves. When a man is buried and his companions disperse from him, an angel comes to him with a hammer in his hand and seats him and says: What do you say concerning this man? If he was a believer he says: I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muḥammad is His servant and His Messenger. So he says: You have spoken truly. Then a door to the Fire is opened for him and he says: This would have been your abode had you disbelieved in your Lord, but since you believed in your Lord, this is your abode, and a door to Paradise is opened for him, and he wishes to rise toward it, so he says to him: Remain still. And his grave is widened for him. And if he was a disbeliever or a hypocrite, he says to him: What do you say concerning this man? So he says: I do not know; I heard the people saying something. So he says: You neither knew, nor recited, nor were guided. Then a door to Paradise is opened for him and he says: This would have been your abode had you believed in your Lord, but since you disbelieved in your Lord, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has exchanged this for you. And a door to the Fire is opened for him, then he strikes him a blow with the hammer heard by all Allah's creation except the two heavy ones. Allah keeps firm those who believe with the firm word.""**"
"4264","Jābir رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describe the trial of this community in their graves, the coming of a stern angel to the believer and the disbeliever, their answers concerning the man, the exchange of seats in Fire and Paradise seen together, the believer's wish to bear glad tidings to his family and the command to remain still, and that every servant is raised in the grave upon the state in which he died. > **""Indeed this community will be tried in their graves. When the believer is admitted into his grave and his companions turn away from him, a stern angel comes to him and says to him: What did you say concerning this man? So the believer says: I say he is the Messenger of Allah and His servant. So the angel says to him: Look at your seat which was for you in the Fire; Allah has saved you from it, and He has exchanged your seat which you see in the Fire for your seat which you see in Paradise. So he sees them both, and the believer says: Leave me to bear glad tidings to my family. So it is said to him: Remain still. As for the hypocrite, he sits when his family have turned away from him, and it is said to him: What did you say concerning this man? So he says: I do not know; I said what the people said. So it is said: You did not know. This is your seat which was for you in Paradise; Allah has exchanged your seat in the Fire for it. Every servant is raised in the grave upon what he died upon; the believer upon his faith and the hypocrite upon his hypocrisy.""**"
"4265","'Ā'ishah رضي الله عنها reports that a Jewish woman came begging at her door and invoked protection from the trial of the Dajjāl and the trial of the punishment of the grave, and she detained her until the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came and asked what she said, whereupon he ﷺ stood and raised his hands outstretched seeking refuge, then explained the trial of the Dajjāl and the trial of the grave, the questioning of the righteous man without fear and of the evil man in terror, and their two openings. > **""As for the trial of the Dajjāl, there was no prophet except that he warned his community of him, and I shall tell you of him what no prophet told his community: he is one-eyed, and Allah is not one-eyed; written between his eyes is kāfir, which every believer will read. As for the trial of the grave, by me you will be tried, and about me you will be asked. When the righteous man is seated in his grave, neither terrified nor bewildered, it is said: In what were you? So he says: In Islam. So it is said: Who is this man who was among you? So he says: Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah; he came to us with clear proofs and guidance from Allah, so we believed him. So an opening is made for him toward the Fire, and he looks at it crushing one part another, and it is said to him: Look at what Allah has shielded you from. Then an opening is made for him to Paradise, and he looks at its bloom and what is in it, and it is said to him: This is your seat therein, and upon certainty you lived, and upon it you died, and upon it you will be raised, if Allah wills. And when the evil man sits in his grave, terrified and bewildered, it is said to him: What did you say? So he says: I do not know. So it is said to him: Who is this man who was before you? So he says: I heard the people saying a saying, so I said as they said. So an opening is made for him to Paradise, and he looks at its bloom and what is in it, and it is said to him: Look at what Allah has turned away from you. Then an opening is made for him toward the Fire, and he looks at it crushing one part another, and it is said: This is your seat therein; upon doubt you lived, and upon it you died, and upon it you will be raised, if Allah wills. Then he is punished.""**"
"4266","Al-Barā' ibn 'Āzib رضي الله عنه reports that they went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for the funeral of a man from the Anṣār, and when they reached the grave whose laḥd was not yet dug, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat with them around him as though birds were upon their heads, prodding the earth with a stick in his hand, then raised his head and twice or thrice commanded seeking refuge, then described at length the descent of white-faced angels and the departure of the tranquil soul with its ascent to the seventh heaven and return, its questioning and widening and handsome visitor, and the descent of black-faced angels and the departure of the foul soul with its rejection from heaven and return, its questioning and constriction and ugly visitor. > **""Seek refuge with Allah from the punishment of the grave. O tranquil soul, come out to forgiveness from Allah and pleasure. Write the book of My servant in 'Illiyyīn, and return him to the earth, for from it I created them, and into it I shall return them, and from it I shall bring them out another time. Who is your Lord? What is your religion? Who is this man who was sent among you? What is your deed? My servant has spoken truly, so spread for him from Paradise, and clothe him from Paradise, and open for him a door to Paradise. Bear glad tidings of what delights you; this is your day which you were promised. I am your righteous deed. My Lord, establish the Hour, my Lord, establish the Hour, so that I may return to my family and my wealth. O foul soul, come out to wrath from Allah and anger. The gates of heaven shall not be opened for them, nor shall they enter Paradise until the camel passes through the eye of the needle. Write his book in Sijjīn in the lowest earth. And whoever associates with Allah, it is as though he fell from the sky and the birds snatched him, or the wind swept him to a distant place. Hah, hah, I do not know. He has lied, so spread for him from the Fire, and open for him a door to the Fire. Bear glad tidings of what displeases you; this is your day which you were promised. I am your foul deed. My Lord, do not establish the Hour.""**"
"4267","Al-Barā' رضي الله عنه reports in a narration with Aḥmad similar to the preceding, with the addition of the coming of an ugly-faced, ugly-clothed, foul-smelling visitor announcing disgrace, his identification as the foul deed marked by slowness to obedience and haste to disobedience, and the appointment of a blind, deaf and mute angel with a hammer that would turn a mountain to dust, striking him to dust and restored and struck again with a shriek heard by all except the two heavy ones, followed by a door to the Fire and bedding from the Fire. > **""Bear glad tidings of disgrace from Allah and lasting punishment; may Allah give you tidings of evil; who are you? I am your foul deed; you were slow to the obedience of Allah, swift in His disobedience, so Allah has requited you with evil.""**"
"4268","Asmā' رضي الله عنها reports from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the man's entry into the grave, the surrounding of the believer by his prayer and fasting which repel the angel from either side, his seating and questioning about the Prophet ﷺ and his firm answer with its requital, and the coming of the angel to the wicked or disbelieving man with nothing to repel him, his ignorant answer with its requital, and the unleashing of a deaf beast with a whip tipped with ember like a camel's hump striking as Allah wills. > **""Sit. What do you say concerning this man, meaning Muḥammad? I bear witness that he is the Messenger of Allah. Upon that you lived, and upon it you died, and upon it you will be raised. Sit; what do you say concerning this man? Upon that you lived, and upon it you died, and upon it you will be raised.""**"
"4269","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ swore by the One in Whose Hand is his soul that the dead hears the shuffling of their sandals as they turn away, then described the standing of prayer at the head, zakāh at the right, fasting at the left and good deeds, kindness and beneficence at the feet, each repelling approach from its side, the seating with the sun pictured at setting, the questioning about the man sent before them, the believer's testimony and its requital with widening and the verse of the firm word and the two doors increasing joy and the soul placed in a bird hanging in the trees of Paradise, and the disbeliever's fearful sitting, his ignorant answer, his constriction and the verse of the constricted life and the two doors increasing regret, with the explanatory remark of Abū 'Umar and Ḥammād ibn Salamah concerning one of the qiblah who testified without heart-certainty. > **""By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, he hears the shuffling of their sandals as they turn away from him. There is no entry from my side. Sit. What do you say concerning this man who was before you? I bear witness that he is the Messenger of Allah; he came to us with clear proofs from our Lord, so we believed him and followed him. You have spoken truly, and upon this you lived, and upon this you died, and upon it you will be raised, if Allah wills. Allah keeps firm those who believe with the firm word in the life of this world and in the Hereafter. Open for him a door to the Fire. This would have been your abode had you disobeyed Allah, Mighty and Majestic. Open for him a door to Paradise. This is your abode and what Allah has prepared for you. What do you say concerning this man who was among you, and what do you testify of him? Muḥammad. I heard the people saying something, so I said as they said. You have spoken truly; upon this you lived, and upon it you died, and upon it you will be raised, if Allah wills. And whoever turns away from My remembrance, then he shall have a constricted life. Open for him a door to Paradise. This would have been your abode and what Allah had prepared for you had you obeyed Him. Open for him a door to the Fire. This is your abode and what Allah has prepared for you.""**"
"4270","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports in a marfū' narration concerning the man's trial in his grave, where recitation of the Qur'an repels approach from the head, charity repels approach from the hands, walking to the mosques repels approach from the feet, while patience stands as a barrier and speaks of accompanying a beloved. > **""The man is approached in his grave; when approached from his head, recitation of the Qur'an repels him, and when approached from his hands, charity repels him, and when approached from his feet, his walking to the mosques repels him, and patience stands as a barrier. Indeed, had I seen a beloved, I would have accompanied him.""**"
"4271","Abū Ḥāzim reports from Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه, deeming it marfū', concerning the believer overtaken by death witnessing what he witnesses and longing for his soul to depart while Allah loves his meeting, the ascent of his soul and the questioning of the believers' souls about the people of the earth and their remark when one has died yet was not brought to them, the believer's seating in his grave and questioning about Lord, Prophet and religion with the opening of a door and the grave seen as a nap, and the enemy of Allah disliking departure while Allah dislikes his meeting, his ignorant answer, the opening of a door from Hell, the blow heard by every creature except the two heavy ones, the command to sleep as the manhūs sleeps, explained as one bitten by beasts and locusts, and the constriction of his grave. > **""O tranquil soul, come out. Who is your Lord? My Lord is Allah. Who is your Prophet? My Prophet is Muḥammad. What is your religion? My religion is Islam. Look at your seat. Who is your Lord? I do not know. You did not know. Sleep as the manhūs sleeps.""**"
"4272","'Ā'ishah رضي الله عنها reports that she asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ how it would be with her, a weak woman, when this community is tried in their graves, whereupon he ﷺ recited the verse of the firm word. > **""Allah keeps firm those who believe with the firm word in the life of this world and in the Hereafter.""**"
"4273","Abū Rāfi' رضي الله عنه reports that while he was walking behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in Baqī' al-Gharqad, he ﷺ thrice uttered a supplication against one not guided, and when Abū Rāfi' asked what ailed him, he ﷺ clarified he did not mean him but the owner of a freshly watered grave who, asked about him ﷺ, claimed not to know him. > **""You were not guided, nor did you attain guidance. I do not mean you, but I mean the owner of this grave; he was asked about me and claimed he does not know me.""**"
"4274","Ayyūb ibn Bashīr reports from his father concerning a feud among Banū Mu'āwiyah which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went to reconcile, when he ﷺ turned toward a grave and uttered a rebuke, and when asked explained that the man was asked about him ﷺ and answered he did not know. > **""You did not know. He is asked about me, so he says: I do not know.""**"
"4275","Abū Rāfi' رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out at night to supplicate at al-Baqī' with Abū Rāfi', and after supplicating as Allah willed and turning back, he ﷺ passed a grave and thrice uttered uff, and when Abū Rāfi' asked, offering himself as ransom and fearing it was meant for him alone with him, he ﷺ clarified it was for the owner who was asked about him ﷺ and doubted him. > **""Uff, uff, uff. No, but I uttered uff for the owner of this grave, who was asked about me and doubted me.""**"
"4276","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that they witnessed a funeral with the Prophet of Allah ﷺ, and when burial was finished and the people departed, the Prophet of Allah ﷺ described how the dead now hears their sandals, how Munkar and Nakīr come with eyes like copper pots, tusks like cows' horns and voices like thunder, seat him and ask what he worshipped and who his prophet was, the worshipper's true answer with its requital of certainty and widening, and the doubter's ignorant answer with its requital of doubt and the opening to the Fire, the unleashing of scorpions and serpents whose breath would barren the earth, and the compression of the earth until the ribs interlace. > **""He now hears the shuffling of your sandals. Munkar and Nakīr come to him, their eyes like copper pots, their tusks like cows' horns, and their voices like thunder. They seat him and ask him what he used to worship and who his prophet was. I used to worship Allah, and my prophet is Muḥammad; he came to us with clear proofs, so we believed in him and followed him. Allah keeps firm those who believe with the firm word in the life of this world and in the Hereafter. Upon certainty you lived, and upon it you died, and upon it you will be raised. I do not know; I heard the people saying something, so I said it. Upon doubt you lived, and upon it you died, and upon it you will be raised.""**"
"4277","Ibn 'Abbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that when the dead is buried he hears the shuffling of their sandals as they turn away departing. > **""When the dead is buried, he hears the shuffling of their sandals when they turn away from him departing.""**"
"4278","'Abd Allāh, meaning Ibn Mas'ūd رضي الله عنه, reports that whenever he narrates a ḥadīth he informs of its confirmation, namely that the believer when he dies sits in his grave and is asked about his Lord, religion and Prophet, and Allah makes him firm so he answers with his Lord, his religion and his Prophet, whereupon his grave is widened, and he then recited the verse of the firm word and the straying of the wrongdoers. > **""Who is your Lord? What is your religion? Who is your Prophet? My Lord is Allah, and my religion is Islam, and my Prophet is Muḥammad. Allah keeps firm those who believe with the firm word in the life of this world and in the Hereafter, and Allah sends astray the wrongdoers.""**"
"4279","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that the dead hears the shuffling of their sandals when they turn away from him, meaning departing. > **""Indeed the dead hears the shuffling of their sandals when they turn away from him, meaning departing.""**"
"4280","Ibn 'Abbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the names of the two angels who come in the grave are Munkar and Nakīr, and that the names of Hārūt and Mārūt, while they were in heaven, were 'Azrā and 'Azīz."
"4281","'Ā'ishah رضي الله عنها reports that a Jewish woman used to serve her, and whenever 'Ā'ishah did her any kindness the Jewess would invoke Allah's protection for her from the punishment of the grave, so when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to her she asked whether there is punishment in the grave before the Resurrection, and he ﷺ first denied it and asked what prompted it, then declared the Jews liars upon Allah with no punishment before the Resurrection, but after some time he ﷺ came out at midday wrapped in his garment with reddened eyes, calling at the top of his voice about enveloping tribulations like dark night, the disparity between laughter and weeping had they known what he knew, and the command to seek refuge, for the punishment of the grave is true. > **""No, and what is that? The Jews have lied, and they lie upon Allah; there is no punishment before the Day of Resurrection. O people, tribulations have shaded you like portions of the dark night. O people, had you known what I know, you would have laughed little and wept much. O people, seek refuge with Allah from the punishment of the grave, for indeed the punishment of the grave is true.""**"
"4282","Jābir ibn 'Abd Allāh رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered a quarter of Banū al-Najjār and heard the voices of men from Banū al-Najjār who had died in Jāhiliyyah being punished in their graves, so he ﷺ came out frightened and commanded his Companions to seek refuge from the punishment of the grave. > **""Seek refuge with Allah from the punishment of the grave.""**"
"4283","Jābir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by the graves of women from Banū al-Najjār who had perished in Jāhiliyyah and heard them punished in their graves for tale-bearing. > **""They are punished in their graves for tale-bearing.""**"
"4284","'Ā'ishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that two snakes are sent against the disbeliever, one from his head and the other from his feet, biting him, and whenever they finish they return, until the Resurrection. > **""Two snakes are sent against the disbeliever: one from his head, and the other from his feet, biting him repeatedly; whenever they finish, they return, until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"4285","Abū Sa'īd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that ninety-nine tinnīn are unleashed against the disbeliever in his grave, stinging him until the Hour, and had one of them breathed upon the earth no greenery would ever grow. > **""Ninety-nine tinnīn are unleashed against the disbeliever in his grave, stinging him until the Hour comes, and had one of them breathed upon the earth, it would never grow greenery.""**"
"4286","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning the believer in his grave in a garden with his grave widened seventy cubits and illuminated like the full moon, and his questioning of the Companions about the verse of the constricted life and the blind resurrection, their referral to Allah and His Messenger, and his explanation as the punishment of the disbeliever with ninety-nine tinnīn, explained as ninety-nine snakes each with seven heads blowing, biting and scratching until the Resurrection. > **""The believer in his grave is in a garden, and his grave is widened for him seventy cubits, and illuminated for him like the full moon on the night of fullness. Do you know concerning what this verse was revealed: Then he shall have a constricted life, and We shall raise him on the Day of Resurrection blind? Do you know what the constricted life is? It is the punishment of the disbeliever in his grave. By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, ninety-nine tinnīn are unleashed against them. Do you know what the tinnīn is? Ninety-nine snakes, each snake having seven heads, blowing into his body, biting him and scratching him until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"4287","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in the palms of Abū Ṭalḥah رضي الله عنه going out for his need, with Bilāl رضي الله عنه walking behind him out of honour for the Prophet of Allah ﷺ rather than beside him, the Prophet of Allah ﷺ passed a grave and stood until Bilāl caught up, then asked whether he heard what he ﷺ heard, and when Bilāl heard nothing, he ﷺ informed him of the occupant's punishment, and inquiry revealed a Jew. > **""Woe to you, O Bilāl, do you hear what I hear? The owner of the grave is being punished.""**"
"4288","Anas رضي الله عنه reports from one of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ whom he does not suspect, that while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and Bilāl رضي الله عنه were walking in al-Baqī', the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked whether he heard what he ﷺ heard, and when he swore he did not hear it, he ﷺ asked whether he did not hear the people of these graves punished, meaning the graves of the people of Jāhiliyyah. > **""O Bilāl, do you hear what I hear? Do you not hear the people of these graves being punished?""**"
"4289","Umm Mubashshir رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to her while she was in a garden of Banū al-Najjār containing graves of those who had died in Jāhiliyyah, and he ﷺ heard them punished and came out commanding seeking refuge, and when she asked whether they are indeed punished in their graves, he ﷺ confirmed it with a punishment heard by beasts. > **""Seek refuge with Allah from the punishment of the grave. Yes, with a punishment heard by beasts.""**"
"4290","Abū Sa'īd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reports that he was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey while he ﷺ rode his mount and it shied, and when he asked what ailed the mount, he ﷺ explained that it had heard the voice of a man punished in his grave and shied for that reason. > **""It heard the voice of a man punished in his grave, so it shied for that reason.""**"
"4291","'Abd Allāh, meaning Ibn Mas'ūd رضي الله عنه, reports from the Prophet ﷺ that the dead are punished in their graves until even beasts hear their voices. > **""Indeed the dead are punished in their graves, until even beasts hear their voices.""**"
"4292","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ passed in intense heat toward Baqī' al-Gharqad while the people walked behind him, and when he ﷺ heard the sound of sandals it weighed upon himself so he halted until he sent them ahead lest pride enter his heart, then when he passed Baqī' al-Gharqad there were two graves in which two men had been buried, and he ﷺ asked whom they had buried that day, and when they named them he ﷺ informed them they were now punished and tried, explained it as tale-bearing and neglect of urine, split a palm stalk and placed it upon the graves for lightening, and when asked how long they would be punished described it as unseen known only to Allah, adding that were it not for the diversion of hearts and excess in speech they would hear what he ﷺ hears. > **""Whom did you bury here today? They are now punished and tried in their graves. As for one of them, he used to walk with tale-bearing, and as for the other, he would not guard himself from urine. Let it be lightened for them. It is unseen which none knows but Allah. Were it not for the diversion of your hearts and your excess in speech, you would hear what I hear.""**"
"4293","Ibn 'Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ once passed graves with a moist palm stalk, split it in two, placed one upon a grave and the other upon another grave, then went on, and when they asked why he ﷺ had done so, he ﷺ explained the punishment for tale-bearing and neglect of urine and the relief while moist. > **""As for one of them, he was punished for tale-bearing, and as for the other, he would not guard against urine, and they shall not be punished while this remains moist.""**"
"4294","Ibn 'Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that while he travelled by the environs of Badr, a man emerged from a pit with a chain upon his neck calling to him for drink, whether knowing his name or calling as the Arabs call, and another man emerged in that pit with a whip calling to him not to give him drink for he is a disbeliever, then struck him with the whip until he returned to his pit, so he hastened to the Prophet ﷺ and informed him, and when he ﷺ asked whether he had seen him and he confirmed it, he ﷺ identified him as the enemy of Allah Abū Jahl ibn Hishām and his punishment until the Resurrection. > **""Did you indeed see him? That is the enemy of Allah, Abū Jahl ibn Hishām, and that is his punishment until the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"4295","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed a grave and commanded that two palm stalks be brought, then placed one at its head and the other at its feet, and when it was asked whether that would benefit him, he ﷺ explained the continued lightening of some punishment while moisture remains in them. > **""Bring me two palm stalks. Some of the punishment of the grave shall continue to be lightened for him while moisture remains in them.""**"
"4296","Ya'lā ibn Siyābah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ passed a grave and declared its owner punished though not for a major matter difficult to avoid, then called for a palm stalk and placed it upon his grave, expressing hope of lightening while moist. > **""Indeed the owner of this grave is punished though not for a major matter. Perhaps it will be lightened for him while it remains moist.""**"
"4297","Mu'āwiyah رضي الله عنه reports that levelling the grave is from the Sunnah, for the Jews and Christians raise them, so do not resemble them."
"4298","'Uthmān ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān reports that his brother informed him that their father 'Abd al-Raḥmān was struck alongside Ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما, and Ibn al-Zubayr ordered concerning him so he was buried in the mosque of the Ka'bah, then ordered horses passed over his grave lest its trace be seen."
"4299","Abū Sa'īd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that he ﷺ had forbidden visiting graves, then permitted visiting them for the lesson therein. > **""I forbade you from visiting graves, so visit them, for therein is a lesson.""**"
"4300","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that he ﷺ had forbidden visiting graves, then permitted visiting them for the lesson therein for them. > **""I forbade you from visiting graves, so visit them, for you have in them a lesson.""**"
"4301","Abū Sa'īd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated his former prohibitions of sacrificial meat beyond three, of visiting graves, and of vessels, followed by permission to eat and store, to visit without speech angering the Lord, and to prepare nabīdh, while every intoxicant is forbidden. > **""I forbade you the meat of sacrifices beyond three, so eat and store; and I forbade you visiting graves, so visit them, and do not say what angers the Lord; and I forbade you vessels, so prepare nabīdh, and every intoxicant is forbidden.""**"
"4302","'Ā'ishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Prophet ﷺ forbade visiting graves, then permitted them, deeming that he ﷺ said it reminds of the Hereafter. > **""It reminds of the Hereafter.""**"
"4303","Zayd ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه reports that they went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of the Conquest of Makkah toward the graves, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat facing a grave as though conversing intimately, then stood wiping tears from his eyes, and 'Umar رضي الله عنه, foremost among them, met him and asked, offering himself as ransom, what made him weep, whereupon he ﷺ mentioned seeking permission from his Lord to visit his mother's grave as a mother with right upon him and seeking forgiveness for her but being forbidden, then he ﷺ gestured them to sit and announced the permissions after prohibitions concerning graves, sacrificial meat and vessels, with avoidance of every intoxicant. > **""By my father and mother, what makes you weep? I sought permission from my Lord to visit the grave of my mother, for she was a mother with a right upon me, and I wished to seek forgiveness for her, but He forbade me. I had forbidden you visiting graves, so whoever among you wishes to visit, let him visit; and I had forbidden you the meat of sacrifices beyond three days, so eat and store as seems to you; and I had forbidden you vessels, and commanded you vessels, so prepare nabīdh, for vessels neither permit anything nor forbid it, and avoid every intoxicant.""**"
"4304","'Alī رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade visiting graves, vessels, and retaining sacrificial meat beyond three, then declared permission to visit for remembrance of the Hereafter, to drink from vessels while avoiding intoxication, and to retain meat as seems fit. > **""I had forbidden you visiting graves, so visit them, for they remind you of the Hereafter; and I forbade you vessels, so drink from them, and avoid what intoxicates; and I forbade you the meat of sacrifices to retain beyond three, so retain as seems to you.""**"
"4305","Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded visiting graves without speaking evil. > **""Visit graves, and do not speak evil.""**"
"4306","Ibn 'Abbās رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the former prohibitions of visiting graves, of sacrificial meat beyond three, and of nabīdh, followed by permission to visit without evil speech, to eat and retain, and to drink without intoxicant. > **""I forbade you visiting graves, so visit them, and do not speak evil; and I forbade you the meat of sacrifices beyond three, so eat and retain; and I forbade you nabīdh, so drink, but do not drink an intoxicant.""**"
"4307","Thawbān رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated his former prohibition of visiting graves followed by permission with visitation made as prayer and seeking forgiveness for them, his former prohibition of sacrificial meat beyond three followed by permission to eat and store, and his former prohibition of fermentation in dubbā', ḥantam and naqīr followed by permission to prepare and benefit. > **""I had forbidden you visiting graves, so visit them, and make your visitation to them a prayer upon them and seeking forgiveness for them; and I forbade you the meat of sacrifices beyond three, so eat from them and store; and I forbade you what is fermented in dubbā', ḥantam and naqīr, so prepare nabīdh and benefit thereby.""**"
"4308","'Ā'ishah رضي الله عنها reports that she heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mention three things he ﷺ had forbidden, namely visiting graves, sacrificial meat beyond three, and fermentation in muzaffat, ḥantam and naqīr, followed by permission to visit brothers and greet them for the lesson therein, to eat and store sacrificial meat, and the declaration that every intoxicant is khamr and every khamr forbidden. > **""Three things I forbade you: visiting graves, and the meat of sacrifices beyond three, and fermentation in muzaffat, ḥantam and naqīr. Behold, so visit your brothers and greet them, for therein is a lesson; behold, as for the meat of sacrifices, eat from them and store; behold, every intoxicant is khamr, behold, and every khamr is forbidden.""**"
"4309","Abū Muwayhibah, freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was commanded to pray for the people of al-Baqī', so he ﷺ prayed for them at night three times."
"4310","Abū Muwayhibah رضي الله عنه reports in the wording with al-Bazzār that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to him one night and commanded him to go forth to seek forgiveness for the people of al-Baqī', and when they reached al-Baqī' he ﷺ greeted the people of the graves and congratulated them for what they are in compared to what the people are in, for Allah's deliverance, with tribulations approaching. > **""O Abā Muwayhibah, go forth, for I have been commanded to seek forgiveness for the people of al-Baqī'. Peace upon you, O people of the graves; congratulations to you for what you are in compared to what the people are in; had you known what Allah has saved you from, tribulations are approaching.""**"
"4311","Ibn 'Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ, along with Abū Bakr and 'Umar رضي الله عنهما, used to go walking to al-Ḥayyān and return walking."
"4312","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that whoever visits the graves of his parents, or one of them, every Friday is forgiven and recorded as dutiful. > **""Whoever visits the grave of his parents, or one of them, every Friday, is forgiven and recorded as dutiful.""**"
"4313","'Alī رضي الله عنه reports that going out to the graveyards on the two 'Īds is from the Sunnah."
"4314","Ibn Abī Mulaykah reports that 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakr رضي الله عنهما passed away at al-Ḥubashī, and when 'Ā'ishah رضي الله عنها performed Ḥajj she came to his grave and recited verses likening their long companionship to the two companions of Jadhīmah until separation rendered them as though never together for a night, then swore that had she witnessed him she would neither visit him now nor have buried him except where he died. > **""We were like the two companions of Jadhīmah for an era of time, until it was said they would never part; then when we parted, it is as though I and Mālik, for length of companionship, never spent a night together. By Allah, had I witnessed you, I would not have visited you, and I would have buried you where you died.""**"
"4315","Ibn 'Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out to al-Baqī', Baqī' al-Gharqad, and greeted the Muslim and believing people of the abodes, invoked mercy upon the predecessors, and affirmed joining them if Allah wills, meaning them. > **""Peace upon the people of the abodes from among the Muslims and the believers, and may Allah have mercy upon the predecessors, and we, if Allah wills, shall join you.""**"
"4316","Ibn 'Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ passed by Muṣ'ab ibn 'Umayr رضي الله عنه when returning from Uḥud, and stood over him and his companions and bore witness that they are alive with Allah, then commanded visiting them and greeting them, swearing that none greets them until the Resurrection except that they reply to him. > **""I bear witness that you are alive with Allah, so visit them and greet them, for by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, none greets them until the Day of Resurrection except that they reply to him.""**"
"4317","Mujammi' ibn Jāriyah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ went out for a funeral of Banū 'Amr ibn 'Awf until he reached the graveyard, then thrice greeted the people of the graves and declared them predecessors and themselves followers with a supplication of well-being for all. > **""Peace upon the people of the graves, peace upon the people of the graves, peace upon the people of the graves. Whoever among you was from among the believers and the Muslims, you are our predecessors, and we are your followers; may Allah grant us and you well-being.""**"
"4318","Bashīr ibn al-Khaṣāṣiyah رضي الله عنه reports that he came to the Prophet ﷺ and caught up with him at al-Baqī' and heard him greet the believing people of the abodes, then when his shoestrap broke he ﷺ commanded him to right his foot, and when he mentioned his long estrangement from his people's home, he ﷺ asked whether he does not praise Allah Who took his forelock from among Rabī'ah, a people who deem that were it not for them the earth would overturn with those upon it. > **""Peace upon the people of the abodes from among the believers. Right your foot. O Bashīr, do you not praise Allah Who took your forelock from among Rabī'ah, a people who deem that were it not for them the earth would overturn with those upon it?""**"
"4319","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade building over the grave or plastering it. > **""Do not build over the grave, nor plaster it.""**"
"4320","Abū Sa'īd رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ forbade building over graves, or sitting upon them, or praying upon them. > **""Do not build over graves, nor sit upon them, nor pray upon them.""**"
"4321","'Umārah ibn Ḥazm رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw him sitting upon a grave and commanded him to descend from it, neither harming its occupant nor being harmed by him. > **""O companion of the grave, descend from upon the grave; do not harm the owner of the grave, nor shall he harm you.""**"
"4322","'Abd Allāh, meaning Ibn Mas'ūd رضي الله عنه, reports that treading upon an ember is dearer to him than treading upon the grave of a Muslim."
"4323","'Iṣmah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw a man walking in his sandals between the graves and commanded him to remove his sibtiyyah sandals. > **""O wearer of sibtiyyah sandals, remove your sandals.""**"
